The Republican Party's strategy against Obama's re-election
Republican leaders have justified their policy with the necessity of combating voter fraud.
However, the Brennan Center calls such fraud a myth, saying that voter fraud is less common in the U.S. than the number of people who die after a lightning strike.
Moreover, in the past decade, Republican lawyers have recorded only 300 cases of electoral fraud in the U.S.
One thing is certain: these new provisions will have a negative impact on voter turnout.
In this sense, these measures will partially undermine the U.S. democratic system.
Unlike Canada, individual states are responsible for organizing federal elections in the U.S.
And in that spirit, most U.S. governments since 2009 have promulgated new laws that make it difficult for the process of election or enrollment in an election list.
This phenomenon saw a boom after the November 2010 election, during which 675 new Republican representatives were added in 26 states.
As a result, in 2011 alone, 180 bills restricting the right to vote have been tabled in 41 states.
New electoral laws require voters to submit photo IDs and proof of U.S. citizenship.
In addition, these laws limit the period for elections in advance, invalidate the right to enroll on the list of voters on the day of elections and remove the right to vote to citizens with criminal records.
Prior to the 2006 elections, no U.S. state had asked voters to present a photo-identity account.
The first state to introduce this requirement was Indiana.
The constitutionality of the Indian law was upheld by the U.S. Supreme Court in 2008.
The Republican authorities were in a hurry to extend this procedure to other states.
Over the past two years, they have backed proposals for such laws in 34 states in order to force voters to submit photo identity cards.
It should be noted that, unlike Quebec, U.S. citizens don't have universal identity cards, such as a health insurance card.
In fact, 11% of U.S. citizens, i.e
Furthermore, it does not have such an identity card of about five million new voters in 2012.
Acquisition of the required identity card often costs more than a hundred dollars.
The new restrictions are particularly disproportionate for the young, members of minorities and low-income people.
Therefore, 25% of African-Americans, 15% with an income below 35,000 dollars; 18% of citizens over the age of 65 and 20% of voters between the ages of 18 and 29 therefore do not have the required photo ID.
And that isn't all.
Students who are known as voters for their inclination to Democratic candidates are not authorized in several states to use their photo ID issued by their school.
On the other hand, the same states allow members of hunting or fishing clubs, who rather elect Republican candidates, to use the cards issued by these clubs during the elections.
Until 2004, no state had required proof of citizenship for elections.
The first such request was introduced by Arizona.
Since 2011, about a dozen states have passed laws that stipulate the duty of voters to prove that they are U.S. citizens.
These measures are clearly aimed at limitinging the number of votes from the Hispanic minority.
It should be noted that two of the three Hispanics prefer a democratic party.
In 2011, Republican legislators backed laws in eight states abolishing voter enrollment on Election Day.
In addition, they limited the right of persons and groups to provide assistance to voters who want to enroll.
These limitations have their consequences.
For example, during the general elections in 2004, campaigns for the registration of voters helped to register about 10 million inhabitants.
However, measures taken after 2009 caused a decrease of 17% when registering new voters in 2010 compared to 2006.
Republican legislators then in the other five states passed laws aimed at shortening the period for elections in advance.
For example, during the 2008 general election in Florida, African-Americans accounted for 33% of voters who voted in advance, even though African-American voters in that state were only 13%.
It was the same with Hispanics.
They accounted for just 11% of all voters, but 24% of citizens voting in advance.
On the other hand, whites, who accounted for 76% of all voters, made up just 46% of voters who voted in advance.
Of course, democratic legislators and their supporters strongly opposed approving laws that restrict voter registration.
Democratic governors have blocked several bills with their veto.
In order to suspend the most controversialous laws, the U.S. Attorney General even intervened.
In part, they managed to reduce the damage.
For example, laws requiring the presentation of a photo identity card were passed by only 16 out of 34 states.
However, the newly introduced rules will make the exercise of the right to vote quite undeniably difficult in 2012.
Democratic critics speak primarily about the bias of approved laws and see them as an obvious effort to influence the results of the 2012 elections in key states.
A 2011 report from the Brennan Center shows that the states where those laws have been passed represent 171 out of the 270 votes in the electoral choir necessary to win the presidential election.
It's too early to declare with certainty that these legislative changes at the level of the electoral system will have a significant impact on the outcome of the 2012 presidential election.
However, one thing is already certain today: these new provisions will have an adverse impact on voter turnout.
In this sense, these measures will partially undermine the U.S. democratic system.
Detection of prostate cancer: undergo an examination or not?
The PSA test sometimes yielded flawed results that were incorrectly negative or even improperly, leading to unnecessary medical procedures.
All of this increased the hesitancy of the men, who were already reluctant to go for the examination at all.
To be examined or not?
We've asked for an opinion from two experts.
In studies conducted in the U.S., there has been a high level of contamination between the individual tested groups, so it is difficult to interpret these results and make valuable recommendations.
Another study, this time European, reported a different mortality rate between patients who underwent an examination and those who did not attend them.
This study has also shown after a 12-year distance that those who don't go for the examination have a 30 to 40% higher risk of metastases.
Therefore, I recommend an examination from 50 years of age or from the age of 40 if your direct relative has had prostate cancer.
Men of African-American origin are also at risk.
The key is to make the right decision as soon as the cancer is exposed.
There are aggressive types of cancer and species that are indifferent.
It is therefore necessary to correctly explain to the patient the level of danger of its type of cancer, to offer it possible alternatives, not necessarily to treat prostate cancer, which does not have an impact on life in the long run, and to focus in these cases rather on the active supervision of the disease.
Today, a number of men who have been diagnosed with cancer are untreated because their type of cancer is not aggressive and doesn’t endanger their lives.
Rather, we will propose active oversight and, in the event of progression of the disease, we will offer them treatment.
Increasingly and with greater accuracy, we set criteria that help in deciding which species to heal and which not.
However, I recommend taking the examination.
However, it is important to have an interview with a doctor and determine whether it is appropriate to undergo an examination or not.
In cooperation with the International Urological Society, the Movember movement has created a tool that allows it to evaluate for and against in connection with PSA testing.
You can download this document (currently in English, the translation will be available later) at this address: http://ca.movember.com/fr/mens-health/prostate-cancer-screening
Prevention of the disease
Unfortunately, there is no miraculous procedure for cancer prevention.
Despite advances in research, it seems the best means of limitinginging the risks of the disease by adopting the principles of a healthy
It is estimated that if everyone ate healthily and moved sufficiently, up to 30% of cases of cancer could be prevented.
"If everyone stopped smoking, this figure would rise to at least 50%," André Beaulieu, spokesperson for the Canadian Society for Cancer Research, underlined.
In contrast, it is estimated that approximately 10% of the disease causes heredity.
Some percentage also remains completely unexplained.
For Canada's cancer research society, the anti-smoking fight remains a priority, even as the number of smokers declines.
Cigarettes are worth 85% of cases of lung cancer.
They are also a risky factor for several others.
This is extremely harmful to human health.
"There are still 1.5 million smokers in Quebec today," spokesman André Beaulieu said.
Encouragement: 10 years after the smoker stops smoking, the risk of dying from cancer drops by half.
Weight
According to the SCC, bodiness and obesity are another risk factor for the occurrence of the disease.
They increase the risk of breast, colon and rectum, esophageal, pancreas and uterus cancer.
"The research shows that regular physical activity for life protects against colon cancer," the report adds.
Nutrition
This organization also recommends limiting the consumption of red meat.
With too much consumption, it can lead to an increased risk of colorectal cancer.
It is also advisable to avoid sausages, for which the same applies.
Preservation of meat by smoking, drying or salting can lead to the formation of carcinogenic substances.
"They can cause damage to the body's cells and lead to the development of cancer," the report adds.
Vitamins
Recently, various scientists have focused on links between vitamin supplements and cancer.
However, research has not yet yielded unequivocal conclusions.
According to SCC, studies focused on vitamin E are contradictory.
Although, according to one of them, the risk of prostate cancer will be reduced, another study talks more about increasing it.
Even the effect of vitamin D on cancer is not unambiguously demonstrated.
Mr Beaulieu further insists on the importance of talking about his fears and precedents from the family with his doctor.
"Giving an examination doesn't mean you're going to have cancer."
Higgs boson explored
The announcement of the probable discovery of Higgs boson sparked a legitimate fume last summer.
There is a view that this boson participates in a mechanism that is responsible for all matter in the universe.
Furthermore, it is the last particle, the existence of which is predicted by a standard model - our best or "least wrong" explanation for the nature and behavior of matter - which, however, has not yet been empirically observed.
Among physicists, however, there is not yet a hundred percent certainty that it is Higgs boson.
There's no doubt that we have an entirely new particle at our disposal, which very much resembles the Higgs boson, as predicted by a standard model.
The new data released this weekhone during a major physics congress in Kyoto rather confirm it, but there is still a lack of data so that we can have absolute certainty.
However, let's assume it's a Higgs boson, because the risks of error appear small, and let's look at what it's about.
In this world, an unquestionable law applies, according to which two things cannot occur at the same time in the same place.
You can't get around this rule - and don't try too hard, otherwise you'll hurt yourself.
Although particle physics is a very strange world, it turns out that a similar law also applies to it: Pauli's excretory principle, according to which two particles cannot occupy the same space at the same moment if they are in the same "quantum state" - this "status" roughly creates some of their properties.
It also divides physical particles into two categories.
On the one hand, we find orderly citizens, or fermions, who obediently adhere to Pauli's principle.
And at the other end there are bosons, wicked anarchists who don't value anything at all - certainly not of this principle, which means that they can be in the same place at the same time.
These bosons are further divided into two groups according to the device (by the way absolutely amazing), which is The Particle Adventure, in Berkeley laboratories: mesons, which will be left unnoticed here, and "strength particles" through which great natural forces spread and with which Higgs boson is probably somehow related.
However, it must be said that these bosons are not as exotic animals as could be imagined.
If you read this article, it's thanks to a boson with an unusual banality: a photon, or "particle of light," which is the "bearer" of electromagnetic force.
When a particle with an electric charge speeds up or changes its direction, it "disturbs" the electromagnetic field in this particular place, similar to the pebble thrown into the pond.
From this "disturbance" an electromagnetic wave (light, infrared or ultraviolet radiation, etc.) arises, and this wave is nothing more than a photon - and thus one of the bosons, "power-bearers."
Stable field
It's also with Higgs boson, only with the difference that it's necessary to "disturb" a different field, Higgs field, for this boson to appear.
This Higgs field is much, much more stable than the electromagnetic field; to excite it, it is necessary to achieve very high energy values, similar to the frozen level of the pond, only a very large boulder ripples.
For this reason, it is necessary to use such a huge accelerator part as an accelerator in the CERN laboratory - a large hadron accelerator with accommodating bundles is a circle with a circumferency of 27 km! - in order to develop such energy.
The analogy with the electromagnetic field suits us again to clarify the relationship between Higgs and matter.
With an electromagnetic field, not all particles or materials react.
Some, for example, magnets do so, but others don't - a piece of paper, for example, will never be held by itself on the fridge.
And anyway, all particles do not react with the Higgs field: those who do so have a certain mass, while others (such as photon, let's look at it) don't have it.
And Mrs. Nováková asks, why are all these researches "bringing us"?
From the point of view of science, it is a confirmation of the validity of the standard model (SM), which allows physicists to examine any discrepancy between observations and SM predictions.
Moreover, few physicists literally want to be able to find them, because the slightest difference could open the space of "new physics" and close some gaps in the model.
It is necessary to state that the model still has huge flaws, brings no clarification of gravity (oha!) or dark matter, which makes up approximately 80% of cosmic matter (once again!).
Until now, however, the CERN facility has not discovered such contradictions.
Responding
The responses of these researches in the daily lives of Mr and Mrs Novák are very difficult to predict, but it would be a mistake to assume that there will be none.
At the very beginning of the 60's, laser pioneers in Bell's labs had no idea of the revolution that their work would provoke.
They assumed scientific use, but nothing else.
Physicist Willard Boyle - who worked at Bell Labs, where the laser was invented in 1960, and who created the first permanent laser beam (the previous beams were pulsed) in 1962, I've already said it - the laser was initially considered such a "toy into the lab."
Just imagine...
In addition, the application can result from all the tools that surround the research.
For example, the same Willard Boyle created a small light sensor in 1969 during his work focused on optics.
This sensor, even though Boyle didn't plan to do so at first, now serves as the "eye" of all digital cameras in the world and earned him the 2009 Nobel Prize in Physics.
Of course, this does not mean that activities around the LHC have to change our lives, but it means that you really never know...
Palliative Care - The best way to walk away from life... | Le Devoir
Through his Dignified Dying Commission, Quebek has recently become involved in a delicate end-of-life issue.
The debate is about to continue, and at the same time, a bill is about to take place.
There is still a lot of work to be done in this crucial area.
Le Devoir tried to look at this question in more detail.
Less than a few weeks ago, Mr. L. was still living alone in his apartment in Montérégie.
Prostate cancer, which consumed him, gave him a two-year delay.
"They gave me five years to live and it's already seven," he says emotionlessly on his bedrid at Victor-Gadbois palliative care home in Beloeil, where he had arrived the day before.
"It's still a shock, you can't prepare for this," he adds.
Illness has done its work: immense weakness makes it impossible for him to take himself to the toilet, or just to eat himself.
Resignantly, he looks at a tasty-looking breakfast and agrees to help him eat.
He's courageous and he even smiles, he talks to strangers who stop at him, brings him medicine, offers a bath.
Courage for ordinary deathion.
"My biggest wish is to get rid of the diarrhea, it humiliates me," he confides.
A few hours later, a team of doctors found a cure for his problem.
"In all one's life, one learns that men urinate stand-ups," Pierre Brodeur, a psychologist at the Victor-Gadbois facility, recounters.
Going back to the child's stage is an unacceptable humiliation for some people.
"It depends on the abilities of whichever person" whether he accepts the return, the psychologist argues.
In the opinion of a number of people engaged in palliative care, crucial moments take place at the very core of such a return.
Patients who enter the home with palliative care Victor-Gadbois are all suffering from cancer.
Their prognosis is a maximum of three months of life.
At this stage, the team of doctors and nurses caring for them does not give them so-called "curative" care.
In the case of Ms. A., 89 years old, the worst concern is to die "on suffocation in full consciousness."
However, the disease allowed me to discover my children.
"I've got good children," he adds.
"I don't have any desires in my life anymore," she says, and then allows us to put on her breathing apparatus.
Nevertheless, he is still looking forward in the coming days for his son's last visit from Italy.
At Victor-Gadbois, in collaboration with a group of volunteers, body care with the help of nutrition is provided.
This is what palliative care looks like when nothing else can be done.
To make death more comfortable.
In Québek there is one bed of palliative care per 11 700 inhabitants.
That's very few if we realize we're all going to die someday.
Here life continues in optimal conditions, explains Dr. Christiane Martel, one of the doctors in the home.
Whether it is a level of physical, emotional or spiritual comfort.
When a person dies, it can be provided, for example, with a sip of brandy or pepsi, according to taste.
Diabetics don't have to keep an eye on their sugar levels anymore.
Death is a part of everyday life here.
Last night they bore a beer to Mr. X, who died later last night.
His son will finish his beer this morning in honor of the deceased father.
"We're helping patients as well as their loved ones," Nathalie Savard, director of the nursing service, recounts.
At Victor-Gadbois's home, the days go one after another, but they don't resemble each other.
Alongside a ninety-three-year-old man who rejoices in his last meeting with his family thoroughly padded on his pillows while toasting him in his honor, a tragically thirty-six-year-old man, surrounded by his parents, wife and two young children after trying out all options to survive.
"For half a year, there have always been three to five beds, which are designed for people with cancer under the age of 45," Dr Christiane Martel recounts with concern.
53% of patients who come to Victor-Gadbois come from their homes, 47% from hospitals.
Problems with access to palliative care
It is said that 77% of Canadians simply do not have access to palliative care, i.e. care that is intended to alleviate suffering as soon as the patient reaches the final stage of their lives, whether it is at home, in a hospital or in a nursing home.
And several organizations, such as Victor-Gadbois's home and Grand Montréal Home Palliative Treatment Society, specialize more or less exclusively in care provided to cancer patients.
And it is precisely this fundamental unavailability of healthcare in Quebec that leads a number of physicians specializing in palliative care are concerned about the adoption of a law on euthanasia and assisted suicide.
Since October, there has been a manifesto signed in the field of palliative treatment, such as Dr. Balfour Mount and Dr Bernard Lapointe, in which they demonstrate their opposition to such an initiative.
According to Dr. Christiane Martel, the Quebec healthcare system is not powerful enough to provide access to quality palliative care for all people to consent to euthanasia.
As she says, she recently experienced a patient who spent 14 days in the emergency room in huge pains without anyone taking the necessary steps to relieve her.
They are concerned that patients do not ask for killing because they do not receive appropriate care.
At the same time, many oncologists take care of their patients until the last moments, regardless of unfavourable prognosis.
Hélène Richard's hopes of survival were already minimal when she abandoned demanding chemotherapy.
When I told my oncology that I was quitting treatment, she replied that she regretted that I stopped fighting, she told us.
She told me it was over!
There is no omnipotent care
Dr. Martel estimates that 90% of patients who wish to die thank their attending physicians for not fulfilling their wishes when a team of palliative pussies relieved their pain.
However, it must be said that palliative care is definitely not all-powerful in the treatment of pain.
According to Elsie Monereau, director of palliative care at Grand Montréal's Home Palliative Care Society, patients are resisting treatment for pain in 8% of cases.
At the very end of life, doctors often use palliative sedatives, which put the patient to sleep until the momentary of death, whether impactfully or whether sedatives are given to him permanently.
It is impossible to pretend that we do not perceive this part of suffering.
A patient we don't get rid of pains will have an ever-increasing opportunity to access palliative sedatives.
Sicks who are not given a sedative always say the same thing: "I want to die".
But that doesn't always necessarily mean "I want you to give me euthanasia," it means rather "I want you to relieve me of pain."
The implementation of this reportation was made possible by a grant for journalists from the Canadian Institute for Research in Health Care.
Extensive real estate scandals in Quebec
Travel officials, entrepreneurs in construction and public works, political party lobbyists and experts on the Italian mafia are still telling what they know about an amazing "system" in which construction, civil servants, politicians, trade unionists and mafias are mixed.
This "field" came to Quebec taxpayers very expensive, especially between 1990 and 2000.
"It's interesting how the system has crumbled since we've taken major precautions," Jacques Duchesneau, a Quebec MP and former chief of police in Montreal, says ironically.
Thanks to him, a scandal erupted in 2011 on the basis of a rigorous investigation aimed at embezzling funds in connection with contracts for the construction of roads in Quebec, which was approved by then-Liberal Quebec Prime Minister Jean Charest only after long delays.
"Duchesneau's report" confirmed a direct link between the industry, opaque party funding and corruption of civil servants.
"Since the start of the investigation in 2010," he says, "only saved the Department of Transporation on contracts a billion dollars," when some people kept their instincts at bay to acquire a stake in them!
Charbonneau's commission "has already led two mayors to resign," she adds, with the hope that she will manage to "uncover some people's cunning."
A permanent unit to fight corruption established in 2011
Established in 2011, the Permanent Corruption Task Force is also involved in battle with its army of government controllers, investigators and analysts.
In addition, officers of "Marteau's family", who since 2009 have ensured that the Montreal "cartel of money pipelines" will significantly mitigate the inflatability of orders...
In recent weeks they have carried out a number of searches and charged with fraud and corruption by a number of municipal councillors, such as Frank Zampino and Richard Marcotte, mayor of one municipality from the suburbs.
Gilles Vaillancourt, who has just resigned as mayor of Laval, Quebec's third-largest city.
He is suspected of repeatedly accepting bribes in exchange for public contracts.
Formal accusations have already been filed by transport engineers from Montreal and businessmen of Italian descent, such as Tony Accurso and Lino Zambito.
Lino Zambito sparked a sensation by explaining to the commission the mechanism of the "system" of public procurement.
During these years, he himself donated 3% of the values of contracts he had received in Montréal to an intermediary associated with the mafia, who then transferred the money to Union Montréal, for which Mayor Gérald Tremblay ran.
Zambito was generous on all sides after 2000 and donated more than 88 000 Canadian dollars (approximately 68 000 euros) to provincial parties, but especially to the liberals who were in power at that time.
He also acknowledged that he was organising illegally raising funds for former Liberal Deputy Prime Minister Nathalie Normandeau.
Contracts with money pipelines whose prices exaggerated
In Montreal, the "system" of corruption worked very vigorably.
Gilles Surprenant, a former transport engineer, described him in great detail before the committee: within ten years he received gifts from construction companies, invitations to trips, golf tournaments, restaurants, hockey matches and pocket money worth a total of 736 000 dollars in exchange for contracts with money pipelines for which he exaggerated the price.
Other public officials from the transport sector admitted that they had paid rewards for overestimating invoices by 30% to 40% through false extra work.
The mayor's party's organizer, Martin Dumont, then accused Tremblay of turning a blind eye to the parallel budget, which brought dirty money into his coffers.
Based on these revelations, Tremblay resigned in early November, plunging Montréal into a sweeping crisis.
Chantal Rouleau was among the first Montreal MPs to issue a warning sign.
As mayor of Rivière-des-Prairies in the east of the island since 2010, she has advocated against the sale of municipal land purchased for US$5 million and sold for... 1.6 million promoters right in the midst of a flourishing real estate business.
70% dirty money in election campaigns
About the investigation, which will finally be launched, he says that he "pulls the strings in an effort to understand the functioning of the ant's infested system in order to stop the gangrene and catch the culprits."
According to her, the process is "painful, but positive."
We are in the wound cleaning phase, but it is necessary to establish our own investigation unit in Montréal and to ensure that these questionable practices cannot go back.
It's like he's cleaning.
Regularly.
Jacques Duchesneau himself says that "public officials have stolen hundreds of millions of dollars," but he is particularly concerned about the role of "MPs informed of this deceit" if they haven't dipped into these messes!
He estimates that 70% of campaign funding in Québek comes from dirty money, adding with irony: "I've heard it's just a weak decoction of reality."
The Quebec government proposes limiting donations to parties to 100 dollars, but that doesn't solve the situation: "Until electoral spending is strictly reduced, dirty money from politics won't disappear."
It proposes comprehensive scrutiny of the public procurement and party funding system: "Which we can no longer fall; if we focus boldly on the nature of things, we can build again on stronger foundations and with greater scrutiny and compliance with the laws."
If this story defamed the image of Quebek and Montreal abroad, Duchesneau advises all those who mock him to look at their own backyard...
"PSG isn't FC Barcelona!"
This season with PSG, you've set a wholly new pace.
How do you explain this development?
It can be explained by the fact that all individuals went together, or a completely new dimension of PSG.
The big players have come to us.
Every day I get next to them.
The technical team helped me a lot, too.
Every day these facts allow me to increase the level of the game
And it's easier in matches.
In football, everything goes very fast.
I don't lose my head.
From my beginnings in preparation in INF Clairefontaine to the transfer to Saint-Etienne, I have always shifted by individual grades.
So you're taking advantage of the competitive environment introduced by Carlo Ancelotti...
Reinforcements from this summer are accustomed to playing matches at a very high level.
They also know that every training session is essential.
And it follows that a player like me has a greater appetite for the match and wants to give maximum performance.
Moreover, Carlo Ancelotti gives me a lot in connection with my position.
He's got assistants like Claude Makelele, who played in the same post as me.
And does Ancelotti have the decisive word at the right time?
Of course.
Ancelotti has the respect of all technicians.
Today he does not have an equal in League 1 and is one of Europe's best coaches.
He's got tremendous experience and has won a lot of titles with top teams.
He's also worked with outstanding players.
I think he's going to win more titles in Paris.
In January, I had a reassuring conversation with him.
I've had a number of injuries.
The trust it gives me is another reason for my performance.
How do you look at the first part of PSG season?
In League 1, Lyon took our leading position.
But we're staying in tow.
One of our main goals is the Champions League: we very easily advanced to the eight-finals.
What is the goal of your club in this competition?
We'll try to go as far as we can
Now anything can happen.
But we definitely want to be a tough opponent for quality European teams.
We want to win our qualifying group before Porto first so that we can play in the rematch of the eight-finals at home.
Can PSG become a major European club in the short term?
The budget already has...
In order for Paris to become a big European club, it has to win titles and make history.
That's not the case today.
On the financial side, PSG has the resources to make this intention happen.
And it would be a big failure not to win in League 1 like last season?
Of course, that would be very disappointing.
We really want to win the league title a lot this year.
We missed a lot last season.
We were disappointed in May because we have had enough to win.
We've had a great season.
We scored 79 points.
Usually, with 79 points, you're a clear master..
But another club, Montpellier, had an even more amazing season.
I think it'll happen this year.
Even though big teams such as Marseille, Lyon or Bordeaux are also aiming for the title, I think we have weapons at our disposal to beat them.
Do you think PSG's media expect too much?
It's not unusual to expect a lot from us given what's been invested here and what players we have.
We totally accept that.
It's a fact that if we beat Troyes 4:0 at home and there's still something they're blaming us for in the paper, it's not very pleasant.
You ask, what more people might want from you.
You can't, of course, win 4:0 on every weekend.
We'ren't FC Barcelona!
We're trying to set the playing style.
You need time to build a team.
We've proved in the Champions League that we can play.
Take a look at Manchester City, who have not made it to the eight-finals for the second season, and have also invested large sums!
Due to the sums invested, you could be expected to be ahead of 15 points!
In doing so, you're offending other clubs and the French league.
The Lyon and Marseille clubs, who were not doing very well last season, have moved to the next stage with the new PSG.
This shows how League #1 is amazing.
I hope that in May we will be able to smile and tell ourselves that, despite all the difficulties, we managed to win.
It seems as if PSG in everything relied on Zlatan Ibrahimovich's performances.
It's good it's said there's "dependence on Zlatan."
That means Ibrahimovic is very efficient and that he scores a lot of goals.
That's why he came here and proves he's a League 1 star.
Wherever he's gone, he's proven he's a big player, a world-class star.
In our team, we respect people and players.
He, too, respects the people he has around him.
What he has achieved is truly exceptional.
This has also led others to increase their own levels of play.
Thiago Silva, one of the best defenders in the world, also allows others to grow side-by-side.
How did you experience Euro 2012 with the French team?
As a disappointment.
I really wanted to play in this tournament.
Unfortunately because of the injury, I couldn't play the way I would have imagined.
But I've experienced it, and I've come out of it
Today I also manage to play good matches in the national team.
And I've been hoping for that since I joined the team.
We have learned from what has happened in Ukraine, and today we owe ourselves to make our demeanor.
How do you look at Didier Deschamps's first months at the head of the national team?
He speaks for the results.
We're in a good place in qualifying for the World Championships.
The coach chooses strictly, is close to the players and manages to get excited for victory.
This is what Laurent Blanc was.
But I don't want to compare them.
Blanc achieved his goal by qualifying for the Euro.
I hope Didier Deschamps leads our team to Brazil.
Is the draw (1:1) of October 16 in Spain a solid starting match?
This match gave us confidence.
We've all struggled for the team.
Prior to this shock in Spain, I had not experienced such a struggle in my career.
Thanks to Bitcoin you can pay and sell without banks
All the negatives of current financial transactions based on central banks, identified transactions, and processing costs between recipients.
Moreover, as is often true within these technologies, there is an obvious political outlook: the belief that an existing financial system based on banking monopolies leads to financial crises.
Bitcoin, whose inventor is Satoshi Nakamoto (pseudonym), represents both virtual money (transferable to dollars, euros...) and a secure conversion protocol similar to BitTorrent, which allows the exchange of files between pairs of partners.
Thanks to 15 000 computers connected to the network, about 200 000 transactions have already been registered.
About a thousand websites already accept bitcoins as gifts or as a means of payment.
The exchange rate of bitcoin first reached a maximum of 30 dollars (23 euros) in June 2011, five months later it fell to 2 dollars, today it is at about ten dollars (an overview of odds is shown on bitcoincharts.com).
Compared to the world in exchange for real money or financial products, this is nothing impressive.
And yet the European Central Bank (ECB) is interested in this fact in its report on virtual finances, which came out in October.
He refers to Bitcoin as "the most successful virtual money," "competiting the dollar and the euro" and "resemblance to ordinary money."
Bitcoin differs from other types of virtual money because it is like "credits" used to progress in a video game that a person gains from a game or who can buy (and sometimes exchange).
The social network Facebook has also developed a similar system.
Each month, however, the central authority monitors and processes individual communications.
In the case of Bitcoin, all nodes of the network are depositaries of current account books, controllers, issuers of money and buyers and sellers.
How does this network work?
Each transaction between two users takes place in fact between two electronic addresses using email.
However, the user can choose a different address for each payment, thereby ensuring anonymity.
A set of information related to this transaction is electronically signed by a dual key encryption system.
Thus, the network can verify the legitimacy of the transaction
Thanks to the contents of the file, it is also possible to ensure that exchanged bitcoins exist in a public current account book that is distributed throughout the network.
The key phase lies in writing this new transaction into this book.
It proceeds by solving the mathematical task specified by the computers, the solver of which, a temporary central banker, will have the right to complete this additional line.
This is the phase of dismantling the file; i.e. the transformation of a huge file into a shorter and uniquitive digital imprint.
Computers "take over" this new transaction and add a number to it, only then "cut" the whole.
The goal is to find a number that provides a specific imprint (a lot of zers at the beginning).
As soon as this number is found, the other nodes will easily check its correctness.
Transactions are then indestructibly displayed connected to a string of all other transactions; any adjustment would result in a change of imprint.
If the user would like to pay twice with the same money very quickly (within ten minutes), the network will confirm only one of both transactions - the other will be abandoned, because each of them has a different imprint.
The computer that solves the challenge wins 50 bitcoins.
In order to avoid inflation, this remuneration is regularly shared by two, probably by the end of 2012.
Thus, the number of bitcoins in circulation is limited to 21 million, but they can be divided up to a hundred-millionth, so there is room for the margin...
The difficulty of the assigned task also increases with an increasing level of calculation.
The network has already experienced its rises and falls.
Websites that provide services for Bitcoin have been hacked and stored bitcoins have been stolen.
“The loophole used doesn’t concern the protocol itself,” said Pierre Noizat, who founded Paymium, a real-money payment company that uses the Bitcoin network.
The ECB is also reviewing money laundering options thanks to this anonymous service.
But this problem also applies to cash.
Prominent players like Wikipedia reject gifts of this nature.
Others, as a WordPress blog platform, accept them.
Adi Shamir and Dorit Ron of the Weizmann Institute in Israel have recently analyzed a book of current accounts and proved that almost 80% of bitcoins do not circulate.
Huge discounts were launched in November.
"Thirty-thousand dollars have been exchanged," rejoices Jon Holmquist, who works for Coinabul to convert bitcoins into gold.
Pierre Noizat, who is also the author of a pedagogical book about this money, has great confidence in the potential of this technology as a transaction network.
Its system, Paytunia, is equivalent to a credit card (with real money) or non-contact mobile payment, but uses the Bitcoin system to authenticate transactions, which makes it cheaper.
In addition, the bearer manages his identity and can remain anonymous.
The system is easy to set up with merchants where there is no need to install new terminals or software.
It is enough to communicate an address that can be "photographed and recognized" by the phone, Pierre Noizat specifies that it already has thousands of users.
There is a general movement to question the hierarchical systems and the transition to more horizontal systems.
“It’s going to take a while for Bitcoin, it’s going to take a while, but 2013 could be crucial.”
Moreover, in its report, the ECB foresees a re-evaluation of the various risks that are now considered increased if this kind of money is successful.
We've left Afghanistan.
And what's next?
French troops have left their area of responsibility in Afghanistan (Kapisa and Surobi).
NATO and U.S
It's time for the Afghan army to take control of its territory and for the Afghans to choose their own future and not wait for everything from us.
The greatest harm was suffered by people in the Afghan countryside, which we punished, because we considered them terrorists.
And so do we, for we have lost 88 slain soldiers, and all those wounded and crippled.
Talibans consist of foreign extremists, former leaders who fled to Pakistan, but are often peasants who reject the presence of foreign armies as in Soviet times.
They just want to defend their ancient and archaic traditions, even if they were joined by jihadists, Pakistanis, Arabs, Uzbeks, Tajiks.
They are now experiencing tolerance and frequent assistance from local rebels, but if Western forces withdraw, their support will decline.
The departure of the French troops from the Nijrab foundation, which I watched from the peaks of almond mountains thanks to French loans, took place orderly.
Convoys of trucks and armored vehicles arrived in Kabul without being attacked by helicopter escorts.
From the end of 2014 in Kabul, the Taliban will no longer be as active.
Since their unstoppable procedure between 1994 and 1996, conditions have changed considerably.
Kabul was empty at the time and the earth was in blood and fire as a result of struggles between different resistance groups.
Their takeover of control of the country at that time was considered a kind of liberation, a return to security.
The Afghans paid the price for the obscurity of these peasants, which was completely dominated by Al-Qaeda, but their situation has not improved to our time.
Former mujahideen, the Afghan government and current Taliban agree in an effort to keep women in subordinate standing.
Principal dignitaries from the war against Soviet troops returned to power in 2001.
They became businessmen, seized state-owned land, which they sold as building land to refugees from Iran and Pakistan, and used huge U.S. contracts for subcontractors.
They discredited themselves; most of them, moreover, were never even in combat.
From what I've heard in campaigns, people long for a government they won't create thieves.
Many young people want to leave like those who managed to take advantage of U.S. generosity: capital flight is considerable.
Young people are tired of war and associated ideologies.
During exiles in Iran or Pakistan, they got to know the modern world and were intrigued by its advantages.
About 65% of the population is under the age of 25; there are currently 5 million inhabitants in Kabul, a fifth of the total population.
In cities, public schools are full, girls and boys go there.
These young people will have to be given jobs, they no longer want to return to the obscurity of the former parties or to the corruption of some representatives.
Everyone, including the armed opposition, likes mobile phones; television and Turkish series that show the modern world, watch everywhere.
The military is already present.
Will the authorities who take the lead be considered legitimate?
Former commanders of the fight against the Soviet army are already renewing provincial militias, which will be outside the control of central power.
Afghanistan, a mountainous country with a strong influence of local identity, should look forward to a certain degree of decentralization following the example of Western states, but the U.S. wanted to turn it into a centralized state with a strong presidential influence, so the post of prime minister, which has existed since the adoption of the constitution in 1964, was abolished.
President Karzai doesn't want foreign controls, especially on the occasion of elections planned for April 2014.
However, his country has been heavily dependent on foreign aid since the 50’s and previous years.
No industry has managed to recover, no dam is working, no major irrigation system has been repaired.
Everything is imported; nothing is produced, only fruits and vegetables.
Priority is given to a private initiative.
In a country devastated by three decades of wars, government infrastructure control would be necessary.
Rumours spread that Afghanistan possessed enormous mineral wealth.
And that only gave rise to the feeling that Western states were here only to seize it.
Without energy for the processing of iron ores or copper on the site and without means of transporting raw materials through the mountains, however, no mineral extraction can take place.
The Chinese have almost left the Mes Aynak copper mine, leaving international archaeological teams (with the support of the World Bank) the opportunity to explore the vast Buddhist compound to retain the role of the most important employer in the entire province.
One day it will also be necessary for Afghanistan and Pakistan, on which a large part of both imports and exports depend, to restore normal relations.
The departure of French combat troops was completed on November 20.
The new cooperation treaty envisages the continuation of common types of aid: girls' and boys' lyceums, departments of French at universities, the French institute, cooperation in military, legal, medical and agricultural areas, support for the archaeological delegation.
Since 2009 there has been an initiative to "gain a heart and soul" attempting the difficult task of reconciling aid and military activities, in which the "civic-army steps" of the Ministry of Defense (Cimic), which was abolished in 2012, achieved a number of achievements in the area of common interest and steps for agricultural reconstruction in dozens of mountain villages.
These works, in which a number of local workers were involved, have helped dampen the uprising: irrigation, wells, drinking water, afforestation, fruit trees, soil protection, and the expansion of the cultivable area.
What memory will we leave behind after two billion euros of military spending?
A much more modest budget would contribute to improving local living conditions, which are very challenging in these valleys, which are often located at an altitude above 2 000 m.
The embassy received dozens of written requests for minor agricultural assistance from local communities in Kapisa province.
In order for these farmers to free themselves from an uprising led by foreign groups, they wish, in their words, to obtain slight civic assistance for their benefit, which would be properly controlled and directly affected.
Difficult work on the Egyptian constitution
President Mohammed Morsi embarked on another game of poker.
While Egypt remains the most divided in its history due to the proclamation of a constitution that temporarily grants him unlimited powers, he has decided to play vabank.
On Wednesday, he announced, regardless of everyone else's opinion, that the Constituency Assembly would approve the final text the next day.
Less than a week ago, the head of state gave this assembly another two months to complete the necessary work.
For almost two years now, Egypt has been using a makeshift text that has been supplemented many times, weakening the country's constitutional stability and leading to legal conjectures.
This new initiative has only further strengthened the country's ambivalence.
From the point of view of the opposition, the president persists in his "autocratic delirium," continues to "violate his word" and "tramples on the law."
However, his supporters assure that this is a means of dealing as quickly as possible with the constitutional and political crisis and accelerating the transitional process.
There will be a referendum in two weeks.
It is a very short period of time, which will force the Brotherhood to withdraw from the intention of the text to the Egyptians to clarify the article after the article.
For the president, it's also a way to regain national and democratic legitimacy, while the whole country is buffeted by protests.
As he confirmed in an interview with U.S. weekly Time, Muhammad Morsi seems convinced that the Egyptians will vote in favour.
Particularly because this hastily organized vote puts the Egyptian people before the ultimatum: "Either you approve my text, or I keep the unlimited powers" that would otherwise end after the approval of the new constitution.
And in this special atmosphere yesterday, 85 deputies of the above-mentioned constituential assembly, with a large majority of Islamists, approved the text.
Most of the Liberals were absent from the vote.
In mid-November, not long before the constitution was proclaimed, they slammed the door, saying they had not been able to assert their views.
Defenders of human rights, civil society or members of religious minorities mimicked their move.
In order to achieve a quorum, 11 substitutes were hastily added yesterday morning.
Some of them are very close to the Muslim Brotherhood.
Unsurprisingly then, the articles of the constitution were often voted unanimously.
The commentators continued to pause that one of the few controversies revolved that day around... an hour for prayer, when some members of the committee were of the opinion that the hours in the boardroom were not properly adjusted.
The text, which was voted on last night, consists of 234 articles.
The subject of general attention, Article 2, ultimately remains consistent with the 1971 version of the Constitution and provides that "the principles of Sharia law are the main source of law."
Salafist parties, for whom the introduction of Islamic law is one of the fundamental demands, hoped to replace the term "principles" with the term "rules," which would allow stricter application.
For Islamists, the fact that this article has not been corrected represents a sign of their goodwill and respect for other parts of Egyptian society.
"Hypocrisy," argue liberals, who see only a rhetorical problem.
According to them, the Islamization of the constitution takes place through other articles.
They focus in particular on Article 220, which entrusts al-Azhar University with an advisory role, in particular on the issues of controlling the conformity of laws with Sharia law.
According to Sophie Pommier, an expert on Egypt, this is disturbing because "the persons summoned for expression are not elected and have no democratic legitimacy.
The beginnings of theocracy can be seen."
Liberals' fears are fueled even further by the fact that the university's next rector should be less moderate than the existing one.
“For now, there has been no particular religious consequence.
With this constitution, Egypt remains a civic state in a framework.
Most of the lawyers who worked on this text aren't ulamas, but university workers, many of them graduated in French schools," moderates the concerns Alexis Blouet, who prepares work on the constitutional transition in Egypt.
It agrees, however, that "there may be contradictions regarding the interpretation of Article 220, since the terms used in it belong to the religious dictionary.
There is, in particular, a reference to "fiqh".
And in the future, the question may arise as to the extent to which civilian judges are competent to comment."
In addition to religious pages, the text approved yesterday is highly criticized because of the extended powers it grants to the President of the Republic.
The Muslim Brotherhood insists that there has been significant restrictions against the former regime.
Another contested place: the powers conferred on the army.
In accordance with the wishes of the representatives of the army, the verification of the defense budget will not be carried out by parliament, but by the National Defense Council.
Furthermore, proceedings against civilians will not be banned in military courts, as demanded by the human rights association.
These associations are talking about their doubts in connection with the text, which they believe is directed against freedom.
The crime of blasphemy is preserved and the insult continues to be banned, which could have serious consequences for freedom of expression, especially for the press.
Moreover, no article deals with the protection of women, emphasizes Heba Morayef of Human Rights Watch.
According to her, the only positive point is the prohibition of torture referred to in Article 36.
This word has not been mentioned in the previous constitution.
While the Egyptian president was scheduled to speak in a televised address last night, demonstrations are scheduled for this afternoon.
Supporters of the head of state will then manifest on Saturday.
In Israel, holy places, the navel of the world and the sea from a saturated saturated salt solution are waiting for Ukrainian tourists
The Promised Land combines the greatness of biblical truths, modern comfort and the intactness of nature.
AaF has identified the five most important reasons why you should visit Israel.
Bowing to the Holy Places
Visit the Jordan River, where Jesus was baptized.
It is claimed that God's mercy will descend on all those who enter this "spa".
And Galilee - instead of the miracles of Jesus: the transformation of water into wine at the wedding, walking on the water, calming the storm and filling the nets.
Here Jesus also appeared to his disciples and after the resurrection.
But most holy places are in Jerusalem.
Believers will walk the Path of Pain, or the Way of the Cross of Jesus (Latina Via Dolorosa).
It begins at the fortress of Antonia - Pretoria - where the trial took place, passes through the streets of the Old Town and leads to the Church of the Holy Sepulchre on Mount Golgotha - to the place of crucifixion, to the stone of anointing and the place where Jesus was buried.
Here is the symbolic Christian navel of the world, which symbolizes the salvation of humanity.
And the Monastery of the Holy Cross in Jerusalem was built on the site where, according to Christian legend, a tree was planted, from which they made a cross for Jesus’ crucifixion.
There is also the holiest place in Jerusalem for the Jews - the Wall of Lamentations, the rest of the temple, which was destroyed by the Romans in 70 AD.
According to tradition, people of different creeds leave notes with wishes, which are then fulfilled.
Take a trip to the vertical
The ruins of the fortress of Masada remained from a secret hiding place from the enemy that Herodes the Great built for his family in 25 BC.
The ruin is located on cliffs in the mountains at an altitude of 450 m above sea level.
Only those engaged in mountaineering can access it by themselves.
Others will be transported to this historic mountain by cable car.
And in the north of the country at an altitude of 1600-2040 m there is the well-known ski resort Hermon, which is experiencing a huge influx of tourists during the winter months.
A shuttle bus takes tourists from the foot of the hill to this ski paradise.
The total length of mountain slopes is 45 km.
According to an ancient legend, pagan gods supposedly lived at the top of the mountain.
Visit unique museums
There are about 300 museums in this country.
You can’t visit everyone in one trip.
But at least five of them, the most interesting, are definitely worth a visit.
Among them is the Museum of Israel, located near the Knesset (Parliament).
It houses ancient Kumran manuscripts, or Dead Sea coils, which have been found in Caves of the Judean Desert, as well as approximately 500 thousand archaeological and anthropological exhibits.
The Tel-Aviv Art Museum is also worth a visit.
Impressionists and expressionists Monet, Pissarro, Renoir, Cézanne, Sisley, Matisse, Modigliani, Chagall, Picasso are widely represented in its canvases.
In Akkon you can visit the Al Basha museum, which presents visitors with several rooms of ancient Turkish spa with mannequins of spa users and spa nurses from that time.
In Caesarea, it is worth a visit to the unique private Ralli Museum, where you can admire the statues of Dalí and Rodeno.
There's no souvenir guides or souvenir shops.
Admission is free and donations are strictly rejected.
The Fifth Museum - the Museum of the Holocaust or Yad Vashem in Tel Aviv - tells of one of history's most dramatic sites.
The most tragic part of the museum - the Children's Memorial - was built to commemorate 1.5 million children who were murdered in concentration camps and gas chambers.
You enter - and you find yourself in utter darkness.
The stars glitter.
And there are names of Jewish children and the names of the countries in which they perished.
Ukraine is also mentioned.
Improving your health
Israel's three main spa resorts are located on the Mediterranean, Red and Dead Sea coasts.
It offers swimming pools, water parks, dolphinariums and oceanariums.
Interestingly, it is possible to bathe in the Red Sea even in the winter months, because the water temperature in it does not drop below 21 degrees Celsius and the air heats up to 23 degrees.
The dead sea is even warmer, and in it people bathe all year round.
By the way, the dead sea is the most peculiar sea in the world, located at the lowest mainland point of the planet - at 417 m below sea level.
Côte d'Azur water is a saturated salt solution that is easy to maintain even people who can't float.
The surrounding landscape is unrealistically beautiful.
Come here to undergo treatment with the use of salt water - brine, and medicinal mud, which can treat health problems with dermatitis, allergies, asthma, eczema, arthritis, bronchitis, diabetes to heal your ruffled nerves.
Touch a mysterious ancient past
It is protected by an ancient part of Tel Aviv - the town of Jaffa on the Mediterranean coast.
A famous nautical route linking Egypt, Syria, Anatolia and Mesopotamia passed through them.
Mentions of this city are found in the oldest Greek and Egyptian legends.
According to the legend, it is the place where Noe built his ark and Perseus saved the beautiful Andromeda, with whom they lived a long and happy life together.
Tourists indeed love wandering through narrow streets, named after zodiac signs.
It is said that if you press yourself against the walls in the aisle of "your" sign, your happiness will be favorably inclined to you.
In Jaffa, you may meet newlyweds who come here from all over Israel and even from other countries to be photographed.
And in Caesarea - in the city of King Herod - you can walk through the Roman theatre and "conquer" the fortress of the Crusaders.
In Roman times, Caesarea was the capital of Judea and the seat of Roman prosecutors, including Pontius Pilate.
A carefully reconstructed theatre is now used for evening concerts and opera performances.
Tourists, for a word
If you're going to Israel, you don't have to worry about your imperfect English: in this country, about 30% of the population speaks Russian.
It is better to take money with you not in euros, but in dollars, which you can easily exchange for shekels (now 1 dollar is approximately 3.8 shekels).
There are mainly buses, but there is a speed tram in Jerusalem, and in the city of Haifa there is the onling metro line in the country with six stations, which connects the lower city with the upper one.
It's basically an underground cableway.
Traveling through all kinds of urban public transport costs 6 shekels, for which you can drive for 1.5 hours.
According to Jewish tradition, the Sabbath is celebrated in Israel on Saturdays.
From Friday evening to sunset on Saturday, it stops working in marketplaces, in shops and on public transport.
And on Sunday morning, the working week begins.
In many cafes, restaurants and hotels, you will only receive kosher meals: with the exception of pork, seafood, fish without scales and dishes, which contain combinations of milk and meat.
They offer a large selection of dishes made of lamb and beef, soups and desserts, prepared from coconut milk, traditional Jewish porridge hummus, all sorts of sauces, balls of minced chickpeas - falafel, fruits and vegetables.
There are no stray dogs on the streets of Israeli cities.
However, there are very many fattened cats, which "roam according to their taste".
In the evening you can see them sleeping even on the roofs of parked cars.
Paving cats love populated places and tastefully embark on every kindness.
According to the car brand, renting a car will cost from 37 dollars per day (Hyundai Getz) to 188 dollars (Audi A6, Volvo S80) per day.
Plus insurance - 15 dollars a day.
And renting a bike will cost around 15 shekels a day.
Tickets to museums cost an average of 30 shekels.
Expressed by numbers
More than three million tourists from all over the world visited Israel in 2012.
Most U.S., Russia, France, Germany, Italy, England and Ukraine have come to get to know the country and relax.
From January to October 2021, 118.8 thousand Ukrainian tourists visited the promised country, which is 51% more than in the same period in 2010, before the abolition of visas on February 9, 2011.
"Great and powerful" will announce it to Moscow: emigrants will save money on their tongues
While MPs and human rights defenders are still debating the law on mandatory language exams, there have already been fraudes in the country selling "forged" certificates
13 million foreign workers come to Moscow, St. Petersburg and other Russian cities every year.
Most of them are citizens of Central Asia: Uzbekistan, Tajikistan, Turkmenia.
Their only goal is to make enough money to support families at home.
On December 1, the law came into effect, according to which every working migrant will be required to pass an exam from the knowledge of the Russian language.
So far, this rule applies only to those who intend to work in the services, housing and municipal economy sectors, lifestyle and retail sales.
FMS, however, promises that in time, the relevant tests will be mandatory for all visitors.
However, the exams will consist not only of linguistic knowledge, but also of Russian history and the basis of law.
The knowledge of the language will also have to be confirmed for obtaining and extending a work permit.
The exception applies only to citizens of countries where Russian is the official language.
Even people who have successfully achieved attestation and obtained an academic diploma until the collapse of the USSR in 1991 are not subject to this law.
Benefit, Conviction and Protection of Rights
A network of rehearsal sites will work under the auspices of Pushkin's Institute of Russian Language, the University of Friendship between Peoples, the Moscow State University, the State University of St. Petersburg and other Russian colleges.
Migrants can pass exams in all cities; more than 160 such centers are already opened in Russia today.
Members of the State Duma and FMS (Federal Migration Services) also supported the initiative to support the introduction of testing.
But before the law came into force, human rights defenders were able to ask the question many times in the press: and what will it really bring?
What will change the obligation to know the language for the Russians and for the visitors themselves?
Thus, first of all - the representatives of the migration service correspond - it will make it possible to reduce the number of people suffering as a result of "slavish work."
Many people today are talking about protecting the rights of migrant workers, explains Viktor Sebelev, Chairman of the Board of Directors of FMS of Russia in Tajikistan.
The protection of their rights should begin prior to their departure.
Only the system of organized recruitment will 90% solve the problems of foreign workers.
Problems arise in emigrants without professions, without education, who do not know the language and do not have a medical examination.
Without the knowledge of the language - he says with the conviction of Sebelev - a future migrant is doomed to clashes with rogue people who, under the pretext of aid, ensure a "voucher" into gruesome, tight-lived hostels, in which he and dozens of others, like himself, will suffer for months without food and documents and work for 12 to 14 hours a day.
We receive a lot of complaints from our migrant workers.
"In their homeland, they promise one thing, but after their arrival they cheat them, confiscate their passports, do not pay their promised wages," confirms the head of the Main Tajik Labour Migration Administration MS Tolib Sharipov.
Don't curse!
All the less so, that many citizens of the Central Asian republics, who intend to go to work in Russia, recognize that they not only don’t know the language of the country they want to get into, but they don’t even have experience with their work.
Of course, this is not so much their fault, but rather misfortune:very few Turkmens, Uzbeks, Tajiks can afford to get at least a reasonable basic education.
After all, their families also need to feed children something, not to mention the fact that they need to be dressed decently, worn and equipped for school.
As children mature into working age, they have to go for work at the first opportunity.
Without knowledge of the language, it's hard - they admit.
"You feel humiliated and inferior."
It is true that, in the light of the Linguistic Examination Act, human rights defenders point out one important matter.
Testing will only be carried out for those migrants with legal status.
If they don't have it, they won't either take exams or get any other official work.
In the meantime, most migrating workers live illegally, as they used to be in Russia.
"Welcome, or Uncalled Entrance Prohibited"
Many of the incoming workers say that getting official status in our country is not easy.
The reason - bureaucratic obstacles and all those linguistic difficulties.
In addition, legalization costs money: from 12 to 16 thousand rubles.
But false registration can be obtained quickly and only one and a half thousand.
The employees of the Russian police know that we have basically fake documents, without registration, and this results in cases of extortion.
"They require a hundred, two for cigarettes, for tea," said Umed Khushkadamov, a citizen of Tajikistan, with reporters.
"Come on, don't be stingy, buy some painting"
On the first day of the application of the Language Act, it was found that not only migrants' evidence of registration was false.
FMS employees have already seized several false certificates of passing the language exam.
Counterfeitted documents are printed on an ordinary color printer.
Of course, they did not get to the new owners for free: each of the migrants, in the hope that it would facilitate the problem of passing the tests, paid seven thousand rubles for this document.
That's two-and-a-half times higher than passing an official test: it will cost migrating workers three thousand rubles.
Thus, the main task of the immediate future is to secure the system from corruption, so that workers cannot simply buy the documents, in which government officials and human rights defenders agree.
In the meantime, government authorities can promise foreign workers who have failed tests for the first time, the necessary time to take a course in the basics of the Russian language.
For visitors who do not speak Russian, it is assumed to work in areas where active dealing with people is not required.
MVD (Ministry of the Interior) does not take into circulation the illegal arms market
The proportion of offences using legally held weapons is very low
The MVD of Russia presents a proposal to tighten the law in relation to owners of civilian weapons.
It is the reaction of government authorities to recent events: the stimulus was cases of gunfire at weddings, which were without casualties, and a massacre prepared by the Moscow lawyer Dimitry Vinogradov, which resulted in the death of seven people.
Police wants to ban the carrying of dangerous weapons in public places and increase the age from which a license can be obtained to carry weapons - from 18 to 21.
This idea is supported by Irina Jarovaja, the head of the Duma Committee on Security and Corruption, which has vowed that amendments to the Weapons Act will be submitted to the State Duma in the near future.
Not everyone is satisfied that Russian state authorities are trying to deal with the problem of "tightening screws."
An open letter has appeared on the net, whose authors - representatives of various social shooting organizations - demand the rejection of the "senseless tightening" of the law.
The percentage of hooliganism with registered weapons is minimal, Vasily Lesnikov, a lawyer and criminalist, said in an interview with Russia's BBC editorial.
According to the statistics of the Ministry of the Interior, in the six months of 2012, 142 offences from the use of firearms registered by the authorities of the Ministry of the Interior were committed, while over the same period, a total of 1168 thousand offences were committed.
The authors of the open letter are convinced, the tightening of civil weapons legislation will not prompt the perpetrators to switch to the "black" market.
According to them, it is currently possible to find any weapon in Moscow at a low price.
At the moment, the Ministry of the Interior maintains that the situation with the proliferation of illegal weapons is under control.
Suppliers: from plants to officers
The "Black" arms market is supplied by several channels.
There are five main canals, says retired colonel Victor Baranec, who has worked for ten years at the workplaces of the Ministry of Education and the general staff.
Screenshot pages that accept gun orders
The first of these - an "army or military route", that is, a weapon that has been stolen during fighting in the Caucasus.
"Weapons were also stolen by Russian officers and caucasians themselves," Baranec says.
Another type of "black weapons" are guns stolen by criminals from security forces officials.
Baranec explains that this includes theft from police depots and weapons stolen directly from employees of law enforcement agencies.
Illegal "mainly" goes on sale from military depots.
In many military depots there are often explosions.
"It has been proven that some of the fires were established deliberately - in order to obscure the mank," the former soldier says.
According to Baranca, gun manufacturers also bring their contribution.
“At the moment, there are private arms companies that aren’t competitive on the international market and decommission the weapons they deliver to the black market, including Moscow,” he said.
Another source of the "black" market is smuggling.
A particularly large number of pistols and vending machines come from poor countries, such as Kyrgyzstan.
"That's where their production is there, sometimes weapons are produced in small-scale production, and a mafia was created to organize their smooth production," explains the former soldier.
Where'd the gun come from?
Experts added what is the approximate share of the individual sources of supply of illegal weapons to the "black" market.
A report on this issue was prepared in 2011 by the Centre for the Analysis of Problems and Design of Public Governance.
The specialists analyzed the statements of the Ministry of the Interior and the Russian Statistical Office, criminological literature and data of public data portals focused on the topic of weapons.
The vast majority of illegal weapons, the researchers say, come from military and executive bodies.
Half of all guns have found themselves on the black market "due to officials whose work is associated with weapons," the report said.
According to the data available to the researchers, 17% of weapons come from armed conflicts, in 14% of cases there has been theft of weapons in their production, 5% is "black archaeology".
The salesman-consultant of one of the gun shops, who asked us not to mention his name, is convinced that weapons found in excavations by black "archaeologists" are no longer bought - they are simply too old.
Most often, as he says, sellers go for new shipments directly to military warehouses.
Through an ensign, a bidder buys a unit, such as the TT pistol.
You order a gun from him, and he sells it over the fence.
"He'll take it to the city and sell it there for 900 euros apiece with two trays," he recounts.
"The truth is that the police are well in the picture, and therefore, whenever a plan in the clarification of offences begins, they carry out a control purchase with illegal arms dealers," the adviser adds.
"Like in a luxury shop"
The seller and the buyer most often find each other through their acquaintances.
I visited websites and blogs until there was a guy who offered me the opportunity to switch to the "Testing" platform, where there will be a man who would lead me to a "corner" where we'll act together.
I only learned the price there
Military correspondent Viktor Baraněc
If I want to acquire a weapon, I need an acquaintance who knows the way out, tells the salesman - the adviser.- I have such an acquaintance, but I don't trust him fully.
Sellers are on the market, but there is also a need to go, hypothetically "from Mr. Ivan Ivanovich, who asked me to deal that my daughter's tooth fell out."
And now I'm going to get there through someone I'm sure of.
He'll only supply them to me, because he knows I won't.
Beginners look for weapons in different ways.
Former soldier Viktor Baranec tried to buy illegal weapons in the mid-1990s, when he handled an article for publication on this topic.
According to him, the schemes have not changed.
They gave him an album with photos where "everything you thought."
"I felt like I was in a luxury store," the soldier recalled.
To the buyer, in the words of Baraněce, they do not offer rabbits in a bag - you can try everything.
I, a potential client, just don't buy - we go with the seller into the woods and build a target.
"They'll give me a chance to shoot, and if I'll make sure the gun shoots well, we're starting to act," the connoisseur said.
Container on the sofa
The search engines on the Internet will bring you to websites and groups on "Contact", where it is offered to buy a weapon "for different purposes".
This does not require any documents or face-to-face meetings.
"It's enough to have a specified amount of money," they write in an advertising headline on the website "Buy a fighting gun or a vending machine."
On this site, users leave their notifications and ask questions.
And selling to minors?
"Of course, without a license," the "Ivan" user inquires (the spelling is retained).
"Buy TT.Moscow" - succinctly formulates "Fedorenkov" request.
The Federal Security Service has recently dispersed a huge network of planted websites, and persons wishing to buy a military weapon may be raking with a shovel.
Humans flock like hungry fish to bait and then conquer coal in Siberia.
Military correspondent and senior military dignitary Viktor Baranec
I've heard about such schemes: websites are usually registered beyond the scope of Russian laws.
People are taking orders.
The buyer makes a payment through an ATM.
"In response, the seller sends him a photograph and a description with instructions on where the gun is hidden," said Dmitry Kislov, spokesperson for the NGO "Weapon Right".
Viktor Baraněc confirms that after placing an order on the website, you can stay unarmed and find yourself behind bars.
The Federal Security Service has recently dispersed a huge network of planted websites, and persons wishing to buy a military weapon may be raking with a shovel.
"People flock like hunger fish to bait and then conquer coal in Siberia," he confirms.
Makarov for 100 dollars
When buying an illegal firearm, according to the estimate of experts, you will pay an amount usually from 100 to 900 dollars.
According to Dmitry Kislov from the non-governmental organization "Right to a Weapon", you will buy a Makarov pistol for 100 to 300 dollars.
You have to wait a month for up to one and a half months.
Weapons are dispatched from warehouses for long-term storage through the warehouses of the middle article.
According to the official statistics office, compared with the January-October 2011 period, the number of these offences in Russia fell by a total of 7%, amounting to 22.9 thousand cases, and the number of clarified cases of theft and extortion of weapons, ammunition, explosives and explosive devices decreased by 7.8%.
