In recent days, there have been a wave of protests in America against low wages in Walmart's hypermarkets and in restaurants of popular fast-food chains, such as "McDonald," "Burger King," "Taco Bell," "Wendy's" and "Kentucky Fried Chicken."
So far, no one can predict whether this wave will be transformed into a ninth wave, or whether it is destined to disappear soon.
This whole share is supported by unions and a number of left-wing social organizations.
In addition to increasing modest salaries received by rank-and-file workers in Walmart and fast-food systems, the goal of protest organizers is to achieve creation in their trade unions.
At present, this economic sector is practically untouched by the trade union movement.
46 cents a year?
Actions began last week after Thanksgiving, on the so-called "Black Friday," when an orgy of cheap sell-offs struck America, engulfed millions of residents and, in some cases, was accompanied by brawls.
On this day, some Walmart employees, in which 2.2 million people work worldwide, have left their workplaces and demonstrated together with unions and left-wing activists of multinational chain companies whose goods are intended for people in the lower and middle classes.
Walmart sells everything around the world, from diapers to hunting rifles and car batteries to vacuum cleaners, eggs and milk.
Products in its stores are on average 8% to 27% cheaper than in large supermarkets.
That’s why many employees with low wages in Walmart stores only shop at work.
The availability and range of goods have made the Walmart chain one of the largest companies in America.
According to critics, Walmart can afford to sell goods so cheaply partly because he pays little for his employees.
They also complain about poor working conditions, such as the lack of automatic wheelchairs or handheld scanners.
Protesters on "Black Friday" demanded a salary hike, complaining that the cost of health insurance provided by the company had risen sharply from 30 to 100 dollars a month.
Typical Walmart worker receives 9.5 dollars per hour, but that doesn't matter the amount of expenses, employees say.
Scientists at the University of California in Berkeley show that if Walmart raises the average wage to $12 per hour, it will cost the company €3.2 billion a year.
It's an average of 1.1% more than the company spends on salaries now.
If Walmart shifts the wage increase fully to consumers' shoulders, each visit to the store will cost only 46 cents more.
In a year, customers spend approximately 12.39 dollars more than they do today.
Walmart's attorneys point out that the protest took place in only nine countries and companies should do virtually no harm.
"Black Friday" in the company's stores lasted from 8 o'clock in the evening Thursday until midnight of the following day, and during that time Walmart sold about 5000 items of goods per second.
In total, its coffers carried out nearly 100 million transactions on "Black Friday."
The company's representative, Dan Fogelman, said in an interrogation for the left-leaning Huffington Post website that a total of "less than five" Walmart employees had left jobs, and that the protest was all "another media sensation" organized by the unions.
"Unoccupied cash register!"
Protests continued this week in New York, where their targets were not Walmart department stores (those in that progressive city aren't much popular, and that's why they're almost not here), but "McDonald" and other cheap restaurants.
McDonald says he sells billions of portions, and then doesn't even pay you sick pay and doesn't pay you properly for your honest work!
Jumaane Williams, New York City Council member
Currently, the minimum wage for working in accordance with federal laws and New York state regulations is 7.25 dollars per hour.
Fast-food restaurants are gradually increasing this wage, but only slightly, and, on average, their rank-and-file workers in New York are getting 8.90 dollars an hour.
There's no one with a lower wage in this expensive city.
I don't quite understand how you can exist in New York for that kind of money.
Nearly a quarter of U.S. teenagers who work behind a part-time counter after school and live with their parents go through their jobs at "McDonald."
Few people see their main source of livelihood or plan to stay there for long.
At the moment, I constantly meet "McDonald" workers who complain that they have to live out of this money and sometimes even feed their children.
On the other hand, there's a commentary on the Wall Street Journal forum whose author notes it's irresponsible to have children if you don't know how to feed them.
Participants of the protests, which began at «McDonalda's 40th Street and on Madison Avenue at 6:30 a.m
They also demanded that unions be established in the fast-food sector.
U.S. law prohibits the administration from preventing the establishment of trade unions and punishing trade union activists with bullying and redundancies.
On the other hand, governance rarely makes their lives easier.
However, it is also difficult to introduce trade unions in fast-food establishments for objective reasons.
One of the main reasons is an unusually high employee turnover.
Disagreement
Noisy protests took place in a number of Manhattan's other inexpensive restaurants.
The culmination of that event was at noon a gathering at "McDonalda" near Times Square, where a number of local Democratic politicians performed, especially City Council member Jumaane Williams, who said, "McDonald" says he's selling billions of portions, and then doesn't even pay you sick and pay you properly for your honest work!"
The protesters also received the support of other prominent New York Democrats, such as City mayor Bill de Blasio, who said that "we have to speak out together in support of fast-food workers so that they can achieve the fair reward for their work and the economic prosperity that every New Yorker deserves!"
As the New York Times described it, it was the largest event of its kind in the history of the U.S. fast-food sector.
But only a few hundred people took part in it, although not all of them were fast-food employees, who employ tens of thousands of people in New York.
Whether this event will become a spark that will ignite the flame of the mass movement is still unclear.
"So far, the brain manages to mislead on average"
Among fans of modern virtual reality technologies is the popular theme of augmented reality; more recently, this means perceiving the world especially through the prism of special glasses.
At the beginning of the summer at its annual conference, Google unveiled a working model of this news, and in November it was announced that Microsoft had also filed an application for an appropriate patent.
An interview with the head of the interactive 3D technology group at Microsoft's Cambridge laboratory, Sharam Izadi, shows that for the company's scientists, glasses are a thing of the past.
They are attracted by the prospect of manipulating virtual objects in the air with bare hands, the creation of virtual public spaces.
- Please tell us in general terms, what does your research group deal with?
- We deal with the interactions of humans with machines, but at the same time we try to shift the boundaries of this interaction.
So far, humanity is stuck in the stage of working with pixels on a flat screen, at most sometimes touching the screen with its finger.
We want to look forward for 5-10 years and anticipate significant changes in this interaction.
For example, the Xbox console and Kinect sensors - this is a step forward, and almost no Xbox is for sale today without Kinect sensors, because for everyone there is a very interesting ability to drive using gestures.
- What's waiting for us in the future?
- Despite the fact that the Kinect system transferred the interaction to a physical level, very many interactions still take place on a flat screen, sometimes in 3D.
The input flow of information has improved (the system receives a larger amount of data), but the output flow is still not particularly.
We're trying to change that, we're working on truly three-dimensional imaging systems based on a variety of technologies, including projectives.
It is necessary to let the computer world into our physical world, to make it more real.
However, for this purpose, it is necessary to get to know not only the user, but also the space around him.
Then we will be able to add virtual objects to the real world in a much more suitable form.
And in the first place - to hell with those stupid virtual reality helmets!
- What do you think about voice control?
That's a popular thing, but doesn't overestimate?
- Surely it cannot be considered a panacea - the question of privacy arises, because we do not always want to inform others in our surroundings about their activities and intentions.
In fact, all types of interaction with computers are good, but in their own environment.
For example, to control devices in public places, we had a project in which we thought about gestures, but not quirky ones, only about successful, short ones.
Gestures would not be captured by the camera of the device, but by a bracelet that would monitor the movements of bones and muscles.
So far it is large, but theoretically it can be reduced to the size of a wristwatch.
In general, however, the future has a mixed type of control, for example, gesture + voice.
- And how?
For example, how do you ask me to give you this can of lemonade?
You'll say it and you'll show at the same time.
- I usually just talk.
- Oh, it's going to be very difficult to recognize.
- So you want to get users to adapt to what the machine can or can’t at any given time?
It's not necessary at all, but it's a rapprochement.
I think that in the near future we will have to work primarily on the development of new types of sensors that can more accurately identify human reactions.
These can be, for example, laser sensors that provide a fairly good depth resolution, this is very important.
- If you’re talking about your work with Xbox Kinect sensors, what are your requirements for modern cameras?
Isn’t there enough resolution, depth or something else today?
- Basically the current generation – that’s what we can bounce back from in our work on three-dimensional recognition.
Of course, it would be a good idea to earn 8 megapixels at 1000 f/s.
But it's not just about the megapixels themselves, it's like matrix and depth.
At the current point of view, all current technologies are insufficient for us - this adds to the work of algorithm designers.
So we have to keep in mind not only the resolution in X and Y axes, but also Z.
So very important is also the speed, the number of frames per second.
Human movement is fairly dynamic, and the current 30-frame data streams per second are, frankly, a little small, especially for gestures.
Stephen Betishes from our laboratory in Redmond made a touch sensor with an adjustable processing delay of 1 to 100 ms, while the current, mass-produced sensors are closer to the second value (60-100 ms).
Not everyone understands the extent to which this affects a person's interaction with a machine.
For me, the equivalent of such a device would be very useful at work, I just don't need so much touches, but I need some frames in an extra second.
- And increasing the number of cameras is not necessary?
- Three "cameras" are now used in the Kinect system, one of which is actually an infrared transmitter and the other - a receiver of a reflected signal.
And already the third camera - this is actually a common sensor of the visible zone.
It is not used to determine the depth of the object.
A larger number of cameras could potentially solve the problem...
Or make it worse, because it would increase the necessary volume of calculations.
It would be nice to create a flexible Kinect analog system, play with the curvature location of the cameras and see how this will help in determining the three-dimensional position.
- If I remember correctly, Microsoft (unlike Google) didn’t present its glasses publicly.
Don't you think that when it comes to the day-to-day use of augmented reality technology with special glasses, it's one of the most promising platforms?
- Of course, walking and constantly holding the smatphone in raised hands is not very comfortable, but I think: the strongest option would be the "transmissible" form of the expansion of reality, when it would be possible, on the basis of the clouds platform, it would be possible to switch at any time from glasses to a smartphone, a projection display and further around.
Glasses - this is a very personal device, in this is their strength (only you see private things), and at the same time weakness - augmented reality based on glasses will not allow you to work on virtual objects at the same time with other people.
- Imagine for a moment that manipulation of virtual holographic objects in the air is available not only to Tony Stark in "Iron Man", but also to an ordinary mortal.
There's one problem that critics often point to: there's no touch response!
His hands don't feel anything!
What answers does your group prepare for this challenge?
- In my lectures, I often say that augmented reality - this is the seventh consecutive breakthrough in the interaction between human and machine.
I think the addition of tactile perceptions could easily become the eighth breakthrough.
So far, one of the interesting tricks is used - the use of the other hand as a certain kind of substrate for imaging.
In an excellent way, it records compression!
But there are also technologies that are really aimed at adding palpability to these "images in the air" - for example, the interference of several directional ultrasonic rays at the defined point where the finger is located, evokes a tactile sensation, but it's still very weak - as if you were blowing on your tummy fingers.
There's also a wristbreast that affects the nerve endings in the fingers - also a promising direction.
- Have you tried to deceive your brain?
To make him think he's feeling what he seems to feel when he's seeing something?
It's a good idea, we haven't tried that yet.
There is yet another challenge that will be fought for for a long time - how to get a person who is physically in a very cramped space to believe that he walks through an open, practically endless space - we are also working on the concept of cross-country trails (not such as in clubs), moving platforms and also huge balls.
If deception of the brain becomes a target, then work is still for many years.
That's what makes working on virtual reality so appealing to researchers - many things are in their utter beginnings.
Taste instead of culture - Rosbalt.ru
"Rosbalt" is a continuation of the "St. Petersburg avant-garde" project, dedicated to citizens who are at the forefront, is an avant-garde of culture and art.
At the top of the list were already important figures of the art scene of St. Petersburg, whose achievements go beyond the city, often finding recognition in Europe, without achieving all-Russian fame.
"Rosbalt's new hero" is stunning artist Cyril Miller.
The whole city knows Cyril Miller - a bearded man who is all dressed in red, whom you meet here at the Russian Museum, here at the Summer Garden, here in fashionable pubs and on openings.
Cyril Miller's paintings, wherever they are exhibited, always attract crowds of spectators.
Cyril Miller is one of the purely social and philosophical St. Petersburg narrators, creators of new mythologies.
Cyril Miller - this is a striking man of the St. Petersburg avant-garde of the late 80's and early 90's.
In addition to being an urban man, his appearance on the streets evokes a smile on the face of the citizens, increasing everyone's mood.
He recently took a bottle and became a St. Petersburg fashier, because he matured into this complex role with his whole bohemian life, philosophy and image.
