Giving birth in space
The country is at risk.
A global warming or collision with a killer asteroid.
Caravans of cosmic ships with earthlings on dove are embarking on a journey, looking for a replacement planet.
In order to save humanity, the main question arises: how to keep your family under weightless conditions or on a given replacement planet?
I don't think the choice is great.
In fact, there are only two planets that can be acquired, if only hypothetically.
"It's Venus and Mars," says Vladimir Surdin, a high-ranking scientist at the State Astronomical Institute of P. K. Šternberg (SAISH).
However, while there are better conditions for life on Mars, temperatures reach 500 degrees Celsius on the surface of Venus.
It is only possible to live there at a high altitude or in the orbit of Venus... in space.
Resolving the issues of reproduction in the universe began scientists with flora.
Already half a century ago, experiments were carried out on plants.
Four generations of peas grown in orbit are no different from their earthly relatives.
It was then carried out of insects into orbit - a very small Drosophila fruit fly.
In 1979, a quail egg was sent into space to verify how an embryo develops in a weightless state.
We're getting an absolutely normal bird on the ascent.
But then the problems begin.
"The problem arises with the fact that this bird has to find support, it has to stand on its own feet and start moving," says Vladimir Sychev, head of the Laboratory of the Institute of Biomedical Problems (IBMP).
If they don't find support, the birds fall chaotically and make tumblers.
After 10 hours there was a complete extinction of instincts in newly hatched birds.
The birds did not respond to light or sound.
And the trouble was that within four days, they had simply perished.
"Twice we've hatched birds, but then we've rejected it because it's just impossible to work with them there," Vladimir Sychev said.
The last "mini-arch" with animals flew into orbit 16 years ago.
In the spring of 2013, the experiments will continue.
There will only be beings of one sex in the "Bion" biosatellite.
There was an experiment with rats, who were transmitted to space already gravid.
They didn't find anything supernatural.
"It was only on biosatellites, but again - this is a unique experiment, and such experiments need to be carried out," Vladimir Sychev says.
After landing on the ground, cosmic rats gave birth to offspring.
However, it has not yet been possible to approach the problem of reproduction directly in the universe.
It's a difficult task.
Animals simply can't realize their sexual instincts when they lose the usual environment.
Man, unlike animals, in principle can.
Homo sapiens rules abstract thinking and is able to create an appropriate emotional background.
For ethical reasons, similar experiments are not carried out.
But women have been flying to space for 50 years.
Tereshkova was in the greatest danger.
The most precious thing that humanity has is the woman's body.
Our Tchaika flew away, and no one on Earth could tell if the flight to space would last.
Whether he can give birth to a child after such a flight.
"No one's answered that question yet," Vachtang Vačnadze, a veteran of the rocket-spacing sector, says.
In June 1964, more than a year after flying into space, Valentina Tereshkova gave birth to her first female cosmonaut.
The child's father was also cosmonaut Andriyan Nikolayev.
In 1988, the son of Svetlana Savicka's second female cosmonaut was born, who visited orbit twice and who also worked in open space.
However, the risk remains.
We have only a few cosmonauts who, after many years, were born and growing healthy offspring.
"Moreover, it can be dangerous even in orbital flights," according to cosmonaut-pilot, Hero of the Soviet Union and Hero of Russian Valeriy Polyakov.
Nevertheless, humanity must inevitably look for new paths in the field of biotechnology, radiation protection and the formation of artificial gravity.
The CPK Hydroboratory is a mandatory stage of preparation for flight.
Here in zero-attraction conditions, cosmonauts acquire the habits necessary to work in an open universe.
Water mimicks the state of weightlessness.
Although for adults, water is a comfortable, but, in a way, a strange environment, for young children it is a natural element.
Small ichtians appear to confirm that life on earth comes from the ocean.
There is a connection between the fact that the baby is on average 9 months in the mother's amniotic fluid and that it is then easier for them to get used to water.
In principle, it is logical, because from the moment of birth, from the moment of that first bathing, only 2 weeks will pass.
"That's very little for the baby to forget something," says newborn swim instructor Marina Aksenova.
In short, if there is a more natural state for a newborn child, then for women, the inevitable gravity, an attractive force, is more natural.
In a state of weightlessness, there is very quickly withering the abdominal muscles, the muscles of the pelvis, the ability of the body to expel the fetus decreases.
We can leave this aside if muscle stimulators during childbirth will work.
After all, it is possible for childbirth to take place in a special chamber, in which the mother's body expels the fetus.
"And what next?" asks Valery Polyakov complex questions.
On the other hand, the baby also needs artificial gravity.
If there is no natural attractiveness on the body, it is not able to properly form the skeleton or the system of muscles.
It is not possible to dress the newborn in orbit as an adult in a special suit with a load in which he could train.
He'll just be deprived of what he urgently needs for his development.
"If we go with you into an experiment that admits the birth of a child in a foreign environment, we will bring a disabled person on Earth who will be completely unsuited to the local environment," Igor Pestov, head of the IBMP bioethics commission, predicts.
The birth of children in the universe today is just a theoretical question.
However, there will come a time when it will become a reality, and when Earthlings will embark in their ships on a long planet that will become the home of their descendants born in the universe.
SAO chief: Švarcsystem control fails due to politicians
In connection with public contracts, the Czech Republic has good controllers and high-quality legislation, but it is lagging behind in its application.
In an interview with Aktuálně.cz, Miloslav Kala, vice president of the Supreme Audit Office, said this.
"The law will never be perfect, but it has to be the right application - that's what's lacking in my opinion," Kala said.
Similar conclusions are also indicated by the joint control of Czech and German controllers.
As an example of malpractice, he cites Petr Nečas's approach to the so-called "švarcsystem".
Recently, the Prime Minister said that the ODS will not bother the businessman with his controls - is he forbidden or allowed?
"The law has to be set up somehow, and if it bans something, even the prime minister cannot prevent the activities of the office, which is obliged to go to check and enforce duties," Kala says.
At the meeting of the Audit Committee in the Chamber of Deputies, you talked about a joint project between the Czech and German parties, in which legislation on public procurement in both countries was compared.
What exactly was it about?
It's a parallel check that we started about two years ago.
In simple terms, European legislation regulates how to deal with public contracts, followed by individual national legislation and then by the practice itself.
We have put all of this together, and even though the control is not yet completed, we have shown very interesting differences - generally speaking, the legislation in our country is perhaps even "stricter and more perfect", however, the practice itself is better in some parameters in Germany.
It is therefore confirmed that it is not enough to create more and more precise rules, but it is necessary to deal with the application of the law itself.
Why can this project help you, what does it mean for you?
Such joint control could contribute to stopping efforts to refine laws, reduce and improve limits when it does not have such a positive impact.
Efficiency means getting an appropriate thing for a reasonable (which doesn’t always mean the lowest) price in order to exclude enrichment and possibly criminal conduct.
But by lowering the order limit, we don't necessarily have to achieve that.
Perhaps the system will be overwhelmed with so much "paper" that those who want to look for holes in this system will draw from it much more easily than if the limit remained higher.
You are talking about domestic problems with the transposition of public procurement legislation into practice.
How does the control system work in Germany?
Is there an office similar to our SAO, or is it set differently?
As far as the SAO is concerned, the Bundesrechnungshof, which is organized just like us, also has a collegy, it is formed in a slightly different way, but in principle both authorities proceed similarly.
The powers are similar to a certain extent, but by the fact that Germany has a federal arrangement, it also has these auditoriums at the level of individual Länders - in this way the system differs a little from ours.
BRH can only control federal money, represented in our country by state resources.
Public funds, which are managed by the regions and municipalities in our country, are checked there from the level of the Federal Court of Auditors.
When it comes to legislation, it’s simpler than ours?
In general, I would not like to compare it without specific data, however, in some respects Germany is referred to as a model, but it can certainly not be said that it is better in everything.
Is it because they have better controllers?
I certainly don't, but I'm saying again, I don't like such comparisons.
But it has to be said that even the countries that we consider a role model are facing a number of problems.
Otherwise it wouldn't be of any benefit for them to cooperate with our office, wouldn't it?
As for the domestic legislation, what the amendment to the Public Procurement Act has meant for your office, are its effects already manifested?
Due to the fact that the period of validity of this amendment is relatively short, it has not yet manifested itself in our control actions.
Because we do ex-post controls, there is a need to take into account certain delays.
So far, we observe this only in the process of preparing future inspections - we started our new tool "detecting the risks of inefficiency", through which we ran through almost 14 thousand public contracts, and we analyzed them - there, of course, those changes will manifest themselves, because the limits will change, the conditions of some types of the selection procedure will be adjusted, etc.
Do you consider the approval of this law rather as a benefit, or an additional burden on the bureaucracy system?
I think this law is certainly a step in the right direction, and I hope that will be confirmed.
The problem that may arise here will "tighten" the rules and it will not be possible to follow them.
Even with the functioning of the previous rules, the persons audited are obliged by the provider (for example, in the case of regional operational programmes by the Regional Council) that any non-compliance with the Public Procurement Act violates budgetary discipline.
Is it worth tightening the law in this way?
I don't think it's that way.
The system must exclude those who want to attack and abuse it, and not punish those who commit formal misconduct that has no impact on decision-making.
Such a system will only put more pressure on bureaucracy.
So how to get out of it?
Let's see where the road doesn't go.
Recently, the prime minister said that the ODS will not bother entrepreneurs by inspecting the so-called "švarcsystem" - and what does that mean?
Is Švarcsystem prohibited or allowed?
After all, the law has to be set up somehow, and if it bans something, even the prime minister cannot prevent the activities of the office, which is obliged to check and enforce duties.
He can say, "We're going to change the law and we're going to allow it," but he can't say we're going to act like nothing.
There are fairly strict rules in the Public Procurement Act as what is to be formally done - which is right to protect public tender.
But on the other hand, it is tragic when a bidder with the best offer is excluded only because of formal deficiencies.
The law will never be perfect, but its application must be correct - that's what's missing with us in my opinion.
The roads are sometimes frozen, but mostly passable
In several places in the Czech Republic, the roads are frosted and also snowy.
However, the roads are usually passable, somewhere with caution.
Carlsbad Region
In the Karlovy Vary region this morning the roads were passable, but in some places frozen and also snowy.
The temperature dropped between five and ten degrees below zero, but is supposed to warm slightly during the day.
Snowing in the region has ceased and there is only a weak layer in the lowlands.
However, there are about 30 inches of snow on the ridges of the Ore Mountains.
There was also lower visibility due to fog, according to data from the dispatching of regional road workers.
The R6 expressways and first-class roads are now passable in the region without restrictions.
Cautiousness, however, is appropriate, for example, on some bridges, where the surface can be frosted and slippery.
All second- and third-class roads, including mountain roads, are also passable.
However, there are still remnants of frozen and misguided snow on them in some sections.
Especially in higher positions it is necessary to pay close attention to driving.
Pardubice and Hradec Králové Region
On some roads in eastern Bohemia there is a risk of frosting, there may be a layer of snow on the roads in higher positions and in the mountains, the Directorate of Roads and Motorways informs on its website.
Road workers warn drivers of icing, it can form especially in the higher positions of the Pardubice region.
Ice can be in the vicinity of Lanškrouna, Ústí nad Orlicí, Polička, Svitavy or Vysoké Mýto, especially on the roads of the second and third classes.
Roads I/43 and I/34 near Svitavy are treated chemically.
Snow is mainly on the roads in the Giant Mountains and the Eagle Mountains.
The snow layer is in the highest positions on the roads in the districts of Rychnov nad Kněžnou and Trutnov.
In Eastern Bohemia today it is most often clear to semi-bright without precipitation.
Temperatures usually range between minus three and plus one degree Celsius, blowing a slight wind.
Pilsner Region
Roads in the Pilsen region are passable this morning, but in places with caution and the ride must be adapted to the weather.
It's frosty in the morning, temperatures range between three to nine degrees below freezing.
After the previous snowfall and subsequent cooling, some communications may be frozen.
Drivers must also expect fog, but visibility will gradually improve.
This is according to the information of the dispatching of county road workers.
The D5 motorway is passable almost unrestricteded, but caution is advised by road administrators between 80 and 131 kilometres.
Most first-class roads have a surface dry and frozen.
However, there may be frosty sections in the southern Pilsen and Tachov regions.
The second- and third-class communications are wet, and also in this case, warnings about frozen sections apply.
Drivers should be especially careful on less frequented roads in the Šumava region.
The Olomouc Region
Drivers heading to the highest positions of the Olomouc Region have to take into account for the slurry on the road today.
After the chemical sprinkle, it remained on the Červenohorské saddle and on its way in the direction of the Videl Cross.
The roadmen were driven into the field at night by snows, in the Šumperk region, according to road managers, snowed about three centimeters.
In other parts of the county, roadways are mostly passable without restrictions.
"In the Šumperk region there are remnants of snow in the highest positions.
Drivers have to reckon with snow porridge on the Červenohorské saddle in the direction of Jeseník," the Šumperk Road Administration dispatcher told ČTK today.
Their colleagues in the Jesenice region also went into the terrain at night, but the paths to the highest positions are now, according to them, bare and wet after chemical treatment.
In the Olomouc region, roads are unrestricted, but in Šternberk, drivers should be careful in the forest sections, where the roads remained wet.
Usti nad Labem Region, Liberec Region
A number of hard-to-drive places are reported by road workers in the north of Bohemia this morning.
In addition to several places with fallen snow or glaciers, the mountain road from Telnice to Knínice in the Ústí region is also closed, according to the police database.
Temperatures are held even in lower positions below zero, so both ice and snow are likely to last on the roads. In the lowlands, especially southeast of the Bohemian Central Mountains, on the contrary, there are no problems, and roads are usually dry.
Operational complications have not yet been reported.
The occurrence of glacies is reported by road workers, especially from around Štětí.
According to meteorologists, conditions were ideal for her at night - during the day rain and melting snows, at night clear and frost.
Unfavorable conditions are also reported from the main routes on the I/13 road on the border of the Ústecký and Liberec regions.
The closure from Telnice to Knínice was forced by benten branches of trees, which bent to the road below the deposit of snow.
Šimon Ornest: At concerts we want to accumulate positive energy
What do you think is the end of the world in less than a month?
It's just one of the other alarming messages that we humans like to catch.
Together with The Tap Tap, we make fun of it and say that we are the only band in the world that can gather enough positive energy to delay or prevent the end of the world.
Moreover, in December you will organize an absolutely unique project three concerts against the end of the world.
Can you introduce it to your readers?
It's a nationwide benefit we've been planning for two years.
We decided to take advantage of the marketing potential of the end of the Mayan calendar announced for December 21 at 11:10 a.m.
The day earlier on December 20 from 21 o'clock, 3 concerts will take place simultaneously in Prague, Brno and Ostrava.
It will end around the time the Mayan calendar will end on Kiribati Island in the Pacific, which is 12 hours ahead.
Who came up with this idea?
Originally it was probably my idea, then we invented everything together with our artist Honza Augusta.
In addition to the fact that we want to accumulate enough positive energy and prevent the end of the world, we also want to think together with the public about the state in which we will one day pass on this world to our children.
On the occasion of the end of the Mayan calendar, we have also created a unique collection of items of shoes, T-shirts, bags or original sprouts against the end of the world, whose purchase at www.e-tap.cz people can also support us.
Together with other performers, The Tap Tap also sang the so-called anthem against the end of the world, which is called "The End of the World Canceled."
It's already very popular on YouTube today, will it also be heard at these benefit concerts?
Of course, at the very end, that is, if everything succeeds and the end of the world does not...
All performers will sing it simultaneously at all three concerts.
Anthems will already connect in a unique live broadcast on Czech Television.
Tomáš Hanák writes the words about this song and plays the main role of Jesus in the clip, Xindl X...
How did you come to cooperate with them?
We also cooperate with other personalities of the Czech cultural scene, due to the fact that we organize a lot of benefit events and concerts...
We try to involve them intensively in these projects.
It turns out that most of them are interested and entertained by cooperation with us.
What will the proceeds from concerts against the end of the world go for?
On the equipment of a barrier-free educational center Studeo, which we have been implementing for the sixth year with the Tap civic association in the Jedlička Institute.
On behalf of the students of the Jedliček Institute schools, they regularly attend lecturers and devote themselves to them in activities that they enjoy and are interested in.
The students themselves do not have the means to go to the lecturers themselves, so we try to allow them in this way.
As part of the completion of Jedlička’s institute, a separate building will be built in the future, to which we will move with this project.
Several musical groups and performers will perform at each concert.
How did you choose them?
We tried to put together a program that will appeal to all generations, including children.
For example, Chinaski, Support Lesbiens, Illustratosphere with Dan Bárta, The Tap Tap, Marián Bango and Jiří Suchý will perform in Prague.
Full details can be found at www.kpks.cz.
Are you planning any similar "bombastic events" for the future?
In May we will perform for the first time at the Prague Spring, so we will definitely prepare an interesting program with interesting guests.
Next year we would also like to play in the Czech National House in New York, and I personally, when we are already in the USA, I would like to connect it with performances in Washington and Chicago.
Your foreign plans are not minimalist, so far you have performed, for example, in Madrid, Brussels, London or Moscow.
However, The Tap Tap is a group made up of handicapped people.
How do you manage these journeys logistically and organizationally?
It's not as bad as it seems at first glance.
We have five members on an electric wheelchair, its trolleys have to travel in the luggage compartment, and of course we carry a large number of loads and crates with tools...
However, so far it has always been done without any problems, ČSA and British Airways were excellently prepared for us, how many times I was surprised.
Even in Moscow, from which we returned recently, it was without problems.
Thanks to trips abroad, you can compare how individuals vary in barrier-freeness, public access to the handicapped and the like.
What have you encountered so far?
After Madrid, Luxembourg, London and other places where everything works better than in our country, we have just witnessed in Russia that in the east everything is still in the very beginning.
Compared to Prague, Moscow is very barrial, here it is still unusual for a person on an electric wheelchair to move himself in the city center.
It doesn’t even matter for such obviousness as giving wheelchair users priority in the elevator.
Fortunately, there are already civic associations that try to draw attention to the problems of people with disabilities.
And what, on the contrary, do we still have to catch up with the more developed countries?
There's a lot of things we're still behind...
However, it is important to mention that the improvement and improvement of the situation is always dependent on the efforts of the people involved.
It is very common in London and Madrid that even people with severe disabilities move independently in public, they can go to the toilet, to the museum, anywhere...
However, it is not very usual that a large number of people with disabilities actively participate in social life there, on the contrary, we are a little ahead with The Tap Tap!
Respecting the public or barrier-free is one thing, but only when we become important athletes, artists, actors, politicians or lawyers, will things really start to change.
So far, these are isolated instances of people who have a strong will.
The Band The Tap is currently very popular, but let's go back a few years ago what made you want to start it in 1998?
As an educator, I joined Jedlička’s Institute, where I had many young people around me who were interested in doing something.
And because I'm a musician myself, among other things, I'm playing saxophone, so together with my colleague I started an interest music club.
Well, over time, as our presenter Láďa Angelovič says, it got a little out of hand (laughs).
But the popularity came only in recent years, or am I wrong?
It's true that it helped us when we started connecting with well-known singers and also actively working to promote the band.
We've realized that a job that isn't seen basically doesn't exist as if it doesn't exist.
Thanks to contributions from the European Union, we can also afford high-quality lecturers, equipment and so on.
Was your goal to make it with The Tap Tap this high?
From the beginning, I felt the potential to do something differently.
Showbusiness is crowded with things where one imitates the other.
It's logical in a way, everything new is accepted carefully, and it takes a long time.
There are very few things that are unique, but I dare say that Tap Tap is one of them.
The first impression in a person who sees us for the first time is, of course, regret, that's natural...
But that regret is completely unnecessary, because people with disabilities are none suffering from all abandoned beings we have to regret.
They are people who can live a full life and develop themselves, provided, of course, that they have the necessary resources to do so.
I'm saying that if there's one thing for people with disabilities, it's not just for them, it's for the whole of society.
Did it also help this success that you're a strict boss, as many people have labeled you?
If we want to do quality work, we have to be uncompromising in many things and require some discipline.
But I think that's perfectly normal.
Some people come to us with a romantic idea and with their head in the clouds, and when they find that they have to go for a rehearsal twice a week, come to the product and spend a lot of time on the road to concerts, the enthusiasm quickly leaves them.
But that's how it works everywhere, in every group that wants to work and who wants to achieve something.
Currently, The Tap Tap has twenty members.
How many of them were at its beginnings in 1998?
Only one Lāďa Angeloviā.
We're an open group, people leave and come, it can't be prevented.
Those who have the will and the taste always have the door open with us
It will take place the day before the expected end of the world, on Thursday 20.12.2012 from 21 o'clock
It will take place in the Incheba Arena, Brno Fléda and Ostrava Plynojem with the participation of 12 bands and other musicians from the Czech Republic
At the end of the evening, all three towns will connect with telemost during the joint singing of The Tap Tap "End of the World Canceled"
The aim of the concert is to collect funds for the equipment of the multifunctional barrier-free educational center STUDEO at the Jedlička Institute in Prague in the amount of 25 million CZK
Admission to the concert is 400 Kč, children up to 12 years old have free tickets in the Bohemiaticket pre-sale
Poland and the cosmos.
Last week, at a meeting of the ministers of the countries of the European Space Agency, Poland was adopted as the 20th member of this European space agency as the second country from the countries of the former eastern bloc (after the Czech Republic, which became a full member of ESA on November 12, 2008).
Poland began close cooperation with ESA in 1994 and participated in a number of the agency's projects over the following years.
However, Poland's trip to space began much earlier.
Polish enthusiasts had been involved in flights to space before the Second World War; but they had not always encountered sympathy.
I will recall, for example, the lecture by A. Šternfeld at the Warsaw Astronomical Observatory, which on December 6, 1933 represented ideas from his groundbreaking work The Entrance to Space.
The ideas of a young engineer (born 1905) left the listener cold, and after years Šternfeld remembered that only Dr. Jan Gadomski was interested in his work.
In 1934, Šternfeld was awarded the Robert Esnault-Pelterie and André Louis Hirsch Prize for his entry into cosmonautics in France.
Remined Dr. Jan Gadomski (1899 - 1966) later became a great promoter of astronomy and cosmonautics.
He has published hundreds of articles in Polish journals and has written a number of books devoted to these scientific disciplines.
Gadomski became a world-renowned promoter of cosmonautics, and his contribution was also appreciated by the fact that one of the craters on the far side of the moon was named by his name.
As early as 1925, a rail drawer was built in Poland, which was supposed to be equipped with a rocket engine.
Unfortunately, the author of the proposal is not known and the details of the project are not known.
Nor is it clear whether the rocket should have been used to start the drezina or to brake it.
The information about this dressage is known only from the contemporary press.
Since 1933, Polish artillery has beginned to deal with rocket missiles.
The research was provided by Komórka Techniki Uzbrojenia in cooperation with Prof. Mieczyslaw Wolfek and Prof. Gustaw Mokrzyckiegi.
It is clear from the preserved materials that the research has reached the stage of practical tests.
However, with the arrival of German troops, the research was interrupted.
In 1937, the design of a photo-electrical fighter missile, designed by Rohoziński, appeared in the professional press, and in the following year the work of Rakiet - torpeda powietrzna and rakietobomba lotnicza author Leliwa-Krzywoblocké was published.
Both projects were intended for the military use of rocket engines.
Just before the war, the Temporary Scientific Advisory Committee (Tymczasowy Komitet Doradczo-Naukowy), which coordinated all the works, was dedicated to all projects of military use of missile technology.
The committee was founded in 1937, but after a two-year-old activity its activities ceased the start of war.
Other works devoted to cosmonautics are appearing after the war in the Polish press thanks to the work of the Polskie Towarzystwo Astronautyczne.
For the first time, there is a mention of the company in the November issue of the 1954 magazine Problemy, in which there are four more comprehensive articles devoted to astronautics.
In one of them, from Prof. M. Subotowicz, it is proposed the creation of a company that will deal with spacecraft.
At that time, projects of artificial satellites were already unfolding and it was clear that space research was a promising field.
From the beginning of 1956, the Polish Astronautic Society (PTA) tried to join the International Astronautical Federation (founded in 1951) and already in the autumn PTA became a full member.
And in the following year, the first chairman of PTA Kazimierz Zarankiewicz (1902 - 1959) became vice-chairman of the International Astronautic Federation.
He held this position until his death in 1959.
Since 1956, PTA has made a significant contribution to the development of a very successful meteorological RM (Rakieta Meteorologiczna), which became Poland's first-team missile to conduct scientific research.
The first type of RM-1 was completed in 1957 and took its first launch on October 10, 1958.
The 1800-metre rocket was about 80 cm long and its weight was less than 5 kg.
An improved version of the RM-1A was built later, and in the summer of 1959, flight tests of a two-stage RM-2 rocket were launched in the Bled Desert.
The racket was 1.4 meters long and its weight was about 11.5 kg.
Another development type should have already been capable of actual scientific work - the RM-34 rocket was supposed to have an range of up to 14.5 km, and its task was to monitor the high-rise wind.
However, further developments were suspended in 1962.
The succession of the first RM missiles was the Meteor-1 rocket developed between 1962 and 1965.
The racket was designed as a two-stage rocket, its total length was 510 cm and the starting weight was 32.5 kg.
Three types have been developed (marking Meteor-1A, -1B and -1C), which differed in the size of the space for scientific instruments.
In the Meteor-1A rocket there was space of 0.4 liters, Meteor-1B with a volume of 0.34 liters and Meteor-1C with a volume of 0.62 liters.
The maximum range for all three types was 37 km.
Between 1965 and 1968, the Meteor-2 rocket was developed at the Aviation Institute, the first flight tests of which began in October 1970.
The Meteor-2 rocket had a starting weight of 380 kg and was able to carry a payload weighing 10 kg to a height of around 60 km.
Meteor-2H and Meteor-3 were built.
It is still necessary to mention the entry of Poland into COSPAR (Committee for Space Research) in 1960 and the creation of the national committee COSPAR two years later.
Poland was also involved in space research on Soviet artificial satellites as part of the Interkosmos programme, and in 1978 the Polish pilot Miroslaw Hermaszewski became the second intercosmonaut after Vladimír Remek.
Repealing the Public Works Act is not an option.
In the past week, the Constitutional Court has repealed the Public Works Act.
The resolution sparked a living debate in public.
It will certainly be interesting to look at the issue from a broader point of view.
Liberally oriented economic systems in the EU, as well as in the globalized world, are based on the principle of more, unregulated competition.
In its effect, this means that individual economic departments, national economic systems, stand in permanent conflict with each other.
The cause is the principle of free trade and the free, unrestricted movement of private capital, together with unbridled financial speculation.
Due to significant differences in the price of labour, (wages) there is a price tension.
It is necessary to understand under this term when the producer seeks, by importing cheap goods, in competition by "undermining" the price of a competitor in order to deduct a greater market share and thereby increasing its own profits.
On a mass scale, this means, for most entrepreneurs, either translating production abroad, or buying cheaply there.The result is high unemployment in countries where labor prices are high compared to other economies.
Since private capital has no social responsibility, and thus responsibility for the unemployment generated by it, the state’s social expenditure necessarily grows.
The whole situation is enhanced by the complete unwillingness of the masters of entrepreneurs to pay taxes and thus to compensate for the economic and social society-wide damage caused in the pursuit of profit.
The situation is so well known that there is no need to provide specific statistics.
Unscrupulous practices of private capital create a situation in individual economies where governments of individual countries are forced to enter mutual competition in order to artificially reduce the social level of one’s own population in order to attract foreign capital to the country.
In other words, governments give private capital their own population, regardless of social decline.
This is mainly reflected in the changes in current laws.
The aim is to economically compel the own population to accept the price dictation of private capital, especially in terms of wages.
This is done both by a system of economic coercion in the case of long-term unemployment and by limiting the rights of employees in the field of labour law.
The result is increasing poverty, along with an increase in disparities between the poor and the rich.
There have been public dispensaries in Germany for many years for the poor, who are unable to support themselves with their own work.
The number of these people is already going into the
In order to increase the competitiveness of the German economy, there are quite commonly cases that properly employed people receive such wages that the state has to repay them to an existential minimum.
Such a scandal has also been published in the case of auxiliary employees of the Bundestag
The austerity measures of virtually all southern EU states undoubtedly lead to the same situation when, under the pressure of a disastrous slump in the social level, people are forced to either emigrate, as was the case in the 19th century, or to live for hungry wages on the margins of society, in the hope that private investment capital might come to the country.
But here it is necessary to ask where it will come from?
If it comes from other EU countries, then poverty will move from one country to another, or it won't come at all, because Chinese, Indian, Brazilian, or Turkish, Moroccan, or Egyptian, or African are still still working for a fraction of Europe's wages.
This applies to all of Latin America.
Liberal theories and the media say that the state cannot participate capitalically in its own economy, that a controlled economy leads to an economic disaster.
Private capital insists that the state must not interfere with the economy through management interventions.
Then it is necessary to ask whether private capital does not influence, now it actually governs politics and thus the whole state in its own egoistic sense.
Here's the unequivocal answer, yes.
The proof is the existence of a virtually almighty and ubiquitous lobby in all states.
The result is a diligent situation manifested in the area of corruption, the provision of mutual advantages, legislation, where practically everything is reprehensible, but nothing criminal.
In Germany, the situation is such that state ministries, for lack of funds, arrange drafting laws to private legal firms, which are heavily connected with the industry.
These bills are then passed in the Bundestag.
In this way, all power does not come from the people as proclaimed by Western-type constitutions, but from financially strong groups that prefer their own interests.
It is clear that liberal-oriented democracies are now rapidly getting into a situation, as Appiános describes it in his work The Crisis of the Roman Republic in the times of Caesar and Pompey: "The state has long been in complete disarray, and the authorities have been occupied by violence.
For bribes, unlawful acquisition of favor and with the help of stones or swords.
Briberation and corruption went without any inhibitions, and the people went to the polls with a pre-sold voice" . ..."characterous people did not apply for office at all, so once as a result of this disruption the state was eight months without consuls". . ."Indeed, it began to say that the only remedy in a bad situation was self-government and that it was necessary to choose an energetic man.
Conclusion As in antiquity, contemporary society is based on an unscrupulous preference for one's own benefit, regardless of society-wide interest.
Private capital is no longer inherently able to understand and exercise society-wide benefit.
The result is now, as well, an unparalleled decadence of elites without any pursuit of deeper-depth reform.
Therefore, we must seek the causality of the emergence of fascist and communist regimes in the reckless liberalization of the economic systems of the 19th and 20th centuries.
In the current situation, the demise of these systems in favour of liberal-type democracy can be regarded as a certain break with the expectation of the next round.
Particularly disastrous is the fact that current elites completely ignore in the future the possible hundreds of thousands of loss of lives, humanitarian and social disasters, which we are already witnessing, along with crimes against humanity, as we know them from old and new history.
Repealing the Public Works Act is not a solution, at least not permanent.
Under the pressure of competition at both the international and internal European levels, the government of the Czech Republic will be forced to continue to look for ways to further reduce the social level of the population.
Therefore, this trend is systemic.
The solution is targeted political-social reforms, which will strengthen the capital participation of the state in the economy, strengthen the influence of citizens on the government and weaken the monopoly position of private capital in society for the benefit of the state.
Israel: Laboratory of Chaos.
"Nothing comes out of the violence, and it can never," Sting in Fragile's song whose main verse in the refrain is "let's not forget how frailed we are"
"If my sons didn't want wars, there wouldn't be any" dying Gutele Schnaper, wife of Mayer Amschel Rothschil in 1849
After the latest wave of violence between Israel and the Gaza Strip, as always, a lot of reactions swarmed.
One stands behind Israel, arguing his right to self-defense, and the Palestinians fitting into the role of terrorists, others supporting Palestinians, arguing the racism of an Israeli state, genocide perpetrated against Palestinian Arabs, and the state of Israel fitting into the role of a terrorist state.
I don’t want to think about who is the culprit in the periodically recurring waves of killing and the victims, after all, today’s inhabitants of Israel, including self-governing territories, were born into the current political situation and did not experience the beginning of the violence.
My point is to offer readers a view behind the curtain, a look at who is primarily served by the 95-year-long tension (counted since the Balfour Declaration in November 1917) on a small piece of Middle Eastern country.
Some of my considerations are supported by available historical facts some are derived from them based on my own understanding of who, respectively, which group of people is the main driver of the events of modern history.
The history of mankind is, first and foremost, the history of struggle for power.
In every epoch, we can look for an Alexander the Greater or Napoleon.
What's not so obvious is whether it was always these personalities who guided their steps completely autonomously, or whether there was someone behind their throne who directed their actions to a predetermined goal.
