Argo movie: When it's at its worst, call Hollywood
In November 1979, a mob demonstrating Islamist students occupied the U.S. Embassy in Tehran, capturing 52 diplomats hostaged.
They were dismissed in exchange for the deposed Shah Mohamed R�eza Páhlavi, who fled to the U.S. after the revolution, which had been the patron of his regime for several decades.
For the U.S. administration, the situation did not offer a positive way out - she couldn't affort throwing the Shah overboard because it would fundamentally jeopardise confidence in allies in other countries.
The release of hostages, however, in Iran, where the revolution resulted in the establishment of a theocratic regime, was incapable of achieving.
It was a blow to the prestige of the United States, which was later exacerbated by the fiasco of an attempt to liberate hostages by force.
Imprisoned diplomats were eventually dismissed after 444 days, after negotiations were mediated by the Algerian government.
Their lot at home triggered a wave of solidarity as well as anti-Iran sentiment.
The debacle in Iran contributed significantly to Jimmy Carter's loss to Ronald Reagan in the 1980 presidential election.
Director Ben Affleck's film Argo describes an episode of that history that brought America a minor victory.
Just before the embassy was occupied, six employees escaped from it.
After complex peripates, they ended up in the residence of Canada's ambassador.
The CIA, in cooperation with Canadian authorities, managed to get them out of Iran, helped by an extravagant cover story - they went to Canadian passports as members of the film crew, which in the country surveyed the locations for shooting a sci-fi big-film.
Combination of genres
Tony Mendez's "exfiltration" expert also demanded Hollywood's collaboration.
In order for the legend to be plausible, it was written about a film project in specialized magazines, press conferences were held, a fictional production company had a real office.
The details of the operation have been kept secret for a long time, the film is based on Tony Mendez's memories.
Affleck's film is an odd combination of multiple genres.
On the one hand, a realistically conceived political thriller, at the same time the so-called "caper movie", the story of a successful piece, a betrayal - an example could be a series of Danny's partners.
In the movie, moods alternate - on the one hand, documentary sequences from Tehran (in the subtitle sequence are shown iconic period photographs of events and the way in which the same situations were presented in the film - there are no big differences).
On the other hand, lightweight passages from Hollywood, treated with irony and slight exaggeration.
And then scenes from the headquarters of the CIA and other institutions - the men in suits discuss the situation at the negotiating tables, in the corridors of the office, on the phone...
Ben Affleck managed to reboot his career in a remarkable way.
The mocked-up actor became a respected director, and even his acting ceased to be the target of ironic remarks.
Argo is his third feature-length film, after the dark crime Gone Baby Gone (2007) and the thriller City (2010).
At the same time, it's Affleck's first film, which doesn't take place in the director's home Boston.
The feeling for the atmosphere of the place was one of the characteristics that elevated the director's older films above the Hollywood standard.
And Affleck also shows it in Argo, where Tehran "plays" Canada.
The best scenes in the film take place in the streets, in the reconstruction of real events - the opening sequence of the fall of the embassy is impressive in its clarity, which at the same time conveys the emotions of confusion and unpredictability, which occur when history suddenly moves.
Affleck and his collaborators achieve a similar effect in the scenes of fiction (fake staff at the Tehran bazaar).
Too many events in too many places
The director had to deal with the fact that the told story does not actually offer much in the film sense of thrilling scenes.
Quite successfully works with it, he sometimes thinks something about reality - everything doesn't get along so gracefully anymore (the scene in which the impending crisis at Tehran's airport averts a phone call in America, and the chase on the runway looks pretty far-fetched).
The weakness of Arga, however, is distraction resulting from the need to show too many events in too many places.
Alan Arkin and John Goodman are very claramous in the roles of Hollywood collaborators, but their characters deserve more space and are far from the only ones in the film.
Affleck's film somewhat loses its dramatic turn, it's a film that can be watched with slight interest, to appreciate the realization and retro style evoking thrillers of the 70's.
But it's hard to pull.
As a reminder of one living history in a certain way to date, and a testament to how exaggerated the notions of all-powerful and omniscient secret services are, but it is sufficient.
Rules for inflating balloons, bananas and circus
The server www.bankovnipoplatky.com, which annually announces a survey on the most absurd bank fee, has now decided to announce a competition for "the most absurd regulation or ideas from the EU".
"We were led to this by the last event when the EU plans to adopt a 40 percent quota for women in the leadership of major European companies," Patrik Nacher, the organizer of the poll, told Právo.
Among the nominated absurdities is, for example, a recent ruling by the European Court on unifyinging insurance rates for men and women.
So far, women have been advantageous in life insurance prices because they are objectively less risky for insurance companies.
"Other incredible ideas from the EU can be nominated by the end of this year.
The vote itself will take place by the end of February 2013," Nacher informed.
The controversial EU regulation also includes the mandatory incorporation of biocomponents into fuels, which, as a result, is harmful to nature, the ban on the use of reliable mercury thermometers simply because they contain a relatively small amount of a dangerous substance, or a directive on the size of chicken cages, due to which it has significantly priced eggs this year.
He has previously faced criticism of a de facto ban on the use of the term spread butter or decommissioning classic light bulbs from sale.
High-quality bananas should have 14 centimeters
The EU apparatus often decides under the pressure of one or another commercial or industrial lobby, whose demands are usually defended in Brussels by a state or a group of states (so the Czech Republic now enforces the demands of its banks).
Lobby's interests were also due to the fact that, for example, high-quality bananas had to reach a length of at least 14 cm in the Union and were not allowed to show "anomalous curvature".
At the same time, the European Commission defended itself by merely harmonising existing inconsistent national norms that have complicated trading.
In spite of opposition from some countries, the EU has already softened fruit and vegetable standards, pointing out that existing directives lead to food waste.
Last year’s EU regulation, according to which it must be mandatory for inflatable balloons sold, according to children under the age of €8, are not allowed to inflate unattended adults.
The EU has appealed for U.S. research, according to which balloons between toys are the leading causation of children's suffocation deaths.
Similar restrictions now apply to children under the age of 14 when blowing into pilgrimages.
Curious ideas are born at home too
Relatively absurd is also the rule concerning European officials themselves - anyone who has an official function in the EU cannot use the name Macedonia with regard to the sensitivity of the topic for Greece and must talk about FYROM (Former Yugoslav Republic of Macedonia) instead.
In cooperation with the Association of Liberal Economists Laissez Faire, Bankovnípoplatky.com has nominated, in addition to some of the above-mentioned absurdities, the EU regulation on the volume of food stocks on the territory of a Member State.
The EU has set the maximum volumes of food stocks that may have been located on the territory of the Czech Republic on the day of our accession to the Union.
The Czech Republic then exceeded, for example, the specified volumes for preserved mushrooms, for which he was threatened with a high fine.
The organizers of the survey were also impressed by the idea of paying some countries for not having the sea, and the idea of granting subsidies to apply for a subsidy.
However, these ideas did not arise in Brussels, but in Prague.
"We're handicapped that we don't have the sea.
We will ask the European Union for a refund," Jaroslav Palas (ČSSD), Minister of Agriculture (ČSSD), said in autumn 2004.
As an argument, he stated that a lot of grain was born in our country and, as a result of so-called intervention purchases, the state was supposed to stand full of warehouses, which he had to export.
According to Palas, the EU was supposed to pay us hundreds of millions of euros for the fact that the Czech Republic has continued to ports.
In the end, the European Commission met the Czech Republic by organising a tender for the purchase of grain from countries that do not have access to the sea.
The subsidy for obtaining the subsidy was offered to municipalities by the Ministry of Regional Development managed by Minister Pavel Němec (US-DEU), in particular they were supposed to apply for subsidies from Brussels.
EU: Bizarre regulations are an exception
While regulations in Member States are easily criticised, efforts by the EU for regulation, and at the same time the functioning and development of the entire Union deserve recognition, according to a number of experts.
According to experts, a more serious problem is, for example, the drawing of EU subsidies for projects, which have nothing to do with the deepening of European integration, but which have been enforced by Member States in budgetary negotiations.
Emotions among Czechs are also aroused by the fact that, like other countries of the Union, the Czech Republic in Brussels must fight for the right to a specific designation of its traditional products, while not always succeeding in this struggle.
After a six-year fight with the Germans and Austrians, the Czechs defended the designation of Olomouc cheeses, but domestic rum, whose tradition dates back to the 19th century, had to be renamed by our producers.
The naming of rum can only carry products from cane distillate, and not from sugar beet.
In the official list of registered EU products in the company of world-renowed feta and gorgonzola cheeses, marzipan from Lübeck in Germany or Parma hams have already found, for example, Carlsband crackers, Pohořelice and Třeboň carp and Žatecký hops.
The EU trade mark can also boast Pardubice gingerbread and Hořice tubes.
People want me to save a republic, but I'm an amateur, Okamura says
Mr. Senator, how do you think he wants to run for president?
It doesn't matter to be a senator or a president
If it worked without problems in our country, I wouldn't run anywhere.
But I can no longer see how the last twenty years of the country has been stealing, how thieves are running around here, people's taxes and the retirement age are increasing.
I didn't have the ambition of being a politician.
But when I see something I don't like, I try to come up with a solution to change it.
And since I'm forty and I'm an independent non-partisan, I don't have any other way to influence things other than running for senator or president.
You made it to the Senate, but soon you went to the Castle.
Aren't you turning your back on the people who elected you?
I've been saying all along that I'm going to fight for the Castle according to the outcome of the Senate elections.
I later made it clear that if I was elected senator, I would run for president.
However, my goal is not a function, a function is a tool how I can realize my visions.
That's why I need the greatest possible influence and the strongest possible mandate.
But the problem is not only that as a nation we scold in a pub and at television, but that we trample on anyone who wants to try for change.
This will be joined by the media that dishonests a person and who confuse freedom of speech with freedom to lie.
For example, it was said that I bribe journalists or that I was an adviser to Jiří Paroubek.
Let's get to your visions.
In the fight for the Castle, you set off with the thesis of material and criminal responsibility of politicians and retroactive property declarations over twenty million.
To do this, you have to change the law.
However, as president, you do not have such authority, and only the Senate as a whole can propose laws.
How are you going to solve this?
When, as a citizen, I lobbied for a free trade, I managed to enforce it.
The problem is politics - when someone comes up with a good suggestion from the left or the right, he deliberately rejects himself, and that delays citizens.
As an independent non-partisan, I have much greater potential to get support from all parliamentary parties.
My advantage is that without partisan labeling and dogma, I can take something that's best for our country, from every side and apply it.
Do you consider yourself to be a right-wing or left-wing person?
From the Czech point of view, it seems to me that people rank me rather to the left.
It doesn't matter to me at all whether a little to the left or a little to the right.
It's important for me to go ahead.
It doesn't matter to me whether someone belongs to the left or the right, but I want to bring people together.
I will always support any publicly good solution, even if it is proposed by the KSČM or ODS, as well as against a bad idea.
You're excited when someone says you're a populist.
But aren't you proving what you said?
When you’re creating a business plan in a company, you also have an ideal goal and vision.
And you're trying to get closer to that.
Someone may call it populism, but all the suggestions I'm talking about are already working in the world, or significant experts have talked about.
But without the support of parliament, you'll only have passwords.
And you can’t do that in politics in the long term.
Or do you think that if you go to people and talk to them, that you will be able to enforce, for example, criminal and material liability?
There's no other option.
Convince politicians, journalists and the public, and try to get them on our side to enforce it.
If elected president, there is no problem holding a televised live broadcast, where I will ask parliamentary party presidents to enact substantive and criminal responsibility for politicians, officials, judges and prosecutors.
And they'd have to say why they don't want it.
If there is a strong personality to name mischievours, it is possible to put pressure on the political scene.
Taking the direct election of the president, that was a merit of public pressure.
To be honest, I'm an amateur, that I'm neither a genius nor an intellectual.
Because of my opinions and visions, I'm trying to find allies.
In politics, I've only just started and I'm going to try to get the majority support for my program.
I'm trying to push things myself if I can't, I'll quit in six years and go back to the private sector.
It sounds a bit like Okamura should save the Czech Republic.
I'm no savior.
I know there's nothing I'm going to don't do in one person, that's why I've bypassed a number of well-known people whether they wouldn't run for the Senate either.
I went to Radim Jančura, who refused because of the amount of work.
So at least I supported the investigative journalist Jana Lorencová, who uncovered scams with light heating oils.
I've run with people being really dissatisfied, but now I'm at odds.
Sixty percent of people didn't go to the polls, and those who went have mostly elected establishment leaders.
We're only two independents in the Senate.
People have elected the Senate in such a way that it will be difficult to enforce changes.
Nevertheless, I will fight for my visions, such as the direct election of mayors or governors.
Are you considering setting up your own party?
I haven't thought about that yet, because I don't have time to examine the pure past of every potential party man, and I don't even have the money for it.
I don't even have money for the presidential campaign, there's only 20 grand in my transparent account.
You don't have money?
You're talking about property returns, what's yours?
I'm estimated at about ₣60million for private property.
In Prague I have a plot for about 25 million, an apartment for ten million, another apartment for eight million, a collection of works of art for about ten million, Aston Martin for 3.5 million and Škoda Superb for a million, and then I have a few million in my account.
By the way I have Aston Martin because it was my boyish dream - I always liked James Bond, who rode in the car, was gallant to women and at the same time struggled against wrongs and mischief.
Do you drive Aston Martin, do you have a fortune of £60million and you don't have campaign money?
You're saying you want to change the republic, but you don't want to put your own money into it.
It's not very convincing.
I don't have £15 million to campaign.
I'm supposed to be in debt?
I've put 2.5 million into the campaign.
The fact that I don't have any sponsors testifies that I don't think there's so much interest in my program.
And I'm not obligated to pay the campaign
The cost of campaigning will basically cover what I'm going to get a senatorial salary.
But I wouldn't use it, for example, I wouldn't be able to pay my son an English school that costs 30 grand a month.
If I just wanted to make money, I wouldn't run anywhere.
So you're going to keep doing business so you can live?
Didn't you say you'd dampen these activities?
But that has nothing to do with the amount of salary.
As promised, I partially suppressed my activities.
For example, my deputy will become a travel director in the spring.
People would like me to be a Samaritan who will save a republic.
But I need to live from something, too.
As an entrepreneur, how much were you used to taking per month?
Two hundred to 400 thousand, which I'll take.
And if I was president, I'd end my business.
The full interview can be found in Saturday's edition of Law.
MVRDV's architects are proof that real adventures don't just take place in their heads - for example Spijkenisse and the recently built Mountain of Books - 2 images
"The building looks cheerful to me, it looks futuristic. Finally, there's something interesting to see again," says Lisette Verhaig, who walks around the edge of the road.
Stefan Spermon, an IT technician in a near-to-be-sized establishment, says, "Of course, the house is nice."
However, I wonder why we still need a library today.
They all have internet, iPads and e-books.
None of these old-style libraries are volunteered today, don't they?
Spijkenisse, a housing estate in close proximity to Rotterdam without its own job opportunities, which is not even worth a visit, holds a special record.
This municipality, with a population of 80 000, has the lowest level of education throughout the Netherlands.
In order to solve this stupid situation, several years ago the decision was made to contribute to general education and to build seven nicely painted reinforced concrete miniatures of fictitious bridges depicted on euro banknotes.
However, the success of the educational offensive was not dizzying.
And so city councillors recognized that there was only only one way to become masters of statistics: we must have a library!
Winy Maas of Rotterdam's MVRDV architectural studio, a master of insanely daring bar charts, and a contractor of funny, often even cynical buildings, accepted a thing with the usual calm, and at a competition meeting in 2003 he appeared with five books under his arm and a grin on his face.
And as the commission looked around in amazement and shrugged its shoulders, the insolent Maas piled its gifts upon itself according to its greatness, ending its activated speech with the words "Dear town village!"
So this is my suggestion for the Mountain of Books in Spijkeniss, the so-called Boekenberg!
Nine years later, the mountain, for 30 million euros, was piled.
It is part of the revitalization project, which also includes an underground car park, a supermarket, a post office and several adjoining residential and terraced houses with a total of 50 apartments.
In early November, the Mountain of Books was awarded the second place "Best Library of NL 2012."
In addition, the project is nominated for the 2012 Dutch National Wood Award.
Small-town grey, which has not yet been able to offer more than a post-modern pedestrian zone and an unusually ugly town hall, behind whose white façade you would rather expect a dairy, makes it richer by a bit of contemporary architecture.
Above all, however, Spijkenisse now has the first public cultural structure in the history of its existence.
The long road to the book
The first impression: a reader's eldorado under the cheese hatch.
There is an elevator through the measure of the mountain, but you will discover real spatial and reading passions only when hiking the topography.
From all sides the glazed interior is bright and clear, the floor made of burnt box and elegant street lanterns clearly express the atmosphere of the public square.
The urban environment is perfect.
You see park benches, dogs and boys and girls playing football.
And books, books, books everywhere.
"Normally standing shelves with books along the façade, and in the middle there's a large dark space that's mostly incoherent and impersonal," says Winy Maas.
We built the classical spatial configuration upside down, and the space for the reader was ejected from the inside out.
The interior of the Mountain of Books is skillfully used: on Wednesday there are offices, internet library, chess club, ecological center and headquarters of house technology.
A special delight is the black shelves for books, which are once the cladding of the walls, one sill and other times the railing of the stairs.
Appearance, structure and odour is foreign.
Even sworn architects and civil engineers shake their heads over unknown building materials.
"We wanted to work here with recycled materials," says Joop Trouborst, head of the project at the municipality of Spijkenisse, when asked by Standard magazine.
And so, in a Frisian farm one day, we came across a suitable waste product from agriculture.
For many years it has been used in greenhouses and fields in the Netherlands as a substrate under the roots of a millimetre fleece of synthetic matter.
It is cheap and time-efficient.
Thin textiles last one, two seasons and then are ripe to be dumped in bulky waste.
For the library, the fleece - for the first time in such quantities - was pressed onto plates with a thickness of four centimeters.
By the action of heat and pressure, the so-called Landbouwplastic (KLP) turns into a dark, homogeneous and burdensome material that smells a bit like a new car and a bit like sports shoes.
After 105 steps you can get to the top.
At the end of a nearly 500-metre journey, you’ll be rewarded in a literary café not only with a fantastic view of the city, but also with Dutch croquettes and ficuses in pots.
They take care of the atmosphere, but mainly about the correct humidity of the air in the mountains of literature.
Donations for a New Soul
"You probably wouldn't believe it, but this house, despite its many glass surfaces, is a sample project in terms of ecology," Trouborst says.
It heats up and cools the earth's heat.
Although the Mountain of Books stands under a glass hatch, the sun shines only briefly, even on sunny days.
Wide glued wooden trusses, which stand perpendicular to the glazed facade, serve as shadings and capture a large part of sunlight.
The climate in the room is very pleasant.
The rest can be handled by fully automatic blinds.
Stefan Spermon, an initial IT business skeptic, has already visited the new library.
Lisette Verhaig has already been there.
And also traditional Chinese medicine teacher Cynthia Bogarde, whom Boekenberg even refers to as "the long-awaited soul" of Spijkenisse.
The reason: every resident of the city was invited a few weeks again to open to donate one book from their personal fund.
In this way, optical gaps were to be filled in the previously not fully equipped library, which currently consists of 70 000 pieces.
The concept has proven itself.
Shelves are completely packed.
"There's nothing worse than a half-empty library," says architect Winy Maas.
"I think, thanks to our invitation, every inhabitant now has a relationship with the new house.
Everyone knows that his book is part of this building.
Even if it's just for decoration.
In doing so, MVRDV succeeded in the royal discipline, which in professional jargon is called identity formation.
Spijkenisse wrote the history of literature.
However young and uneducated he may be.
That's ultimately the starting point for identity.
Szabo: "Germans have to play a bigger role"
Germany abstained in deciding on the adoption of Palestine.
According to U.S.-Periot relations expert Stephen Szabo, Berlin is on the narrow edge of diplomacy.
Deutsche Welle: Germany signaled earlier earlier this week that it would vote against a proposal for Palestinian observer status in the United Nations.
Then Berlin abstained.
Why?
Stephen Szabo: Germany doesn't support what the Israelis were doing in Gaza.
However, Germany must now be cautious because of its special relationship with Israel.
But at the same time, I don't think he's going to support the U.S
Germany wanted to demonstrate its independence - without, of course, being too critical of Israel.
During the uprising in Libya in March 2011, Germany also abstained when it came to establishing a no-fly zone.
In the end, it was enforced by NATO.
Is it difficult for Germany to take a clear position on serious international issues?
Yes, that's the case.
The reason for this is that it is changing its foreign policy, in the direction of a policy that has been, so-called, driven by the U.S., towards Germany’s foreign policy.
The situation is all the more difficult because Europeans do not have a coherent and unified policy.
The Germans are between two fronts.
They are expected to play a more independent role, but they are not used to it.
I think they're just getting settled into their role, but they're on their way to a more "normal" foreign policy.
To the foreign policy they have in France or the United Kingdom.
What does "normal" foreign policy mean from a German point of view?
It shows readiness in the case of national interests and attitudes that are independent of the attitudes of the USA or European partners.
I think that German foreign policy is motivated by economic policy, i.e. exports and its relations with certain areas, such as Russia, China or the Middle East.
The economic interests of Germany are, to some extent, other than the economic interests of other superpowers, and therefore Germany must protect its interests.
Did economic interests have had an impact on attitudes in the Middle Eastern Conflict and voting at the United Nations?
Firstly, Germany has major outlets in the Middle East, especially in countries around the Persian Gulf.
Therefore, it must be careful not to deter the public, but also the elites in Arab countries.
It plays a role anyway.
But I wouldn't want to attach too much importance to it, it's not exactly a one-sided relationship.
However, it plays an important role in German reasoning.
Did Germany's relations with the U.S. hurt when Germany abstained in the case of both important decision-makes, for example, in the vote on Palestine?
I think there is a great understanding of the German attitude in Europe and even in the USA.
That's why I don't think it was such a dramatic turning point as on the Libya issue.
Perhaps Germany will even gain some respect for it.
This signals that it must be considered as an international player and its interests must be taken into account.
There are different views on the Palestinian initiative in Europe.
The U.S., on the other hand, was clearly in favour of a veto
Are there differences of opinion between the U.S. and many European nations?
These differences have always existed as a result of U.S. internal politics.
I think the Obama's government probably secretly has a considerable understanding of Europe's stance.
But as a result of the political situation there, he cannot, of course, take this stance publicly.
I don't think the differences in opinions at all really are as big as they always seem.
If you look at relations between Obama and Prime Minister Netanyahu, Obama isn't too keen on Netanyahu's policies at all.
Isn’t it difficult for Germany to reconcile its close relations with Israel and the US, on the one hand, and with the position of the most important partners in the EU, on the other?
I think that's exactly what makes it difficult for the Germans.
Of course, it would be a little easier for Germans if there was a coherent and unified European policy, which is not happening at the moment.
Therefore, they cannot be part of a wider line and, instead, they have to drive things forward from their own position.
That's what they're doing with the euro.
I believe that Germany will assume a leadership role in the future and push Europe towards a European position.
Of course, this is not easy for Germany because of its relationship with Israel.
This has always been a sensitive issue.
But I think it's clear to the Germans that they have to play a more independent role.
Does Germany see itself as a major international player? Does he even want to take the lead role?
Or is the leadership role still difficult for Germany?
Germany is still not used to it, it is still unpleasant, and for obvious reasons it is still difficult for this country to play a greater role.
For example, when we look at the European crisis, whenever Germany accepts a bigger role, anti-German sentiment arises.
That doesn't make it easier for the Germans.
It's actually an old problem: we don't want to be surrounded by hostile countries.
From this point of view, Germany is in a much more difficult position than the US.
It has to be acceptable to all sorts of neighbors and opinions, and it's not easy.
The influence of the U.S. in European politics is steadily declining now, but the EU is not filling this vacuum at the moment.
The Germans will just have to play a bigger role.
Even if they don't like it, they're uncomfortable with it and it's going to make them even more unpopularous - c'est la vie!
Stephen Szabo is deputy director of the Transatlantic Academy in Washington, an institute in which academics and policy experts from Europe and North America jointly study the challenges of the transatlantic community.
Szabo is also a member of the German Marshall Fund, where he specialized in German politics, U.S. foreign policy and transatlantic relations.
"Brand protection" in China: when Puma and Armani are suddenly Chinese
Armani is a world-renowned brand, Polo Ralph Lauren too.
But what's Armani Polo?
Behind this lies the designation, which is officially registered in China, but has nothing to do with the original company.
However, he enjoys protection as long as they are not sued by the brand's real owners.
And even then, it's not clear if they'll be able to get rights.
"It is becoming increasingly difficult for foreigners to protect their brands in China," says Thomas Pattloch, a lawyer in Taylor Wessing's office who specializes in protecting rights violations in the Far East.
There's a new case on my desk every week.
Counterfeiters only need a few extra letters to log in their tags.
For example, Gucci will become Lu-Gucci and instead of Prada, they will have Prada-Kny registered.
In this way, German companies, such as Puma, are legally copied.
Pattloch opens the binker with records of the trademark office in Beijing.
On September 14, 2010, a Chinese company protected the trademark Zegna DF Puma, an artificial name that parasitizes even on the Ermenegildo Zegna fashion house.
It is known that the Chinese are world champions in copying and violating intellectual property.
In large cities there are several-storey department stores, in which counterfeits are almost exclusively sold.
Pattloch's cases, however, are different. On behalf of his mandants, he proceeds against Chinese firms receiving the official right to use labels that are already protected elsewhere.
The Chinese call it "Bang Mingpai," an associated brand.
This word focuses on "Bang Dakuan."
This means women who hunt rich men.
Chinese authorities are unaware of any blame.
"It's damaging the business, we've got to combat it," Pattloch urges.
"The brand dilutes itself, the sole position disappears, the image damage is enormous."
Financial losses and judicial costs of affected industries go to millions, especially for expensive sample products.
According to CLSA market research data, China, with a volume of 15 billion euros in a year, is the third largest market for luxury goods and a market that is growing at the fastestest.
However, it is difficult to achieve the deletion of dubious records in the brand register, and it costs something.
The procedure can stretch up to nine years, with an uncertain outcome.
Pattloch recounts cases where the court dismissed the lawsuits because the reproached name became a "market reality" after a long duration.
When the plaintiff's party is unlucky, it has yet to pay the plagiator because it has damaged his brand in China, Pattloch says.
There's the law of the jungle sometimes.
Well-known cases also apply to graphic elements.
In 2009, Daimler lost a legal dispute with construction machinery manufacturer Sana, a business recently taken over by the German producer of Putzmeister concrete mixture pumps.
Therefore, the Chinese are still allowed to use an emblem that resembles a Mercedes star.
Geely, which bought Volvo, originally used a blue-and-while logo that resembled a BMW. The dispute was settled, Geely had to change the logo.
Fashion maker Lacoste lost in China to forgers from Hongon and Singapore, who turned the well-known crocodile in the opposite direction.
Chinese authorities are unaware of any blame.
While CTMO acknowledges that there were deficiencies there in 2010 because of too few employees and premises.
But last year, he reportedly "the course of the work process returned from an emergency to normal."
For example, the number of unprocessed opposition proceedings fell by 22 percent.
57,000 of these cases were closed, 75 percent more than in the previous year.
Nevertheless, there are still 81 500 complaints pending at the office.
It's expensive to do something about it
As is often the case in China, these are impressive numbers.
Last year, CTMO received more than 1.4 million brand protection requests, nearly a third more than 2010.
That's a record, and it means China's 10th time in a row leads a list of new applications around the world.
This also applies to the number of all valid trademarks - there are a total of 5.5 million.
In 2011, 1.8 billion yuan were levied on fees.
In simple terms, each sign-up costs 1280 yuan, or 160 euros, on average.
There are several times against this, as the case of the German family business Freudenberg shows.
For more than seven years, the group has been fighting against a Chinese plagiarist.
Although the Germans achieved that its illegal production of imitated automotive parts had been abolished.
However, the forger has retained Chinese rights to the Freudenberg brand.
We would forget it ourselves, because surnames cannot be protected in Germany, says Hanno Wentzler, chairman of Freudenberg Chemical Specialities in Munich.
CTMO also dismissed an objection from the Munich firm.
In the other two instances, Freudenberg won the law, but the opponent in the dispute continues to fight to this day.
You have to be very careful
The matter is now being discussed in the Supreme Court.
Wentzler has the confidence to bring the matter to a good end and praises the professionalism of the courts.
But he also says: "Driving is extremely costly and costs us a lot of time, money and nerves."
Internal costs cannot be quantified. In the company’s archive, they even had to inspect hundred-year-old files to produce evidence.
Five years ago, Freudenberg vainly offered a "high six-figure amount in euros" in vain to settle the dispute.
"You can see from that what this means for us," Wentzler says.
There is a threat that the danger from the Far East will also affect Europe.
And that's if the impersonators get unprotected marks there.
For example, a Chinese manufacturer wanted to apply the Freudenberg designation for footwear and leather in Germany.
This area of commercial activity has long been relinquished by the Group, yet it has been able to avert registration.
"You have to be very careful," Wentzler says.
Both he and Pattloch's lawyers are advising German companies to be very careful when trading with China.
It is not enough to rely on international trademark rights. Strangers would rather have "everything worth protecting in any way, to apply even in China," says Wentzler.
Otherwise, it can be much more expensive than registration.
Indeed, if Freudenberg succumbs in the last act of its drama with the brand, then it will probably have to pay royalties for its own name to the counterparty, explains Wentzler.
Or we'll be pushed out of the market in a contentious segment.
World AIDS Day: stomping, singing, help
In Heidelberg, the Imbongi Choir is being tried - and in Swaziland, orphans are delighted by their parents who have died of AIDS.
The story of the connection, which surpasses much more than 8733 kilometers.
First of all, there's stomping: cowboy high boots, basketball shoes, women's pumps and men's loafers are looking for tact on the dance floor and quickly find it.
One-two-three-four.
Then the voices of the singers - alt, bas, tenor and soprano - are slowly surging, moms and prosecuting each other.
The 29-year-old conductor Fiete Hopf almost lifts it out of his textile boots as he unifies the voices with his supply wild gestures.
It’s Monday night. In the music hall of the Institute of Medical Psychology in Heidelberg, Imbongi’s choir rehearses a new song.
Fifty singers and singers between the ages of 23 and 69, ranging from human genetics researcher to housekeeper.
"Om'Obani" is not an easy song, every choral voice has a different lyric and, moreover, in a very foreign language - zulu spoken by eleven million people in South Africa, Botswana, Lesothu, Malawi, Mozambique and in parts of Swaziland.
Help for self-help
Unaids, the United Nations' program to combat AIDS, estimates that around 34 million people around the world who are infected with HIV virus.
Of these, 23.5 million live in South Africa.
There are 245 000 orphans in Swaziland after their parents who have died of AIDS.
More than 40 percent of the population is HIV-positive.
For 180 orphans after their parents who died of AIDS, in the village of Esitjeni, Voices for Africa found patrons in Germany.
70 of them attend second-grade school.
For 15 or 20 euros per month, patronage can be taken over.
It guarantees the child tuition, school uniform and one hot meal a day in the Gogo-centre.
Imbongi means "narrator of stories" or "the one who commands".
No one can speak in this space, just sing.
For almost a decade he has been rehearsing a choir of songs in this foreign soft language and occasionally also bringing them back to where they originally came from: to the south of Africa.
At an air distance of 8 733 kilometers from Heidelberg is in the northeast of the Swaziland kingdom the village of Esitjeni, which depends on the strength of the voices of the German corps.
Forty percent are infected
About 2000 people live there, some still in simple clay or straw huts. A large part of them are children.
More than 300 of them no longer have parents because they have succumbed to HIV.
In Esitjeni it can be seen in a small, by what the bench of Swaziland: the highest HIV infection rate, the lowest life expectancy in the world according to UNICEF.
Circumcision of the male foreskin, which has been proven to reduce the risk of infection by half, is not accepted by the population.
More than forty percent of people in Swaziland carry the virus of weakened immunity within them. It is no rarity that they die in less than thirty years.
This village was discovered during a joint trip to Africa in the spring of 2005. Above all, however, members of Imbongi saw many children on the streets who lacked not only parental care, but completely everything: food, clothing, education.
Without completed school education, they have no chance in a poor country at all.
Initially, it was still a private deployment of individuals, which for a few euros a year allowed to send a child to school and give him one hot meal a day.
A year later, however, a choir was founded "Voices for Africa", which has since cared for orphans from parents who died of AIDS, almost professionally.
Facts about sexually Transmissible Infections
Which are the most serious sexually transmitted diseases?
The bacterial SPI includes syphilis, chlamydia and gonorrhea (gonorrhea).
Frequent viral SPIs are HIV, human papillomaviruses, herpes genitalis or hepatitis.
Munks and scabies belong to parasitic SPIs.
Who belongs to the group most affected?
Syphilis and gonorrhea occur primarily in men who have sex with men.
Robert Koch's institute assumes that at least four out of five of all cases of syphilis that have been reported in Germany are transmitted through sexual contacts between men.
In heterosexual adults, sexually communicable diseases that occur frequently, chlamydia infections, trichomonades, candidiasis (mold), gonorrhea and human papillomaviruses.
HIV is relatively small among heterosexual adults in our country, but about 20 percent of those newly infected with HIV belong to this group.
Chlamydia infections occur significantly more frequently among adolescents than in other groups of the population.
According to European surveys, three-quarters of all infections affect young people aged 15 to 25.
In addition, human papillomaviruses are often found among juveniles.
How has the number of infections evolved?
Not all sexually transmitted diseases must be reported.
According to the Robert Koch Institute, the number of people infected with syphilis has more than doubled from 1697 diseases in 2001 to 3698 diseases in 2011.
The number of people newly infected with HIV has declined since 2007.
In 2011 there were about 2700 cases.
That's roughly one-tenth less than the year before.
What symptoms point to STD?
Infectious diseases can cause ulcers in the genital area, difficulty in urinating, discharge, pain in the lower abdomen and blisters or warts.
However, they often do not cause any pain or other symptoms and therefore remain undetected.
How is it possible to protect yourself?
Dangerous contagions can reduce condoms, but they do not offer one hundred percent protection.
Because the causes of sexually transmitted diseases can also be partially transmitted by infection from impurities and close physical contact.
Therefore, experts recommend, especially in the case of frequent alternation of sexual partners, regular examinations.
In the case of early diagnosis, it is possible to cure the majority of SPIs and prevent later consequences.
Thanks to patronage, charitable gifts and, last but not least, royalties that the choir earns by singing all over Germany, some money will be collected.
"In total, we have already sent about 200 000 euros to Esitjen," says Annette Lennartz, chairwoman of the association.
Money from Germany is managed in the village of Zodwa Dlamini itself, a self-confident woman who is able to assert herself.
It also takes care that orphaned children find shelter, for example, at one of their grandmothers.
Gogo, as old women are called zulu, are the support of the village.
Some of them took 14 orphans, gave them a roof over their heads and make sure that the children arrive every day on time in their uniforms for school.
Children, who no longer have anyone, go to Khanyisile shelter, a lonely woman paid for by the association as well as two cooks who cook daily for more than 200 hungry children.
"Voices for Africa" also established a sewing school, built two chicken coops and, together with the U.S
Even though the disease is so obvious throughout the country, everyone is silent about it.
King with 14 wives
"AIDS is absolutely taboo," says Annette Lennartz, "because he has something to do with sexuality."
In fact, it's quite strange in a country where the king officially maintains 14 wives.
The last absolutist monarch of Black Africa, King Mswati III, is known for his immeasurable lifestyle.
Polygamy instead of democracy.
This state-approved way of life, among other things, bears the fact that the HIV virus has spread so quickly in recent decades.
But also many migrating workers who spread the virus around the country.
Annette Lennartz says condoms are free on every corner, "but no one uses them.
Culture is different - unprotected intercourse."
In order to promote cultural exchange at least a little, Imbongi travels once every two, three years together in the south of Africa and sings songs full of grief, combativeness, confidence in the future and the self-confidence of blacks, which many in the southern tip of the black continent still know from apartheid.
A bus full of white people who sing songs in the language of Negroes - such an award not only brings mood and joy, but has also already drawn tears in many wild border soldiers.
There is always a path to Esitjeni, where singers visit children over whom they have patronage.
Although this village can hardly be found on the maps, it is more than well-known in the Ezulweni Valley.
"Go to Esitjen, there's light," people say.
And when you go back to Heidelberg, which is 8733 kilometers from here, to the trampling singers in the rehearsal room on Bergheimer Straß, you can see that the light shines here, too.
Transmission: NASA has discovered ice on Mercury
The Messenger probe has found evidence that there's ice on the planet Mercury.
Ice cover could be up to 20 meters thick.
The U.S. National Aeronautics and Cosmonautics Administration (NASA) has demonstrated the existence of ice on planet Mercury.
Although it's closest to the Sun, there's also frozen water, according to three studies published on Thursday by the professional magazine "Science."
The Messenger probe has found evidence that there is an ice blanket in the area of this planet, which lies in permanent shade.
It is supposedly at least 30 centimeters and maybe up to 20 meters thick.
This water apparently came from comets, or perhaps even from the asteroids that crashed into Mercury.
But let’s not put the ice in connection with the existence of life on this planet, said lead scientists working on the Messenger probe, Sean Solomon.
The temperature on Mercury can reach up to 426 degrees Celsius.
However, the findings could help us to clarify how water and other essential elements of life reached other areas of the solar system.
Probes, telescopes and small robots such as Phoenix, unobserved by Earthlings, are involved in exploring the depths of space.
From time to time, they send images to the Earth, small windows to endless distances.
The image comes from a photographic instrument developed by German scientists at the Max Planck Institute.
The eight planets of our solar system, in addition, the dwarf planet Ceres.
Like Pluto, which circles the sun beyond Neptune, Ceres is not a "planetary" according to the International Astronomical Union's new conceptual definition.
This cut-up from the Spitzer telescope's infrared image shows a "family portrait" of countless generations of stars. The oldest stars look like blue dots, harder-to-disclose are the pink-colored "newborns" in the obstetric hall of stars.
This region, where stars are formed - scientists unromantically call it W5 - was discovered by the Spitzer telescope in Cassiopeia at a distance of 6500 light-years.
This changing heat of a dying star was captured by NASA's Spitzer Telescope.
The murmy ring consists of a material that the star ejected during the dying process.
In the giant Trifid nebula, which is 5400 light-years away from Earth, new stars arise from gas and dust.
A photograph of the galactic "cradle" was taken by NASA's Spitzer Telescope.
Cluster of Pleiades, also called "Seven Sisters", can be seen with the naked eye at night.
Thanks to the telescope, however, the colors stand out better.
The Helix nebula may look like a red eye to an observer in this infrared image.
It is located at a distance of 700 light-years from the constellation of Aquarius.
Its resemblance with the earthly continent earned this nebula the name of North America.
The combination of normal and infrared photography is the cause of an extraordinary discoloration.
This "baby" among the stars was able to shoit the infrared detectors of the Spitzer telescope in full glory.
Saturn and its rings: how they came about is one of the greatest astronomical mysteries.
Perhaps these are the remains of Saturn's moon, which disappeared 4.5 billion years ago without a trace.
One of the largest and sharpest images of the Hubble telescope: the vortex galaxy
According to its coloration, images of spiral galaxies become real works of art.
The footage, published by the European Southern Observatory, depicts the Trifid Nebula several thousand light-years away in the constellation Sagittarius.
The name Trifid comes from the Latin word "triffidus" (divided into three parts, triangular), because the dark strips of dust divide the glowing heart of this stellar obstetrician into three parts.
In the constellation of Snakes, astronomers have photographed a precursory of cosmic precipitation: at a distance of 400 million light-years from Earth, the nuclei of the two merging galaxies arise and must inevitably result in a collision.
This birth of a star was recorded by the Hubble telescope in the spiral galaxy M83.
If you don't like technical shortcuts, you can call it Southern Windbreaker.
A photograph from the Hubble Space Telescope shows a cut-out of the Iris Nebula in the constellation of Cepheus.
This 1400 light-year-long nebula consists of dust beans, which are ten to a hundred times smaller than the usual domestic dust.
This image was compiled from optical and X-ray images of various telescopes.
It depicts a ring made of black holes, which is 430 million light-years away from Earth.
This group of galaxies, called Arp 273, was recorded by NASA's Hubble Space Telescope.
This larger spiral galaxy is called UGC 1810.
In this stellar nebula is the brightest group of the young stars of our Milky Way.
The cradle of stars gives rise to ever new additions.
Also, these stellar clouds, connected to the Rosette Nebula, are still giving birth to new young stars. They are 5000 light-years away from Earth.
There's no dust yet in this bright glowing galaxy with a small black hole, it's just gas.
Scientists believe that it originated only shortly after the Big Bang, when there was mostly hydrogen throughout the universe.
Our views into space: the most important telescopes
The telescope was reportedly invented by Hans Lipperhey in 1608. Even before Galilee Galilei, he used it a year later to observe the stars.
Since then, optical telescope mirrors have continued to widen - and the views they convey are getting deeper and deeper.
The world's largest telescope for 30 years, from 1947 to 1975, the Hale telescope at the Palomar Observatory near San Diego.
The mirror in this picture had a diameter of five meters.
There is a Large Binocular Telescope in Arizona.
It allows you to peer into the universe with two mirrors, each with a diameter of 8.4 meters.
The interior of the Gran Telescopio Canarias on one of the Canary Islands - La Palma is huge. Only the mirror measures on average 10.4 meters.
The Southern African Large Telescope in South Africa is segmented to save costs.
However, its diameter reaches about eleven meters.
Disadvantage of the inexpensive method of construction: the telescope is ensured by a hook at a height angle, thus limiting its mobility.
The Hobby-Eberly telescope in Texas is also fixed at a height angle.
Its peculiarity is the high amplification of luminous flux.
In spite of the relatively small diameter of the mirror, this equals the world's largest mirror telescopes.
Using a radio telescope in Arecibo (Portorico), scientists in space are searching for extraterrestrial signals.
This radio telescope has a diameter of 305 meters.
In "Search for Extraterrestrial Intelligence" (SETI) - the search for extraterrestrial intelligence - can be helped by any computer owner who puts its computational power available.
A look at the observatory of the European Southern Observatory (ESO) in the Chilean Andes.
There's a Very Large Telescope standing here - and he's doing his name.
This telescope, with a total of four mirrors, can illuminate even the middle infrared spectrum.
Europe's ultra-large telescope is also planned at ESO's Chilean Observatory.
Its main mirror is supposed to have a full 42 meters and is supposed to be composed of almost 1,000 mirror elements.
However, images can be counted no earlier than 2018.
Two Keck telescopes on Hawaii's Mauna Kea volcano were considered the largest in the world until 2007.
They offer two mirrors, each with a diameter of ten meters.
Keck's telescopes are part of Mauna Kee's observatory, which, in addition to them, also views the universe using the Subaru and IRTTF telescopes.
Also on Mauna Kea, a new giant telescope is to be created - with a thirty-metre mirror diameter.
Here you can admire it in the picture.
However, the Hubble Cosmic Telescope conveys the most significant impressions of the universe.
It has been providing us from distant worlds since April 24, 1990.
Kepler Space Telescope has been searching for extrasolar planets since March 2009 - especially those that are habitable.
On February 2, 2011, NASA announced that 1235 candidate planets had been detected since the mission began.
In the picture are documented the final preparations before the launch of the Kepler space telescope.
James Webb's Space Telescope (JWST) will be fired into space aboard the Ariane5 rocket in 2018 at the earliest.
The primary mirror of the space infrared telescope has a diameter of 6.5 meters.
One of the telescope's tasks is to look for the light of the first stars and galaxies after the Big Bang.
The researchers say there's ice at Mercury's South Pole.
However, there is no reliable data for this, as Messenger circles this planet much closer to the North Pole.
Radar measurements have indicated for several decades that there is ice on Mercury.
Thanks to the Messenger probe, which was sent in 2004 and is the first to orbit Mercury, scientists are now certain.
Drink butter every day and live to be 168 years old
In southern Azerbaijan, many people will live to a biblical age.
There's even a museum of longevity.
Looking for clues in a country where 97-year-old people are still relatively young.
In southern Azerbaijan, many people will live to a biblical age.
There's even a museum of longevity.
Looking for clues in a country where 97-year-old people are still relatively young.
We can can call the Talyš mountain ride wildly romantic.
The microbus rumbles along the road with many bends along the densely wooded hills, predatory rivers and simple farmhouses.
It's all green and juicy, it almost seems like we're in Schwarzwald.
However, this is the farthest south of Azerbaijan. The border with Iran is only a few kilometers away.
Here lives the Caucasian ethnic group Talysh, of which not much is known, except that it speaks perfectly Persian and Azerbaijani and lives to a very old age.
Lerik is the final station.
This town boasts too monumental architecture from Soviet times, which somehow does not fit into the picturesque mountain landscape.
Tourists from Europe do not come here. Arrival from the Azerbaijani capital of Baku is too difficult.
It takes eight hours for 323 kilometers, because the road is only single-lane.
The fairy-tale wealth for which the country owes diesel in the Caspian Sea has not yet reached the countryside.
Pilata Fatulayeva (48), however, is convinced that Lerik has enough to become a tourist attraction.
"Baku became famous for the Eurovision Grand Prix in May - and we will have a festival of the oldest people in the world here next year," Fatulayeva says.
She's the director of a longevity museum, probably the only one in the world.
Here, the life of nearly eight dozen Talyshes from the neighborhood is documented, who are hundreds of years older. Fatulayeva shows a black-and-white photo.
It's my grandfather, he's 120 years old.
At the age of 136, he still fathered a child.
The controversial star of this museum, however, is the herder of Şirali Müslümov sheep, who reportedly was 168 years old.
There is no birth certificate, of course.
And given that the oldest person with documents was 122 years old, Müslümov's age seems extremely questionable.
"He was born in this area in 1805 and died in 1973," Fatulayeva explains.
This man was reportedly married three times and has had 23 children. At the age of 136, he is said to have fathered a daughter.
Wasn't Şirali Müslümov wrong at his age by a few decades?
Rembrandt Scholz, a gerontologist at the Max Planck Institute in Rostock, has also heard of age-old people in Central Asia.
"Conspicuously many people of old age also live in some areas of China, in Japan or in the Hunza Valley in Pakistan," says Scholz, "also in Sardinia there are extremely many very old men."
However, due to the lack of evidence, no scientific evidence of age can be given, especially when registers are missing.
Every day a glass of melted butter
But the fact is that people in the area around Lerike live to a biblical age conspicuously.
There are currently 20 people who are over the age of 100.
So why do so many old people live here in the south?
The Azerbaijani guide Farid Mugimzadeh explains this with the odd Talysh genes.
On the contrary, the director of the Fatulyeva Museum believes that it consists of nutrition.
However, the fact that the caloric diet of Talysh, who love meat, bread and, above all, dairy products, and many of whom drink a glass of dissolved butter on a daily basis, is so healthy, does not seem very plausible from a bromatological point of view.
Or is it an original way of life that keeps people young? In Cengemiran, a tiny settlement near the town of Lerik, Rubaba Mirzayeva lives.
At 97, she is still relatively young for this latitude.
Mirzayeva, who claims to have 143 offspring, lives in a plain wooden house that is typical of the entire Caucasian region.
He's sitting on the ground with a butterfly that he's relentlessly rocking.
There are eight people living under one roof, including one of Mirzayeva's sons and a daughter, both of whom are grandparents long ago.
There are also two young children around.
In the kitchen, tea is prepared for guests, which is served in typical bellieved glasses of "armada".
Mirzayeva's white teeth form the perfect range and under the scarf hides a long dark blonde copy, which her son proudly reveals in front of us.
I've always washed my hair with milk, so they didn't look good and keep my color.
"I've never used shampoo either," Mirzayeva says.
Monthly retirement is enough for life
She always ate only what came from her own yard, tomatoes, potatoes, peas.
I haven’t bought food in a supermarket in my entire life.
Then he tells me about his man, who was in the army.
It was the worst in World War II.
But everything got better when "beloved father" Geidar Aliyev took over the oar.
Propaganda sounds odd from an old woman's mouth.
But the cult, which is made around the figure of the father of nations who ruled his country as a dictator, knows no bounds in Azerbaijan.
He was in power until 2003, then his son Ilham took over.
At least the old people in Azerbaijan don't suffer from poverty.
Mirzayeva receives a pension of 230 manats every month (approximately the same amount in euros). This is the amount from which it is possible to make a good living.
And perhaps Mirzaeva's gray son is right: "Old people enjoy great seriousness in our culture."
They live in the middle of a large family, are loved, cared for and happy.
Unless there's a reason to stay alive as long as possible
The concept of "human rights" is lacking in the constitution
A revolution has returned to Cairo.
Disputed demonstrations in Cairo have exposed the country's deep ambivalence.
There is sharp discussion of a future constitution based on Sharia law.
The Egyptian president did not spare pathos.
We have to make a transition.
"And for succeeding, I am responsible, before the people and before God," he said on state television.
His speech was addressed to the whole nation, but above all to Christian Copts, liberals, enlightened Muslims and secular persons.
All of them, still hopelessly quarreled in a confused opposition, are afraid.
Fear of a "God state" on the Nile at the mercy of the powerful Muslim Brotherhood.
Mohammed Morsi almost apologetically stated that he temporarily limited the competence of the constitutional court and expanded his own to "save the revolution."
But what the 61-year-old-old engineer, which has a doctor’s degree at the University of Southern California, really wants to save, isn’t made clear by the Egyptians.
Is the judiciary deprived of power?
The 234 paragraphs, which were hastily approved by the Hundred-Hundred Constituent Assembly with the predominance of Islamists in a 15-hour night marathon, arouse concern.
As in earlier constitutions, it is also said in this suggestion that jurisdiction is based on the "principles of Islamic law."
But what does "principles" mean?
This was and is a matter of interpretation, and it can be feared that the Islamists will use this unclear wording and the associated legal free space for a stricter Sharia interpretation.
At the very least, this is illuminated by a newly added paragraph: In all matters relating to Sharia, it is necessary to consult al-Azhar University, which is the country's most important Islamic institution, with great influence throughout Sunni Islam.
It may not, but it may mean that the clergy will supervise the legislation, which would give the judiciary a de facto withdrawal.
There are many interpretations in the draft constitution
The problem is also that the civilian jurisdiction of military courts should continue to be maintained.
These courts served under Mubarak's rule to suppress the opposition.
After the fall of the dictatorship, up to 11,000 civilians were in military custody.
As stated in the proposal, the state should also protect "the true nature of the Egyptian family and promote its morality and values."
It is legally formulated so inaccurately that, with the help of this paragraph, state institutions could even control the content of film art and literature.
It means nothing but censorship in comprehensible speech.
Moreover, no paragraph defines in writing explicit equality between men and women.
However, another paragraph forbids defamation or gossip of the Prophet Muhammad and his envoy.
Unclear, however, remains what is defamation and how prosecuted it will be prosecuted.
Equally dubious is the wording that "insulting people" is prohibited.
In this case, is it sufficient for a cartoon of the president or a joke at the expense of law experts?
A matter of interpretation, like many others in this proposal, which Morsi will submit for signature and, in his words, "very soon" will be presented to the Egyptians for the purpose of a popular vote.
"Revolution has returned"
For several weeks now, there has been an opposition to the superiority of Islamists.
Tens of thousands of people, in an unusual statement, gathered Friday night in Cairo's Tahrir Square and pledged to destroy the constitutional charter before it came into force.
"The revolution has returned and we will win," said Hamdin Sabbahi, who ranked third in the presidential election.
The Nobel laugher and former director-general of the International Atomic Energy Agency, Mohamed Baradej, declared that the draft constitution belongs "to the dustbin of history."
Via the short-range news service Twitter has accused Morsi supporters of wanting to carry out a "coup against democracy."
"If he holds a referendum, we will go to his palace and overthrow him," said Yasser Said, a member of the opposition.
"We're not tired yet, the blood of our brothers has not yet been punished," Egyptian media quoted opposition politician Khalid Ali as saying.
Several judges have announced that they do not want to oversee the referendum, making it invalid.
"The Qur'an is our Constitution"
A well-organized Muslim Brotherhood has declared a disdemonstration, out of caution, but not in Tahrir Square, but after a mass prayer on the other side of the Nile before Cairo University.
It was attended by many veiled women and supporters of Salafists, who called out: "The people demand the application of God's law."
They challenged Morsi: "Clean up the earth!" and assured, "The Qur'an is our constitution."
The struggle for sovereignty over the symbolic Tahrir Square, on which it all began, would probably provoke a state similar to a civil war.
It wasn't Mursi's supporters quite obviously wanted to take any chances.
The Muslim Brotherhood said opponents and proponents of the draft constitution had spoken loudly and clearly.
Now it's time to let the people at the ballot box decide in which direction the country should go.
A majority in favor of the Islamist proposal is considered certain.
"The word 'human rights' does not occur even once"
The enforced constitutional dispute, which should actually continue until February and integrate all social forces, has been told by Hafiz Abu SāḶda.
A forty-eight-year-old human rights lawyer and chairman of the Egyptian Human Rights Organization (EOHR) defended members of the Muslim Brotherhood when they were in jail or stood trial for Mubarak.
Not because he shared their ideology, but because human rights are indivisible to him.
For that, he was beaten, sentenced and imprisoned.
"And in the new constitution now there aren't the words 'human rights' even once," he complains in an interview with "Welt am Sonntag."
Morsi's extension of power to all three power forces in the state has forced this lawyer to resign.
These measures are scandalous offenses against the democratic rules of the game and will lead Egypt to a new dictatorship.
"Instead for the president to strengthen civil society, he's effectively abolished it," Sa'da complains.
However, without civil society organizations, democracy cannot work.
Sa'da also feels abandoned by the international community, which pursues the struggle of ideological factions on the Nile with a mixture of curiosity and tension.
That might backfire on him.
A protester in Tahrir Square warns: "You've created a monster you can't control anymore."
Norwegian Rakfisk: The world's most stinking fish?
Five million people living in Norway are enjoying one of the highest standards of living not only in Europe but also in the world.
Could the secret of the country's success be linked to the local appetite for extraordinarily smelly fish?
Take a few overripe cheeses.
Put them in the middle of a pile of dirty and wet football gear.
Let it lie for a week.
That's how you'll get a smelly paralyzing rakfisk, one of Norway's biggest delicates.
I'm in Fagernes, about three hours from Oslo.
There is snow, a wonderful view - and there's an ubiquitous smell in the air.
Rakfisk is a salted trout fermented in water for up to one year - it depends on how much you want the fish to smell.
As it gets dark and getting cold, the Norwegians here in Fagernes are beginning to gather for a festival dedicated to this how to say it best, the most captivating dish.
"You'll eat it raw and then you'll drink it with a glass of aquavit," says Havard Halvarsen, a local professional firefighter, but also the so-called "Rakfisk general," who is responsible for the course of the festival.
All around us, people eat small fish cubes and drink them with plenty of alcohol.
"Some people prefer aquavite rather than rakfisk," Havard says.
The drink can overwhelm the smell.
I'll try a few pieces.
If a fish can avoid your nose, it's not that bad - it's a similar piece of sushi that's been on a long bus trip.
Rakfisk is a product of a very different time when there was poverty in Norway, and since there were no refrigerators yet, fish were dipped into airtight barrels of water and salt in the autumn.
Then, in the middle of winter, when they were well fermented, they pulled up and ate - the senses no doubt dulled with alcohol.
Just one generation ago, thousands of Norwegians were forced to leave their country and look for work, mostly emigrating to the United States.
Currently, the population is growing rapidly - more than 13% are immigrants attracted by large amounts of labour, high earnings, and an extensive care system.
Residents of Sweden, an old rival that was much richer than Norway not long ago, are flowing into the country for work.
The rakfisk is viewed as something important, indispensable, albeit a rather smelly part of Norway's past.
It is one of the more expensive dishes on the market.
Although expensive is everything - a small glass of beer or a sandwich will cost you 9 pounds (14 dollars) apiece.
Norway often doesn't get into global news - and it seems to suit most people that way.
The name of Anders Breivik, a right-wing racist extremist who shot dead 77 men, women and children last year, is still mentioned with distaste.
Instead, gunfirence is called "an event from July 22."
Norwegians can hardly believe that in their peaceful country one of them was capable of such brutality and murder.
Behind Norway's current wealth lies the growth of one of the world's largest oil and gas industries from the early 1970s.
"But oil isn't the only reason we're doing so well," our waitress Anna, who, with her long blonde hair and surprisingly blue eyes, says when serving plates with a lying raffish.
We're, as you say, judicious people.
Her English, like the English of most of the people here, is absolutely impeccable.
We don't show off, we don't like flamboyance.
Norway handles its oil wealth very cautiously - in addition to a small percentage, most of the money from the sector is invested in a special fund intended for future generations.
When everyone else in the years leading to a global financial crash was throwing away money they didn't have, the Norwegians had the strings on their pouch tight.
"Until we can ski in the winter and go on trips in the summer, we're happy," Anna says.
"And eating rakfisk," he adds with a carefree laugh.
I've been standing in the snow queuing for something to eat - I've had enough of the rakfiska.
Such an elk burger is definitely something else and is beautifully juicy.
But there's smelling fiscuours here again tonight.
The hotel where I stay is one of the many places where the rakfisk is served for dinner and where guests choose the best, or rather for the smell of the most demanding fish.
They've got a connection to a live TV broadcast in which a presenter with a bow tie is surrounded by plates with rakfis.
It's like the Eurovision singing competition.
"What score do you have for the best fish up there in the mountains, Thore-Juergen?"
"Here's our scoring, Havard."
There's applause and laughter.
A man falls from his chair, perhaps overwhelmed by an aquavite.
Or maybe the fumes from all the fish.
Enrique Pena Nieto from Mexico has a tough start
As incoming Mexican President Enrique Pena Nieto prepares to take office, Will Grant from the BBC is looking at the challenges he faces and the mixed expectations of his fellow citizens.
The traffic in Mexico City is particularly bad at the moment.
In a city that is clogged even in the best times, steel barriers have been erected enclosing several key routes to the capital and causing chaos on the roads.
However, the aim was not to prevent commuters from getting to work, but to prevent protesters from accessing parliament.
On Saturday, Mexico's new president Enrique Pena Nieto will receive a presidential sash and take over the leadership of the nation.
They are facing a difficult task.
At the time of Felipe Calderon, Mexico was doing well economically, but the country is in the clutches of a drug war that has already taken an estimated 60 000 lives in six years.
"My government has a great commitment towards the Mexican people to reduce violence," President Pena Nieto told U.S. President Barack Obama in the Oval Office earlier this week.
I will propose a new security strategy that will enable us to achieve this goal.
Before he shook hands with the U.S. president, President Pena Nieta gained political experience as governor of his home state of Mexico.
Opinions about the new leader are also different in his old homeland, a populous and vast state surrounding the capital.
Straight man.
For example, in the country town of Valle del Bravo, they remember him for good.
Citizens attribute it to the development of tourism in the resort and the construction of infrastructure.
You can now reach the city on one of the new highways that Pena Nieto had built. These highways are a huge improvement over the leaky and bumpy roads that used to be here.
The albums with his name also hang on the wall of a modern sports center and an impressive interactive museum with the theme of climate change.
"We expect it to bring real and lasting change," says friend and political ally Gabriel Olvera Hernandez, a state MP for President Pene Nieto's party, the Party of the Institutionalized Revolution (PRI).
In particular, we hope for the interesting and real changes in security and the economy that our country so badly needs.
After an uninterrupted 81 years in power, the PRI party was displaced by Vicent Fox.
MP Olvera admits that after 12 years outside Los Pinos' presidential palace, much is expected inside the party from Enriqua Pena Nieta.
And he rejects the opposition's statement characterizing the new president as a man without substantial content.
He's a very straightforward man, very intrigued with the country's excellent vision.
He's a great statesman, and he's a person who can listen.
But on the other side of the state, this is not the impression that many of its former governors have.
In Nezahualcoyotle, also known as Ciudad Neza, the contrast with the cobbled streets of Valle del Bravo cannot be more apparent.
Tucked under highway overpasses in many ways resembles the suburbs of Mexico's capital itself.
And the problems in self-governing municipality are also persistent and urban.
Earlier this year, an army was called in to help manage drug gangs operating in neighborhoods, especially violent violence against women.
In the last two years alone, the bodies of dozens of murdered women have been dumped in desolate places next to a huge garbage dump.
By the time Pena Nieto was governor, more than 1,000 women had been murdered in the state of Mexico, far more than in the notorious city of Ciudad Juarez - a place that is synonymous with the murders of innocent women.
At best, critics of Peno Nieto say that in his tenure in office, he has failed to adequately deal with the problem of women's murders.
And at worst, his leadership accuses him of closing his eyes.
In a concrete house typical of a dilapidated neighborhood, Irine Buendia shows me photographs of her late daughter Mariana Luna and suppresses crying.
According to the official version, Mariana committed suicide in 2010.
However, her family believes her partner murdered her.
"When I arrived at her house, it looked like someone had washed her body," Ms Buendia recalled.
There were signs of blows, and posthumous stiffness had already begun.
As her mother tells the story, Mariana gazes down from the picture on the wall, right next to the cross, on which is the only word: "Justice."
However, as the family says, this is exactly what they have been denied.
The state authorities treated me as if I were an old gossip, a troublemaker and a whey.
They want you to simply accept what they say and remain silent.
"But it can't be right when there have been so many mistakes and omissions," he says.
Sherpa, which President Pena Nieto will receive on Saturday, is accompanied by great responsibility.
Over the past six years, tens of thousands of families in Mexico have been affected by violent crime, and the new president has vowed that for the duration of his tenure in office they will be his priority.
"I hope he's going to be the same president as he was governor," MP PRI Olvera says in Valle del Bravo.
However, this is exactly what the families of the victims in Ciudad Neza are most afraid of.
Bradley Manning didn't complain of mistreatment, military prosecutor says
The military prosecutor is trying to refute Bradley Manning's allegations relating to crude treasure in custody.
The hearing focuses on Manning's stay in military custody in Quantico, Virginia.
The defense wants the case to be dismissed based on the gross treatment of Manning in prison.
The soldier is accused of stealing thousands of classified documents.
The military prosecutor is trying to prove that Private Bradley Manning, who is accused of the largest leak of classified material in U.S. history, has missed many chances of complaining about the crude treatment he allegedly suffered in military custody.
Military Attorney General Ashden Fein, in a cross-interrogation of Private Manning at a preliminary hearing in Ft. Maede in Maryland, claimed that records of weekly visits Manning had with unit officers during nine months of custody in Quantico, Virginia, showed no complaints about ill-treatment.
The cross-examination, which took place during a hearing made at the defense's suggestion to dismiss the case against Manning because of gross treatment at the time of detention, which in itself is a sufficient sentence, took place a day after Manning testified that he was considering committing suicide in custody.
An Army intelligence analyst, arrested in June 2010, is accused of stealing thousands of classified documents while serving in Iraq.
The material was then posted online on WikiLeaks.
WikiLeaks has never confirmed that Manning was the source of her information.
At Friday's hearing, military prosecutor Fein, together with Manning, went through forms that officers filled out after meeting Manning at Quantico detention prison, where he was held from July 2010 to April 2011 in high-security mode.
Manning's officers asked questions and recorded his answers.
When Fein asked him about these forms on Friday, Manning acknowledged that he assessed the treatment by his guards as "excellent" and assessed the overall treatment at the facility as "very professional."
Forms show no complaints about mistreatment, even though Manning's officers directly asked about how he was being treated, Fein argued.
Manning responded that he expressed verbal concerns about the various issues and that the officers who visited him spoke with him about these concerns and indicated that they would deal with them, but did not notice these problems.
"They wrote down "no problems" (after discussing concerns), and that didn't necessarily mean I didn't mention them," Manning said.
Judge Denise Lind also asked Manning why he did not complain about dealing with his person in January 2011 during a meeting with a committee judging suicidal thoughts, which he had previously expressed in a form a few months earlier.
Manning responded that in the course of this meeting, his intention was to achieve a reduction in the "injury prevention" regime.
The military said it had used this restrictive regime (one degree below the surveillance regime because of suicide) for its protection and the safety of others.
"I wanted the guards in custody to know that I'm fine and that I wanted to abolish this injury prevention regime... so that I could enjoy an increased quality of life, as I have seen," Manning said.
Manning testified Thursday about his arrest in Iraq and transport to Kuwait, where he was held nearly two months before being transported to a detention facility at a Marines base in Quantico, Virginia in July 2010.
He said he was considering suicide in Kuwait and fainted there once because of heat.
He stated that that he wasn't allowed to know what was going to happen to him and what was happening in the outside world was very daunting.
"My world shrunk only to Arifjan's base, to that cage," Manning said on Thursday.
I thought I'd die in that cage.
Manning stated that after arriving in Quantico, he spent most of his days (at least 21 hours, but often for more than 23 hours) in a small cell without any company.
Manning said he was only allowed one mattress, a blanket, slimmers, some clothes and glasses.
He said he was trying to walk because sleeping or just lying down was against the regulations during the day.
Manning said that he always slept with light from the outside of the cell in his eyes.
He stated that if the guards couldn't see his face when he turned to the other side at night, they woke him up and had to turn back.
Manning's attorney filed a formal complaint about the treatment of Private Manning in January 2011.
Manning was transferred to Fort Leavenworth, Kansas in April 2011.
On Friday, Judge Manninga also asked about the allegations he made in Thursday's testimony, relating to the fact that, after being forced to sleep one night in his Quantico cell naked, he was also forced to stand naked in front of guarders and other co-inmates during the morning count.
Manning testified that he had not been allowed to obscure himself with his blanket when counting.
During an interrogation on Friday conducted by Judge Manning, he stated that, by order of his guards, he concluded that he had a blanket that he could cover, but admitted that no one had issued him an order to postpone it.
On Thursday, Manning testified that he had been forced to sleep naked the previous night because he tried to show the officer that he wasn't dangerous.
Manning said he told the officer that he could use a belt from his underwear or his slippers to hurt himself, but he didn't.
Manning testified that his underwear, his slippers and glasses had been removed from his cell that night.
His lawyer hopes that the judge will take into account his experience of his imprisonment and that, in the event of his conviction before a court-martial, the beginning of which is expected at the beginning of next year, his sentence will be significantly reduced.
The defense said it plans to pleadicate Manning to plead guilty in lower offences and that he will fight the other charges because they are too extreme.
The beginning of the hearing is scheduled for this week, the military prosecutor is expected to advocate detention terms as justified.
The Pentagon sated a statement that Manning has been held in accordance with regulations relating to all those held in custody with maximum security.
Allegations made against Manning include aiding the enemy, unlawfully causing the publication of intelligence documents on the Internet, the transmission of information related to national security, and the theft of public property or records.
If charged on all counts, he can be sentenced to life in prison.
My Crisis of Mexican-American Identity
He says many people have been forced to leave Mexico because of the lack of options that are there.
Mexicans tend to blame those who have left; they are reminded of difficult times, he says.
Navarrette says Mexican Americans are trapped between two worlds.
On my last trip to Mexico's capital, I barely made my way through the crowd and arrived at a space for an immigrant agenda where I encountered a problem.
Arrows pointed to one of two queues: one for "Mexicanos" ("Mexicans") and the other for "Extranjeros" ("Foreigns").
I've been standing there for a few seconds and I didn't know where to go.
I grew up in Central California, and all my life I've been called "Mexican."
It's an ethnic acronym, the same as when my friends in Boston call themselves "The Irish" and my friends in New York call themselves "Italians."
So later, I ended up with the term "Mexican American."
But this was Mexico.
And it wasn't necessary in my grandfather's home to use shortcuts.
I've just been American.
I speak Spanish, well enough to handle both sides of the conversation in this language.
However, I don't have the vocabulary of a native speaker and I don't know how to get rid of an American accent.
So I pulled out my American passport and queued for "Extranjeros."
I've been thinking of the moment when newly elected Mexican President Enrique Pena Nieto visited President Obama at the White House this week.
On the agenda, they had the usual issues being discussed when the leaders of these two countries meet: immigration, drugs and commerce.
Pena Nieto also wanted to talk about the growth of the Mexican economy, which is one of the reasons why there is the same likelihood that Mexicans will stay in Mexico as to travel to the United States.
He wants to connect with the United States and Canada and create a trade bloc similar to the European Union in North America.
And Pena Nieto has pledged to continue Mexico's war on drug cartels, even if he gave no details.
For Mexico, the relationship with the United States is complicated and full of hostile feelings.
Most Americans have probably never thought of the fact that in 1848 the United States invaded Mexico and forced its leader, under the threat of weapons, to rewrite half of Mexican territory to the United States.
But for Mexicans, who think in the range of centuries, not minutes, comments are ubiquitous.
So a minute after the representative of the United States says something just a little critical about Mexico, you'll hear in the Mexican press and among the elite complaints about how Americans interfere with their neighbor's sovereignty.
And Montezuma's children set off on a war path.
And yet for Mexico, the real challenge is the relationship to more than 35 million Mexican Americans living in the United States.
Do you want to talk about hostile feelings?
There's a lot of those.
In Mexico there are winners and losers, the people to whom the country offers opportunities, and others who don't.
The only reason why so many people with Mexican roots live in cities such as Los Angeles, Las Vegas, Phoenix, Denver or San Antonio is that at some point of our family tree was a person, perhaps a parent or grandparent who was denied an opportunity in Mexico and had to go north.
And more often than it would seem, this person matched the profile - dark skin, weak education, origin from a poor village, etc.
We are their descendants, and we are loyal to them.
Not towards Mexico.
And even though we're now living an American dream, we're going to good schools and we're having a good job, we can never forget the fact that we're living an American dream, not a Mexican one.
Our identities can sometimes be unclear, but our loyalty isn't.
It's towards the United States.
Moreover, we are aware that many elite Mexicans belonging to the ruling class don't like us.
This feeling is mutual.
He sees us as a reminder of humiliating defeat and views us as an inferior race that is not Mexican enough.
Our Spanish will never be good enough, our handcuffs to Mexico never strong enough.
In their opinion, our existence is only about failure.
If our families in Mexico didn't fail, they'd never leave it.
And we wouldn't now be trapped behind a silk curtain, with a good life in the United States, but still with lost souls.
My wife, who was born in Guadalajara and legally traveled to the United States in childhood, reminds me that there are tensions between Mexicans and Mexican Americans because Mexicans understand more who they are, and Mexican Americans displeasure it.
Even though she's a citizen of the United States, she sees herself as part of two countries.
At the same time, many Mexican Americans I know don't feel like part of either.
We love listening to mexican groups, Los Tigres del Norte, but also Bruce Springsteen.
You get the best of both countries, but you don't have roots in either.
In Mexico, he sees us as Americans.
And in the United States, we're considered Mexican.
And in order to make relations even more complicated, some Mexican leaders want to reunite with the diaspora, and some of the intelligence, as I learned during my trip.
They want Mexican Americans to work for Mexico as "provisible ambassadors" and represent Mexican interests in the United States.
We should tell our fellow Americans how great it is to visit this country, and pressure political leaders to consolidate relations with Mexico.
Oh, well.
That won't happen then.
Too many hostile feelings.
And thanks to income inequality and widespread corruption and drug violence, many of us aren't sure it's a great country.
I feel like it's up to you, amigos.
That's fair.
If at least some Mexicans aren’t ready to forgive the United States for how they treated Mexico one and a half centuries ago, they must accept the fact that some Mexican Americans still feel displeasure about the way their family members were treated in a much closer past.
Hmmm.
Maybe we're more "Mexicans" than I thought.
Old Battles, New Middle East
Ending fire between Israel and Hamas could still be an uncertain basis for peace.
Can there ever be lasting peace between Arabs and Jews in the Middle East?
Another round of bloodshed suggests that every one like this is futile.
Among the usual unnecessary conjectures about who started it, many buildings were turned into debris, killed by more than 140 Palestinians, mostly civilians, and six Israelis. And for the first time, rockets from Gaza fell near Tel Aviv, an Israeli metropolis, and by the holy city of Jerusalem.
But even if the Israelis and Palestinians appear to have been stuck in their ancient conflict, the Middle East is changing all around them.
The Arab Spring has changed the old orders, and the Palestinians and Israelis, whether they like it or not, are caught up in the current chaos in the region.
Perhaps this will make their fight even bloodier than ever.
However, there are reasons to think that this may interrupt their deadly stalemate.
A war that's neither won neither lost.
At first glance, it seems that there are currently not many reasons for optimism.
Even if a halt closed on November 21 lasts, this week's fighting has strengthened hawks on both sides.
Hamas leaders, an Islamic movement that has ruled Gaza since 2007, will claim to have forced Israelis to retreat, even as Gaza suffered defeat.
Despite the killings of several Hamas leaders and the closure of $1.7 million of Gaza residents in one of the planet's most pitiful and populous corners, Israel has failed to destroy Hamas.
Indeed, Hamas is gaining influence in the West Bank, another piece of Palestine, which is currently controlled by its staunch rivals from Fatah, a more moderate Palestinian faction.
Moreover, Hamas leaders can judge very well that time is on their side.
Islamists all over the Arab world have gained more influence, so Hamas has made powerful and wealthy friends.
Turkey, a resurrected regional power that once was Israel's closest Muslim ally, sided with Hamas. So did Qatar, one of the wealthiest and most dynamic countries in the Gulf.
Cheering people from Hamas say the Islamic crescrex encircles around Israel, in the north of Lebanon, where Hezbollah party/militias rule, through Syria, where rebels, who are increasingly leaning towards Islamism, can depose Bashar al-Assad, to Jordan, where Hamas allies threaten the king.
And above all, on the southern wing of Israel, the rise of the Muslim Brothery under President Mohammed Morsi in Egypt, by far the most populous and pivotal Arab countries, has changed the balance in the region.
Hosni Mubarak, a secular despot who ruled Egypt for 30 years until his fall in 2011, had no time for Hamas.
On the contrary, the Brotherhood is a cousin of Hamas, and its leaders are more influenced by public opinion.
In future diplomacy, Hamas may emerge as a player who cannot be excluded even by Israel or America.
On the contrary, Israeli hard-line proponents will come to opposite conclusions.
In military terminology, Hamas has been put back into a crate.
Israel's Iron Dome anti-missile system has proved its price, and many Hamas rockets have been destroyed.
Israelis can sleep better - for a while.
In diplomatic terminology, America is as unwavering as ever. Many European countries have also blamed Hamas for starting the last round of violence.
And most importantly - Israel prospered, especially under the leadership of Benjamin Netanyahu, the prime minister, who mostly ignored the peace process.
Even if the rockets from Gaza have killed about 30 Israelis since 2004, there has been relatively calm in Israel from suicide bombers, partly thanks to a wall that extends into the West Bank, the main part of what a Palestinian state might someday, and protects Jewish settlements, which are still growing despite their illegality from international law.
Prime Minister Netanyahu, whose Likud partie merged with an even more radical group led by Avigdor Lieberman at the start of the January 22 election campaign, is in a good position.
Why pamper those treacherous Palestinians by giving them their own state?
If they really ruled the West Bank, they wouldn't have fired missiles like their compatriots did in Gaza?
It's better if we hold them behind that wall and hit them if they stick their heads out.
Possibly hard-line proponents will win, but the Arab Spring may change their calculations.
Although Islamists gaining power in Egypt and other countries do not like Israel very much, their main priority will be to deal with domestic problems.
Israel's defense spending is higher than the spending of all its four Arabian neighbors combined.
The start of a new war with the local superpower will hardly help the new Arab governments heal their economy.
That President Morsi has worked with President Barack Obama to achieve cease-fire is a good sign and could mark the beginning of something new.
Israelis, too, should look at the situation in the long run.
At a time when the rest of the Arab world is becoming more democratic, denying the rights of Palestinians to self-determine a barrel of gunpowder that must one day in areas occupied by Israel - just like a bus that exploded in Tel Aviv last week.
At present, repression is weakening democracy in the Jewish state, and demographics are exacerbating this situation as the Arabian population grows.
Bloody attacks against Gaza taking place every few years in order to push out Hamas will increasingly levy a diplomatic tax.
Both sides need incentives from the outside.
The answer is what is proclaimed by reasonable people on both sides, the majority of the outside world and this newspaper: the two states when Israel advances territory for security reasons.
In the short term, there is a bit of hope that the cease-fire will give pressures from the outside, at least a little more, to enforce this issue.
Egypt, which, along with Turkey and Qatar, has a better position than ever to persuade Hamas to accept the idea of a 1967-based Jewish state with the exchange of parts of the country and shared Jerusalem.
Arabs from the outside also have to push the match between Hamas and Fatah.
This would do more than an imminent offer of virtual state sovereignty at the U.N. for the creation of a Palestinian state.
President Obama also has his share on how to get Israel to the negotiating table.
In his first parliamentary term, he neglected to present his own peace plan.
Now, back in the White House, he seems equally reluctant in his entry into the trial.
It's woefully short-sighted.
America has a vitally important interest in a stable Middle East.
This means a peaceful settlement between Israel and the Palestinians.
Nasty cigarette packaging laws come into force in Australia
Unsightly green boxes, which are the same for all brands of tobacco, are depicted warnings for smokers and sick body parts.
Australia's laws on nasty packaging of tobacco and cigarettes, the first of its kind in the world, came into force, and replaced the logos and colors of brands with general olive-green boxes with hideous pictures of sick parts of the body and the depiction of children and newborns ill as a result of smoking their parents.
In addition to various health warnings and images, they are mandatory from Saturday the only difference between boxes of brand names that are printed in the same small font.
This is the strictest regime in the world regarding tobacco packaging.
Australia's federal government argues the goal is to deter young people from smoking by depriving this bad habit of its attractiveness.
It relies on studies showing that if people don't start smoking by the age of 26, there's a 99% chance that they won't start smoking.
"Even in very young children, you can see that they understand the message that tobacco companies are trying to sell to them together with their product," federal health secretary Tanya Plibersek says, citing studies that, for example, have shown that children associate the crown in the logo with the idea of being a princess.
While Australia has one of the lowest numbers of smokers in the world and changes will have little impact on the profits of multinationals, other countries are considering introducing similar moves.
The tobacco industry has lobbied harshly against the introduction of these laws.
Tobacco companies have argued that this law will increase trading on the black market, which will lead to cheaper and more affordable cigarettes.
"Thanks to this legislation, there will be serious unintentional consequences," says Scott McIntyre of British American Tobacco Australia.
Counterfeiters from China and Indonesia will bring far more of these products, which will be sold on Australian streets.
Others say the laws have increased their trades.
Sandra Ha of Zico Import Pty Ltd, a small-family firm, said the demand for silicone-cigarette packaging used to cover disgusting boxes, two months ago when British American Tobacco, Britain's Imperial Tobacco, Philip Morris and Japan Tobacco lost their appeals regarding laws to Australia's top-country trial, increased from near-zero sales.
Ha said Zico sold nearly 6,000 units to wholesale dealers and expects new inventories.
It's a good business for us.
As a possible hiccup, the experts cite the popularity of social media in that demographic group on which the plan focuses.
After a number of Australian laws banning television advertising, sponsoring sporting events, and demanding that most vendors hide cigarettes from a direct look, tobacco marketing has shifted online.
Australia has banned internet advertising by local companies and websites, but it cannot restrict foreign sites.
"If you're a marketing worker in the tobacco industry and you only have this little space left to promote your products, an online space is an insurmountable place you want to be in," says Becky Freeman, a public health researcher at the University of Sydney.
Ms Freeman has seen an increase in brand ratings on social networks such as YouTube, Twitter and Facebook, carried out by "average Honza."
Do we have to ask, is he just a private citizen who really loves Marlboro cigarettes and put in the job and created this video, or is there a marketing company involved?
British American Tobacco Australia argues that the industry has focused on how it will handle new regulations rather than marketing.
The industry has gone so far as to pay Ukraine, Honduras and the Dominican Republic to challenge new regulations - countries within the World Trade Organization declare that trade is unfairly curtailed, despite the fact that none of the countries are making significant business with Australia.
The SOO decision is expected by mid-2013.
Minister Plibersek says the government has held talks with other governments, which are considering similar packaging laws.
Canada was the first country to introduce mandatory warning photographs in 2001.
It has now spread to more than 40 countries including Brazil, Turkey and Ukraine.
Great Britain, New Zealand, South Africa and India are considering stricter laws.
Many smokers in Australia are resisting.
Pictures have no effect on me.
I'm just ignoring them.
"You're just pulling out a cigarette and shoving the box back," says Victor El Hage while buying a box with a photo of a tumor in his mouth.
Frankly, there's only one reason for me to quit smoking, and that's my little daughter.
James Yu, who owns King of the Pack in central Sydney, argues that, thanks to unified boxes, it's harder for him to level the shelves.
"It took me one hour to unload the cargo, now it takes me four hours," Yu said.
"The government should just forbid them completely, then we'd say, 'OK, it's over, we're going to shut down the shop,'" he said.
In a world of constant connectivity, it's not so bad to be bored
I unexpectedly spent five hours at the airport when our plane was in mechanical trouble and we had to wait for the next plane to arrive.
So I had plenty of time to think about boredom.
I won't lie to you.
Waiting half a day at the airport for the next flight is pretty annoying, despite scattering books, magazines and the iPhone (not to mention duty-free stores).
But some scientists and experts on children's development are increasingly coming with the praise of getting bored.
They say it's good for us (and our kids) to get bored sometimes.
It forces the brain to go into interesting directions, which can promote creativity.
And since most of us are constantly connected to a screen these days, we don't have a chance to enjoy boredom.
Should we support boredom?
Yes. Yes.
Oh, no.
But I'll come back to that.
First of all, like many people, I assumed that boredom is a relatively new phenomenon that has occurred with the arrival of more free time.
But as Peter Toohey, a professor of Greek and Roman history at Calgary University of Canada, who is the author of Boredom: A Lively History (Yale University Press, 2011).
"In fact, boredom has a long history," he said.
A Latin inscription related to boredom, dating from the first century AD, was found on walls in Pompeii.
And then it's also a question of how we're defining boredom.
The difficulty is that it has been defined and discussed in many different ways, said John D. Eastwood, professor of psychology at York University in Ontarium, Canada.
After seeing the research literature and showing the idea to a target group of about 100, Professor Eastwood and his colleagues defined boredom as an experience "I wanted to do something to satisfy me, but I wasn't able to do it."
Boredom differs from apathy by the fact that the person is not involved in anything, but she would like to be.
He says there's no urge to do anything about apathy.
He says the core of boredom is "disturbing the process of attention, coupled with a bad mood and a sense of slowly passing time."
Boredom can be like depression a lot.
But Professor Eastwood said that while they may have a relationship with each other, bored people tend to see the problem as an environment or the world, while people who are depressed see the problem in themselves.
Sometimes we think we're bored when we're just having trouble concentrating.
“The Unengaged Mind: Defining Boredom in Terms of Attention” was published in Perspectives on Psychological Science in September, Professor Eastwood and his colleagues pointed to an earlier experiment in which participants listened to the tape on which a person read an article from a journal.
Some of the groups heared a loud and unrelated television program in the next room, others heard it so quiet that they could barely notice it, while the third group couldn't hear it at all.
Those who heard the TV program quietly reported more boredom than the other two groups - having trouble concentrating, but they weren't sure why and attributed these difficulties to boredom.
When you're trying to focus on a difficult or eye-catching task, distraction can lead to boredom, Mark J. Fenske, a neuroscience professor at the University of Guelph in Ontarium and one of the study's authors said.
On the other hand, when you're doing something boring, "such as looking for the wrong components on a factory line, distracting music can help you not get bored."
In fact, he said, we now know that twisting and doodling, which is often viewed as a sign of boredom, can actually help with boredom fighting and keeping people physically attentive.
"The research shows that children who are allowed to waggle will learn more and retain more information than those who are forced to sit still," Professor Fenske said.
We've all experienced boredom sometimes - the delay of my flight, the roaring loudspeaker, a particularly lengthy movie.
But some individuals have a better chance of getting bored than others.
To help measure boredom, scientists in the 80's of the last century developed the "Boredom Proneness Scale."
The scale includes questions like, "Many of the things I have to do are repeated, and it's monotonous," and "I have so many interests that I don't have time to do them all."
Using these scales, the researchers found that boys tend to get bored more often than girls, said Stephone Vodanovich, professor of psychology at the University of West Florida, especially when it comes to the need to have more and different external stimulations.
Teenagers, however, are generally indifferent.
In 1991, Reed Larson, professor of human and social development at the University of Illinois, conducted an experiment in which he contacted nearly 400 teenagers and their parents seven to eight times a day.
It found that 32 percent of adolescents said they were bored at school and while doing homework, while 23 percent said they were bored when they weren't in school.
On the other hand, 3 percent of parents said they were bored.
Professor Larson said he didn't know whether the percentage expression of boredom would be higher or lower now, 21 years later.
But he said he knew that "adolescence is a peak time for boredom," which is largely due to the fact that children and youth aren't given much control over what they want to do.
So back to my original question: “Is boring good for you?”
As the research shows, sometimes not, because in its extreme form it can lead people to take absurd physical risks, play gambling, or abuse addictives as a way to alleviate it.
On the other hand, many philosophers and writers discussed the connection between boredom and creativity, said Professor Vodnovich, who studied the problem for more than two decades.
"Boroming is the way the brain tells you that you should be doing something else," said Gary Marcus, a professor of psychology at NYU.
But the brain doesn't always know the most appropriate thing you should do.
If you're bored and use that energy to play guitar and cooking, it'll make you happy.
However, if you watch TV, it will only make you happy for a moment, not for the longuest.
So if your child is bored and you give him an iPad, he won't be bored anymore, but he won't learn how to entertain himself or drive independently, Professor Fenske said.
And "this stand-alone proceedings are transferred from one situation to another," the professor said.
Your child doesn't just learn how to entertain themselves, but gets more self-control in other areas.
I don't think we really want to celebrate boredom.
But we shouldn't be too critical of her either.
Rather, our goal should be to feel comfortable when we are out of the reach of the constant throttling of activity and technology.
Professor Eastwood agreed.
"We accuse her of the need to bore herself even more, but boredom is a painful, restless desire to be associated with something meaningful," he said.
What people are actually looking for, he said, is a way to relax and enjoy their free time.
"In an environment in which we're constantly overstimulated," he said, "it's hard to find ways to entertain ourselves when the noise turns off."
There's no handbook in Colorado regarding the New Marijuana Act
Nineteen-year-old Anthony Orozco, a student at a senior vocational school and a footballer from south-eastern Colorado, is facing criminal charges for something that will soon be legal across the state - for possessing a few grams of marijuana and a pipe he used to smoke.
Mr. Orozco said that one September day he was riding with several friends in Lamar, on the plains not far from the border with Kassan when he was stopped.
After the police officer found marijuana in his car, Mr. Orozco was summoned for possession of drugs and equipment for drug use - minor offences, for which there is 100 USD for each fine - and he was given a court date.
"They treated us like criminals," Mr. Orozco said.
But he's a criminal?
In the uncertain weeks after the Colorado vote on the legalization of a small amount of marijuana for recreational uses, the response of hundreds of smaller drug cases depends on place rather than the law.
Hundreds of cases of misdemeanors for marijuana possession have already been lifted here and in Washington state, which approved similar measures.
The police department has stopped accusing adults at the age of 21 and older for holding a small amount that will be legally allowed after the law goes into effect in the coming weeks.
But plaintiffs in the more conservativous precincts of Colorado have said they will proceed with existing cases involving marijuana, and are still blaming people for possession.
At the same time, several cities from the suburbs of Denver towards the western mountains are voting to prevent the opening of new, state-licensed marijuana stores in their municipalities.
"This issue is evolving so fast that I don't know what's going to happen next," said Daniel J. Oates, a police chief in the city of Aurora, east of Denver.
Regulators in the state of Washington are scratching their heads.
And they're looking for instructions on how to set up a licensing system for growing, producing, distributing and selling - all by December 1, 2013.
They say that Colorado, for better or for worse, is ahead in front of most states in regulating marijuana, first for medical use, and now recreational.
"Colorado has a more regulated market, so it will be a good guide," said Brian E. Smith, spokesperson for the Washington State's licensing license.
But as Mr. Smith admitted, no place or system can do more than suggest what might work.
"There's no real precedent by which we can follow," he said.
The So-Called I-502 law takes effect on December 6, which also leaves an one-year vacuum during which there will not yet be a state licensing system, but the legal possession will.
And there are tricky mechanical issues that need to be resolved over the course of that time, for example, how to balance the state's "appropriate approach" to licensed mariajour with its ban on cannabis trade within 330 meters of schools, parks, playgrounds or children's centers.
"Nowhere will it be more difficult to place a licensed cannabis store than in urban areas, especially in the Seattle metropolitan area," said Ben Livingston, a spokesman for the Centre for Legal Cannabis, a recently established research group.
On November 21, Oates, from the city of Aurora, sent an e-mail to his officers informing them that the city prosecutor would no longer prosecute misdemeanors for possession of a small amount of marijuana for every citizen over the age of 21, and that the polices "with immediate effect" would stop recriminating people for these crimes.
Chief Oates said police would enforce city laws regulating medical marijuana growers and continuing to prosecute drug traffickers and dealers.
In Weld County in northern Colorado, District Attorney Ken Buck presents a stricter perspective.
After the vote, he declared that his office would continue to prosecute cases of marijuana possession, mostly as a way to pressure users to seek treatment.
He is currently facing charges of possessing 56 grams or less Marijuana of 119 people, although many are facing further charges.
"Our office is required to prosecute criminal offences that were offences at the time when they took place," Mr Buck said in a statement.
The reaction was also complicated in places such as Mesa's rural county, where voters rejected the marijuana initiative.
Police in Grand Junction, the district's largest city, no longer accuse adults of holding small quantities.
Pete Hautzinger, the district prosecutor, supports the decision, but has also decided not to dismiss all ongoing possession cases.
"I don't think I'm wasting my time continuing to enforce the law until it changes," he said.
Although 55 percent of Colorado's voters supported the measure, bringing recreational marijuana into the arms of the government and the legal system will never be easy.
And contradictory reactions across the state have revealed a deep ambivalence among local officials with regard to this large state green experiment.
It's a "cultural barrier" for district prosecutors," said Sean McAllister, a Denver lawyer who represents defendants on marijuana possession, and a local spokesperson for the National Marijuarius-Reforming Organization.
"They've spent so much of their lives prosecuting people that they still can't actually accept that it's legal," he said.
As the first states that treat a small amount of marijuana as alcohol, Colorado and Washington are well-positioned to become national testing cases to legalize the drug.
Advocates and state authorities are planning new boundaries for legalized sales, and at the same time anxiously await instructions from the federal government, which still plans to regard the sale and cultivation of marijuana as federal crimes.
Advocates for the legalization of marijuana are hoping the Justice Department will submit.
Despite a number of arrests of medical marijuana vendors and patients who use it that have attracted attention, mostly the federal government has left stores with medical marijuana in Colorado, Washington, and 16 other states in operation.
While anti-drug agents are not likely to knock the door out to intercept a small bag with the drug, it is likely to defy allowing state-regulated stores with recreational marijuana allowed by new laws, said Kevin A. Sabet, a former adviser on President Obama's anti-drug drug policy area.
Several Colorado cities are not waiting for the procedure of the federal authorities.
Even before the election day, some local governments had approved moratoriums on any new marijuana stores, although it will still be about a year before any store could open.
Last week, Montrose's city in the west of the state proposed a six-month ban and is likely to come into effect next week.
"We don't want to find ourselves in a situation where we're going to issue someone's license and then we're going to have a big federal issue out of it," Bob Nicholson, a city council member, said.
Our municipality voted against this amendment.
We are looking at what the municipality voted for, versus what the state voted for.
There really are a lot of questions.
Petronella Wyatt: 'I've been forced to leave Oxford because I'm conservative.'
Today's university students are not only attacked for their views.
I don't remember ever dreaming of studying at the University of Oxford.
Both my father and my older brother studied at a university I thought was the world's largest seat of knowledge, a modern wrapped Greek symposium motivating two pillars of civilization, free thought and tolerance.
And so far in the late 80's of the twentieth century until two weeks after joining Worcester College, where I wanted to study history, I packed my suitcases and rushed into the first scandal of my life.
My father collapsed and cried.
My friends were confused.
The Evening Standard newspaper claimed that I gave up because I objected to loving the students in the next room.
Writer A. N. Wilson cheerfully declared that I left because I was forced to drink from battened mugs.
The truth was less comical.
I've escaped.
Yes, I escaped because I was the object of systematic bullying and intimidation.
It's not because of my somewhat bizarre name or the fact that I've come from private school.
I was persecuted for only one reason, and in this cradle of supposed enlightenment it was both bigoted and barbaric: my father, the late Woodrow Wyatt, was a high-ranking adviser to Margaret Thatcher, and I a supporter of the Conservative Party.
You can ask why I'm bringing it up just now.
Simply because the latest reports suggest that a new generation of students to the right of the center is suffering from similar persecution.
The institutionalized and growing hatred of conservative students at Oxford is so great that last week a group of these students demanded the same protection of equal rights as gays, people with disabilities and ethnic minorities.
Conservative members of the Younger-Year (JCR) association at Corpus Christi College argue that because of their political views, they are "often actively isolated, subjected to personal attacks and made clear that they are not welcome."
They are calling for the creation of a position in the University Equal Opportunities Committee to ensure that they are free to communicate their views.
Their situation was also not helped by the recent BBC Two documentary programme Wonderland: Young, Bright and on the Right, which portrayed the Conservatives as weirdos and neo-Nazis.
The show featured graduate Joe Cooke, a former president of the Oxford University Conservative Association (OUCA), who rode a Rolls-Royce, was dressed in a silver suit and had a cane with a silver head.
At other colleges, conservative students say they are treated as "scapegoats" due to the introduction of higher tuition fees.
Twenty-year-old Luke Black, vice president of the Nottingham University Conservative Association (Nottingham University), told Sunday's newspaper that "left-wing influence on universities is growing.
“People think we’re like the Bullingdon Club without meeting us at all.”
Twenty-one-year-old Corpus history student Christi Samuel Roberts, who is behind the movement for greater protection, says such a climate is "unpleasant," while nineteen-year-old OUCA president-elected Stephanie Cherill says there has been a deterioration in JCR's attitude towards the people to the right from the center.
"This poses a threat to an atmosphere of intellectual discussion, as well as to the well-beings of members," he says.
During my first few weeks in Oxford, I was a minority about one member.
In September 1986, I joined an 18-year-old and crippling way shy.
Hatred of the Conservative Party was feverishest.
A year earlier, the university had voted to refuse to award an honorary degree to former student Margaret Thatcher due to cuts in higher education funding.
From the atmosphere, even Stalinists would tremble with concern.
During the first few days of the introductory week, when new students meet with each other and with the professors, I got a taste of the wormwood that was supposed to come.
I found out that not only do professors silently approve the taunting of conservative pupils, but they are also happy to participate in it.
A policy of mining strike, privatisation and resistance of the government against sanctions against apartheid in South Africa has been broughed into wood-panelled auditoriums.
My first lecture was about translating a French text from the 18th century into English, and I wasn’t ready for what followed.
"Miss Wyatt," said Professor Harry Pitt (now the deceased), "translate the first paragraph please."
I've been quite stiff.
Professor Pitt, a little man with a face like cake dough, was very biliary.
"Thatchers refuse to learn French, or are they simply stupid?" he asked.
Other students giggled.
Tears came into my eyes.
"I suggest you take some basic French lessons in your free time, unless you're too busy with social affairs," Professor Pitt growled.
I went back to my room.
At dinner at school, I sat alone when I felt a slight tapping on my shoulder.
He was a second-year student named James, who introduced himself as an OUCA member.
"I know who you are," he said kindly.
Unfortunately, that's right.
Anyone suspected of conservatism is targeted.
It's bad enough for me, but they know that your father's close to Margaret Thatcher, so it'll be even worse for you.
Most Conservative freshmen pretend to be Labour.
Later in a local pub, I cowardly tried to pretend.
I insisted that I didn't agree with everything Thatcher said.
This trick turned out to be unsuccessful.
A first-year student in philosophy, politics and economics who (what irony) went to Eton said, "You're the daughter of a fascist pig."
You're infected.
Other students noted with him.
I've been twisted, dirty.
"How do conservatives love each other?" one asked.
They're beating each other, aren't they?
I felt the way homosexuals must have felt before the liberal legislation of the 1960s.
Could I ever lead a normal life in Oxford?
Would I be forced to meet like-minded people only after dark?
Would I have to turn into Labour and suppress my natural tendencies?
Three years before me unfolded as a purgatory of ignoration and isolation.
The only openly conservative professor was Norman Stone, professor of modern history who resided at my faculty.
He was hated not only for being conservative, but also for being Margaret Thatcher's adviser on foreign policy and one of those who wrote her speeches.
He almost wasn't there.
He hated the place because of his provinciality and pettiness, and for his adherence to a Marxist-deterministic view of history.
In 1997, he took the position of professor at the University of Bilkent in Ankara, Turkey.
"You won't be happy here," he told me.
I started commuting from Oxford to my parents' house in London, seeking refuge with my unbiased metropolitan friends and family.
I told my father that I hate Oxford and why.
He couldn't believe it.
During his studies at Oxford in the 1940s, all political views were accepted.
"But it's the best place in the world," he said pathetically.
They wouldn't do that, not in the city's dreaming moods.
Even my communist friends have always had perfect manners.
His wet eyes gloomed.
Treat it again.
I'm sure it's just a joke.
It'd break my heart if you'd left.
Exhausted by frequent trips to London, my emotional resilience deteriorated.
My friend, also a Conservative Party supporter, succumbed to the pressure and renounced his conviction.
The following week, in the course of teaching, when another professor of history in complete seriousness declared that I was an "enemy of the people," I decided to do the same.
Even as I blushed with shame inside, I confessed that "my parents brainwashed me" and called them "old fools."
Relief was only short-lived.
My father hammered the nail into the coffin of my Oxford career.
At that time, he wrote two columns each week to Murdoch's newspaper.
My door was locked.
I crouched inside, and after five minutes, my pursuers gave up.
When they left, I packed my suitcase and caught the first train to London.
I've never come back.
You can call me a sniffy weak.
But no 18-year-old girl or boy should be the subject of such intimidation and venom in an educational institution.
It is even more tragic, however, that it was Oxford, from which not only 14 Conservative prime ministers emerged, but which to this day also hides behind an undeserved reputation of a place with equality and freedom of thought.
"Valentino prefers elegance rather than fame"
On the occasion of the "Valentino: master of fashionmaking," launched this week in London, ABC spoke with Naty Abascal, Fiona Thyssen-Bornemisz and other famous customers of an Italian designer.
Somerset House, the ancient residence of Isabella I of England, is the only place in Britain's capital worthy to host Valentino Garavani's exhibition.
The designer confirmed this at the launch of the celebratory retrospective "Valentino: master of fashion creation", which represents more than 130 models of top fashion created by his studio in the last 50 years.
"I adore this castle," he says with his unmistakable Italian accent.
This exhibition is the conclusion of a story in which "il signore" Garavani is the only protagonist, but it could not have been written without his revered clients.
Valentino has always felt enchanted by the precious and distant world of aristocracy.
The first hall of the exhibition, which runs until March 3, is brimming with private correspondences and photographs with the signatures of the most important nobles, from Princess Salima Aga Khan to Greek Princess Maria-Chantal and Margaret from England to Lord Snowdon.
Valentino exhibits these personal memories as trophies of his social ascension: from a simple tailor from the city of Voghera in the north of Italy to the idol of the international "bohemian cream."
There's nothing bad to love the royal family.
"At least they don't tire your starling of a cigarette into a great carpet, like some gorgeous pop-music celebrities do," says Baroness Fiona Thyssen-Bornemisz.
In the 60's and 70's we both lived in the Alps and we were good friends.
Valentino is an impressive host and entertains guests abundantly and with elegance.
"We were all very pleased when we were invited to his cabin in Gstaad," explains "Heini's" Thyssen's ex-wife and a close-known friend of forgotten beauties like Marella Agnelli or Eugenie Niarchos.
"Valentino always preferred elegance rather than fame.
He’s still a star.”
Valeria Mazz performs Valentina
Argentine model Valeria Mazza doesn't forget the charisma of the designer either.
"It's been many years since we went to dinner with him after one parade in Piazza di Spagna in Rome.
There were twenty of us, and Sharon Stone and John Kennedy Jr. were among us.
In every trifle of his apartment, decorations, food, music, in everything was reflected and his spirit was smelled.
Everyone invited was able to feel important and beloved thanks to him," recalls the top model, who started working with him during Paris Peak Fashion Week in 1995.
"It will never go out of fashion because his designs are works of art," he concludes.
Nobility parade
Gravani's life is not a story of obsessions, but of truly recurring love.
He loves people with good manners and better titles and they love him.
One of Somerset House's galleries has been rebuilt into a distinctive seventy-metre-long passage that offers a role-exchancing: the visitor turns into a model and marches through a passage to observe the "occupies" of dreams worn by Valentino's masterpieces as dresses chosen by Jackie Kennedy for her wedding to Aristotle Onassis, a costume worn by Monica Vitti in "Night", or a coat of wool
Names such as Sibilla of Luxembourg, Gloria of Thurn and Taxis, Mette-Marit of Norway, Rosario of Bulgaria and Sophie of Habburk shine in the mannequin audience.
Naty Abascalova and designer in 2006
Many of these clients say that Valentino's first love, "can't be forgotten".
"I remember it perfectly.
They were pants, shirts, vest "gillette" and jacket from the autumn-winter collection from 1971-1972.
It was a gift he made for me," says Naty Abascalová, one of the designer's muses.
"I give him priority over others for his femininity and great love for women because it underscores our beauty," adds the former Duchess of Feria.
"I really like the colors he uses, brighten up a lot and give a "spar" to the face.
The proportions are perfect.”
Even the princess and fashion icon Patricia della Giovampaola d'Arenberg can't forget how she put on Valentina for the first time.
When she was growing up and living in Italy, she longed to be of age and have the opportunity to wear one of his evening clothes...
In the late 90's, it was in the late 90's.
"I bought my first dress from Valentino for the occasion of the celebration at the castle of my cousin, Prince Edouard de Ligne.
It was a red dress that had a skirmish with ruffles, a clapped bodicle and a top without straps.
It was a dream that came true," says Rodrigue d'Arenberg's widow.
"Valentino is laxious towards fashion, his passion is timeless," explains an Italian aristocrat whose life unfolds between Paris, New York and Buenos Aires.
Princess d'Arenberg has stored the designer's festive dress "very carefully... because dresses aren't just a dress, it's also a collection of memories in them".
"King" of fashion
The highlight of the exhibition in Somerset House is Marie-Chantal Miller's wedding dress from her wedding to Pavel of Greece in 1995.
It took four months to create this silk ivory dress with sewn-in pearls, twelve types of different laces and a siding four-and-a-half-meter-long work and 25 "girls" (as the designer calls his seamstress).
According to journalist Suzy Menkes, the greatest authority of the specialized press, this dress represents the landmark of peak fashion of the late 20th-century, "the return of clients from high society."
Valentino dazzled over the years by the elitist "savoir-être" is now his best example.
Garavani as Cavaliere di Gran Croce (the highest honour in Italy), Cavaliere del Lavoro, Commandeur de L'Ordre des Arts et des Lettres and decorated with the Order of the Legion of Honour accumulates as many honors as any of his clients' spouses.
"My attention has always been attracted by his selection, his calmness, his well-groomed and his perfect appearance," Princess d'Arenberg acknowledges.
"Last time I saw him was at a gala dinner a month ago at the Orsay Museum.
He was at the table with Countess Jacqueline de Ribes, my great girlfriend.
He was impeccable, time didn't leave a mark on him."
When the princess says it...
The toughest craft in the world: sulphur carriers in the Kawah Ijen volcano
For four euros, carriers in the Indonesian Volcano are gambling with their lives and health as they endure 70 pounds of sulphur down steep stone trails.
There are people for whom their work is hell, and there are those who literally work in hell.
This is the case of Anta Wijayi, one of the 400 miners who earn a living by conquering sulphur from the Kawah Ijen volcano in the east of the Indonesian island of Java.
Because of this, he has to grow every day to the bottom of the crater, where sulphur gases escape from the interior of the Earth and, when in contact with the air, passes into a solid state.
After cutting large blocks of sulfur, which together weigh up to 70 kilos, it carries them on the shoulders in two bamboo baskets along steep stone trails.
To the top of the volcano, which towers 2 386 meters, it is only 250 meters, but it takes exhausted carriers over 40 minutes to climb the turtle pace upwards, as they maintain balance and thoroughly deliberate their footsteps so that they don't slip and crash into the abyss.
They know that any stumbling could cost them their lives, as happened to one French tourist who collapsed years ago from Kawah Ijen rocks.
Miners at Kawah Ijen earn five cents for each kilo of sulphur they mine
When they're up, they make their way around tourists photographing them as if they were monkeys in a circus, and weariously carry away the heavy baskets to the weight that the mining company placed a little lower, at an altitude of 1850 metres.
It is the company PT Candi Ngrimbi, which has benefited in the volcano since 1960 and has never paid its workers more than 662 Indonesian rupees (five cents) per pound.
It then sells it for 10 000 rupees (83 cents) to the petrochemical industry, because this mineral has its place in everyday life and is used for the production of matches, fun pyrotechnics, cosmetics, dynamite and even sugar bleaching.
"Since it's usually 70 kilos, we earn about 46,000 rupees (3.8 euros) for each trip," explains Anto, who usually makes three trips a day.
One trip takes him three hours and at the end he is destroyed, but he can earn 138 000 rupees (11.5 euros) per day.
While this appears to be a negligible amount for such an inhumane effort, it's three times what he'd make in the field.
"Daily wages of miners here are very high. For example, 15 000 rupees (1.2 euros) per day are paid for coffee collection, and the salary for a fortnight is two million rupees (167 euros)," explains the carrier, who previously worked on the tourist island of Bali as a bricklayer.
There was his salary of 75,000 rupees (6.2 euros) per day, and the work was not as difficult, but Anto returned with his family to Banyuwangi, a village near the volcano, for a serious reason that is as persuasive in Indonesia as sulfur: "I married a young girl from Bali who is Hindu, and I took her to Java to convert to Islam."
Anto's got asthma, he's having trouble breathing, he's still coughing, and his eyes are irritated with toxic gases
At the age of 27, Anto risks his life three times a day in the Kawah Ijen volcano, whose sulfur has already begun to take its toll on him, despite having her face protected by a special mask and glasses.
He's got asthma, he's having trouble breathing, he's still coughing, and his eyes are irritated by toxic gases that escape from the volcano.
That's the price they have to pay to achieve their dream.
“I’m going to work for two more years because I want to open a shop or study Spanish or French,” he said.
This sympathetic and intelligent young man affected by fate could be a tourist guide, waiter or receptionist at the hotel, and instead does the work of a carrier.
She shares a dirty wooden hut with other carriers and gets up every day at two o'clock in the morning, as sulfur at night doesn't stop springing up, and its characteristic yellow color turns blue and glows dark.
Despite the surrounding darkness, Anto grows into the crater and illuminates his path with a small lamp, which he has attached to his helmet and bought it with his own money.
About 400 carriers carry on the shoulders baskets with sulphur from the bottom of the crater
Although the company has high revenues, it has not mechanized sulphur mining due to cost savings, nor does it provide carriers working on their own and with such effort, no equipment.
In fact, they don't even get a share of the 30 000 rupees (2.5 euros) from the surcharge for photography, which, along with a ticket for 15 000 rupees (1.2 euros), is chosen by natural park keepers from tourists who come to take pictures of the volcano and its human mules.
"This work is for cattle, and not for people," argues Madrusin, a sturdy 42-year-old carrier who's been working at Kawah Ijen for three decades, since leaving schools.
He manages to lift up to 110 pounds and assures us that he can work "as long as possible" because he needs money to educate his three sons between the ages of 18 and 10.
I won't retire, I'll die here because the volcano has been my whole life
Although sulphur burns the throat and stings in the eyes, when the wind turns unexpectedly and traps miners in dense vapors coming out of the volcano, miners claim that none of them complain that they suffer from serious illnesses... however, this is evident from their frequent respiratory problems, arthrosis, knee pain and wounds on the shoulders caused by the weight of the baskets.
Unainik, who balances his basket on his back, at the age of 53, can load only 50 pounds.
Every day he and his colleagues mine 15 tons of sulphur in the volcano, which three trucks then take down a steep path between the thickets to a warehouse in Tamansari, 18 kilometers away.
“I’m not going to retire, I’m going to die here because the volcano has been my whole life,” Unainik declares, opening his mouth wide, with several teeth missing.
Of his five sons, the eldest, thirty-year-old, also earns by mining sulfur.
Time passes, but the constant misery of one of the world's heaviest crafts persists from generation to generation - crafts performed by human mules in Kawah Iljen.
Singapore is looking for babies to save the economy
Singaporeans blame the fact that they don't have children, careers, stress and the cost of livelihoods and education.
"The population of Singapore needs to grow."
I'm married and I'm a patriot, you're my wife and you're a patriot. Let's do our civic duty and beget a new life!
It may seem unbelievable that this slogan is part of an ad for menthol candies, but still - or perhaps that's why - the video expanded massively to YouTube earlier this year in Singapore.
These sentences are a part of the rap, which uses local allusions such as "putting a bun in the oven" as a mockery of the birth rate in Singapore.
The advertising agency BBH, which created the video, hopes that with this ad they will be able to draw attention to the problem in a funny way.
The agency's artistic director, Douglas Hamilton, says he wanted to use the influence of music to enable people to fulfill "their civic duty."
"It's exclusively an internet issue, so it must have been funny and funny.
It's the country's biggest problem.
We're the worst in the world to reproduce our population, so we felt like it was a theme that we had to touch.
We knew that the government was attempting a lot of things like expanding perfumes with pheromones or organising dating parties.
Many of these ideas could have been creative, but not entirely functional.
So we thought, why not do the most creative thing possible, such as the composition of rap, to solve this problem.”
1.2 children
Whoever doesn't take it lightly is the Singaporean government.
Every year, they spend USD ₸ US$ ₙ encouraging citizens to have more children.
The government's package for marriage and the family provides up to US$15,000 per child, extends maternity leave and allocates financial benefits.
However, all these measures had little effect.
Singapore is a wealthy city state with advanced technology in southeastern Asia, also known for the conservatism of its leaders and strict social controls.
The birth rate in Singapore with regard to the stratification of the population is currently 1.2 children per woman.
The last time the birth rate exceeded 2, which is considered the recovery rate, in 1976.
Why don’t Singaporeans have more children?
Tan Wei Ming, director of the Department of Marriage and Family of the National Population, says it's because of "better education" and "a wider range of career opportunities."
"People thus have a wider range of options at their disposal when it comes to life's goals and priorities, it's not just a wedding and a family," he explains.
These changes in social norms have contributed to an increase in the number of unmarried people and the postponement of marriages and childbreaking. The result is a decrease in the birth rate in Singapore.
While there have been efforts within immigration policy to drastically increase the influx of immigrants as a measure against a decline in the population, among locals it has provoked displeasure.
On some of Singapore's websites, undisguised xenophobia can be seen against many new immigrants, especially Chinese, who are criticised for maintaining low salaries and making little effort to integrate.
An increase in immigration is also seen as one of the reasons why last year's ruling Singapore party recorded its worst election result since independence began.
After the election, there was an effort to solve the problem using quotas and higher fees for foreign workers.
Unexpected Consequences
While the decline in the birth rate has well-known effects on national economic growth, tax revenues, health spending and immigration policies, there have also been some unexpected consequences for Singapore.
The government is trying not to build so many small houses.
For example, it began to influence the real estate business.
The Office of Urban Development has begun regulating the number of small apartments known as "shoe boxes" that can be built in certain urban zones.
These apartments have an area of 46 square meters and have had great success in marketability.
However, there is concern that these apartments can promote a bachelor's lifestyle and discourage developers from building large single-family homes.
But Lim Yew Soon, chief executive of developer EL Developers, argues that their "shoe boxes" are selling much faster than larger homes.
"They're more popular by selling for days to weeks faster than homes with a larger expansion.
That's why they're much better for our cash flow.'
However, he admits that the new standards set clearer rules for developers who were previously limited when they wanted to offer too many small houses in one project.
Too stressed
Singapore is a city country.
Although these new rules could aim to increase the birth rate of the nation, a conversation with Singaporeans working in a financial district downtown suggests that they will not have much effect.
"People are still stressed, houses are expensive, just like education, that's why a lot of people put it off," says a young official.
"Let the others have children.
But it's important for me to rely on my own money and time," says another, about twenty-year-old, youngster.
Both men and women mention careers, stress and the cost of livelihoods and education as reasons that prevent them from having children.
So no matter how much the government tries to force citizens to have children, it's Singaporeans who have the final say about starting a family anyway.
What's private offline is both private and online
Privacy.
According to a dictionary published by the Royal Spanish Academy, it is something that has a personal nature or "an area of private life that everyone has the right to protect against any disruption".
What's privacy for a person under the age of 16?
How does he apply this definition in his daily life and on social media?
Does he understand the dangers he is exposed to when he publishes things on the Internet that he would certainly not say to anyone during normal communication?
Journalists from elPeriódico interviewed five boys and girls between the ages of 10 and 15 who are frequent internet users.
In four cases, they considered the concept of privacy as "something completely inherent" on a personal level and as a "user name and password" when it came to social networks.
"My biggest secrecy I wouldn't disclose," says ten-year-old Jorge, in an effort to explain the importance of privacy in places such as Facebook, Twitter, Hotmail and Windows Live Messenger, where he's had accounts for two years.
"These are very secret secrets, my mum may know about them, but not the whole world," she argues.
"On FB, I'm posting nice photos or photos of us playing.
I'm also talking to friends there.
I wouldn't share a photo that wasn't mine, or a photo that would ridicule anyone," she says.
This boy realizes that it is wrong to publish obscene photos, photos of naked persons or photos of crimes, or to write humiliating or crude comments.
Jorge says he knows all the 35 friends he has on FB and his nine supporters on Twitter.
Most are relatives.
Among them is a mother who has the name and password of one of the accounts.
“I opened a Twitter account so I could express my opinions and write interesting posts.
I don't know if someone will answer me, I'm just putting them there," he adds.
"Social networks are entertaining, I can talk to relatives who are far or quick to communicate with friends," he says.
Without hesitation, he says he would never accept a request from an unknown person.
He wouldn't even care if someone recommended someone unknown.
Fourteen-year-old Jose's case is different.
This teenager's own accounts on Hotmail, Facebook, My Space and Ask, and for the latter network, admits that he doesn't know 20 people from his list of friends.
"I'm not afraid of it because we have something in common, like music," he says.
According to this boy, none of them led him to anything, he didn't even ask for an address or phone number.
"If they urged me or demanded it from me, I would simply remove them from my account," he admits.
Jose signed up on Ask after reading the recommendation on Twitter.
This boy is no foreign experience with what is called cyberbullying (cyberbullying).
"They blackmailed a well-known friend on social media.
They threatened him and demanded money.
He's never learned who it was," he confirms.
According to Jose, the victim did not opt out of his account.
"She's just activated the private regime."
It then explains a series of steps on how to set up an account securely.
Unlike Jorge, this boy would post photos of acquaintances in nasty or awkward situations.
"Of course I'll do it if someone bothered me or I just wanted to.
Even though I know it's cyberbullying," he argues.
Key Questions
Marielos Porrasová, an English teacher and a graduate in Education and Teaching, believes that children and adolescents need to understand that the main goal of these networks is to inform.
“The internet originated as a means of searching for information, but when social networks appeared, the rules of the game changed.”
Marrielos Porras notes that an academic worker Marc Prensky, a graduate of pedagogy at Yale University and the author of the article Digital Natives and Digital Immigrators, introduced these concepts to explain this phenomenon.
"Digital natives are children and young people who were born at the time of this technology.
“We are digital immigrants who have to teach them, and we’re still learning for ourselves.”
He explains that it is a complex topic, "because we ask them to recognize what is appropriate to spread, publish and say, and, on the contrary, not, at an age when they are not yet ripe for it."
Also, to be - in terms of the surroundings - prudent, at a time when what they most desire is to be popular, to have thousands of friends and not to think about the consequences," he adds.
According to this specialist, the most effective way to show children and adolescents what privacy is, asking questions that make them think.
"Telling them not to do something doesn't work," he adds.
Furthermore, Marrielos Porras lists some of the possibilities: There are things you wouldn't tell a stranger, so why are you doing this on the Internet?
Or: Would you like it if someone posted your photo the way you posted to a friend?
Do you know what other people publish about you?
If you're tagging the people in the photos from the celebrations, did you ask these people for permission?
And one more: Does everyone always need to know what you're doing?
The next point is for them to realize that they have to behave in the same way in the environment of the Internet as they do.
They're the same rules.
"Off-the-internet, one is guided by respect, morality and other principles, and it should be the same on social media," he argues.
Surveillance
Estuardo Guardia, a university professor, first-grade teacher and educational counselor explains that it is imperative that children's parents carefully read the rules of social networks.
“If they understand every insidiousness, they have a good basis to talk to their kids about the consequences that setting up an account on the Internet can have.
For example, when it comes to age or what is allowed to share or publish," he says.
Guardia is of the opinion that it is crucial to remind children of the traditional rule "don't talk to strangers."
The summit of South America is over, and the Lima statement has not been released
The Sixth Presidential Summit of the Union of South American Nations (Unasur) held in Peru ended today, without issuing a Statement from Lima, which was announced and which was reportedly signed by the seven presidents present.
The Efe news agency has repeatedly tried to gain access to a document signed at the sixth meeting of Unasur's heads of state and government, but representatives of the Foreign Ministry as well as the presidential office, who at the outset stipulated that it would publish the document after the end of the summit, said it would later be published on the Peruvian government's website.
They went on to say that the content was published by Peruvian President Ollanta Humala in several short press releases.
Journalists had limited access to information throughout the summit.
During the meeting, a video from the presidential assembly was transmitted in the press room without sound with the message "confidential session, sound transmission prevented."
A small amount of information available to reporters was provided by the spokespersons of some governments, who were present, but not by a Peruvian spokesperson.
The only document published during the negotiations was a list of participating presidents, provoking the displeasure of hundreds of journalists from various national and international media who demanded more information.
Later, Peru's presidential office circulated an e-mail to the media with a "final statement" from the summit, but this is just a statement from President Humala, and not an official document that concluded the summit.
Last October, Peru hosted the third summit of South American and Arab countries (América del Sur-Países Árabes, ASPA), and even on this occasion, the originally promised Lima Declaration was not published, even though it was zealously demanded by the press.
On ASPA's official website, it can be found that the document was published last Tuesday.
In both international meetings, Peruvian authorities sought to provide broadcasts for all journalists, but to completely prevent the flow of information.
Among other things, the summit ended with a joint commitment by Chile and Peru to adopt the judgment of the Hague Court of Justice, which resolves a dispute over the border between the two countries.
President Ollanta Humala and the president of Chile Sebastián Piñera met during a regional meeting and confirmed that they will respect the decision of the International Court of Justice in The Hague, which on Monday will begin hearing both sides' advocacy of a dispute launched by Lima against Santiago.
"We will submit and carry out a judgment that will resolve the dispute that we are hearing at this international court today," President Humala, along with his Chilean counterpart, said.
"Chile was, is and will be a country that respects international law, peaceful resolution of disputes, conventions and international courts," Piñera added, greeting President Humal with a handshake at the flags of both countries.
Confirmation by the two presidents to submit to the decision of the International Court of Justice was made public after Colombia adopted the Bogota Pact this week, expressing submission to the judgments of an international court that ruled on the naval border with Nicaragua, and Colombia considered this decision to be completely wrong.
The summit was held without the participation of the presidents of Brazil (Dilma Rouseff), Venezuela (Hugo Chavez), Bolivia (Evo Morales) and Argentina (Cristina Kirchner).
Paraguay was expelled from Unasur in 2011, following the dismissal of then-President Fernando Lugo and did not attend the meeting.
Hosting President Ollanta Humala was tasked with opening morning speeches and ending the summit in Lima shortly after noon.
The president read a final document informing that 16 agreements had been adopted and activity had been planned on 31 projects among South American countries, with an investment of $17bn.
In addition to the agreements adopted, UNASUR member countries will take "important steps towards a South American citizenship target for which residence agreements are being expanded."
He informed that actions to improve "collaboration in the fight against civial insecurity and international organised crime, to improve the availability of medicines and cheap internet in all corners of South America, and joint and effective measures against the risks of natural disasters."
When Europe is in crisis, "economic consolidation (of Latin America) should not have a victorious essence, but should serve to expand our productivity and show our peoples a better future," Humala added.
"We have decided to prioritize a group of 31 symbolic projects that will improve the transport links of the various places of South America, especially rural and border areas... so that our countries are better connected and create new economic ties," the Peruvian president said in a reading speech.
He mentioned that five of these projects relate to Peru and the transverse axis of its territory from the coast to Brazil, and the other two relate to a better connection with Ecuador, but did not give more details.
The final document also affected the political situation in Paraguay.
"We hope that the electoral process in this country will be used to return to the Union of South American Nations", from which it is currently expelled.
Another of the issues highlighted by the summit was the need for Latin America to remain a prosperous and peaceful area that is whole and has good neighborly relations.
In that sense, Colombian President Juan Manuel Santos said ahead of his participation in a regional meeting that he hoped to meet his Nicaraguan counterpart Daniel Ortega in Mexico on Saturday to respect the International Court of Justice's ruling on a dispute over the maritime border, which is disputed by Bogota.
"It's possible I'll meet President Daniel Ortega tomorrow (Saturday)," Santos said.
"We will discuss all the solutions, none are excluded, nor a treaty with Nicaragua that would require negotiations with this country," he stressed.
"I hope to be able to tell President Ortega to lead the negotiations in the most civilized and thoughtful form possible," Santos added.
Santos and Ortega will meet on Saturday in Mexico, where they are scheduled to take part in the inauguration of President Enrique Peñi Nieta.
As part of the summit, the EU's defence ministers met tentatively to approve the 2013 Action Plan, which seeks to strengthen dialogue and a consensus on defence.
UNASUR members include Argentina, Bolivia, Colombia, Ecuador, Peru, Brazil, Uruguay, Venezuela, Chile, Guyana, Suriname and Paraguay, although the latter country is currently excluded.
Peru is the interim chairman of this regional bloc.
Rafael Correa: South America must learn integration from Europe
Ecuador's President Rafael Correa announced today that the creation of common citizenship is a goal in which "South America, in this case, really must learn from Europe."
Correa, who participated in Lima's sixth presidential summit of the South American Union of Nations (Unasur), said on state-run TV Peru that Europeans were "killed in World War 2" and in other conflicts, "but now there is practically one homeland."
In this sense, he defended the project of creating South American citizenship, which Unasur member countries support.
"It is necessary to achieve the free movement of citizens and workers within any South American country that is already taking place among members of the Andean community, but there are still backward areas that want to bring us back to the past."
Among other things, the president of Ecuador has said that he agrees to rearrange the Organization of American States (OAS) on the condition that the influence of Anglo-Saxon states is diminished and those who have signed the San Jose Human Rights Pact will be taken into account.
"Those who are in the lead never commit to anything, and we South Americans, on the other hand, sign everything.
It is incomprehensible that the Inter-American Human Rights Commission is funded in Washington by the United States," he said, alluding to political asympties provided by Ecuador to WikiLeaks founder Julian Assange.
President Correa confirmed that he did not regret this decision because he did not betray his principles and respected his "deep democratic values and human rights."
“At that time there was a reasonable suspicion that Assange could be extradited to a third country, regardless of the ongoing trial.”
In addition, he criticised the Swedish judiciary, who asked him to undergo interrogation on Swedish territory over an alleged sexual offence, while "Sweden's own legislation allows videoconference calls to be interrogated, which could take place from the Ecuadorian embassy in London."
Correa said there was a risk that Assange's physical and mental health would deteriorate.
"I haven't spoken to him since he was at our embassy, but the ambassador informed me that she was having minor pulmonary problems, but nothing serious," the Ecuadorian head of state said.
"But there is a risk that his physical and mental state will deteriorate, because he is confined in a small space without the possibility of moving in the fresh air.
This would affect everyone's health," he added.
President Correa noted that the resolution of the asylum granted to Assange since June at the Ecuadorian embassy in London, on the basis of a permit that allows him to travel to Ecuador, is in the hands of the UK, Sweden and the European courts, stressing that talks have taken place with London in order to resolve WikiLeaks' imprisonmence.
"We don't negotiate about human rights, we don't use that word in this case, but there have been ongoing conversations.
The solution to this problem is in the hands of Great Britain, Sweden and the European courts, because Assange's lawyer Baltazar Garzón is conducting a series of proceedings in various European instances," he mentioned.
He stated that "if the UK issues a pass tomorrow, it will all be over."
And if Mr Assange's Sweden is interviewed at the Ecuadorian embassy in London or via Skype, as the local legislation allows without a problem, and as has already been done in other cases, this issue will be brought to an end tomorrow."
Correa took advantage of this situation to present himself as a defender of press freedom, clarifying that he certainly did not tolerate "under-averagence, bad intentions and a lie that undermine freedom of expression."
The greatest enemies of the free press are not us, mean and degenerate politicians, but bad journalists in the service of profits, coercions and blackmailing," he said.
From this point of view, he was pleased that neither such journalists, "neither bankers, nor hegemonic countries, nor bourgeoisie, controlled Ecuador", and stated that, if re-elected, "it will continue the changes guided in the right way and in the right direction".
President Correa also backed the decision to further maintain a veto expressed by Paraguay regarding Unasur's membership at least until its next election, arguing that the whole "must be firm and not tolerate opportunism and puchism wrapped in legality" because, in fact, it "destroyed the legitimacy of Paraguayan democracy."
In addition, the Ecuadorian president said he deemed it "completely appropriate" the desire of his Colombian counterpart, Juan Manuel Santos, to negotiate with Nicaragua on the maritime borders between the two countries now, at the time after the judgment of the International Court in The Hague, which ruled in favor of Nicaragua.
At the moment, the verdict is not accepted.
"This is a problem between the South American and Central American countries.
Conflicts are unavoidable, but they have to be overcome by the will to walk together.
They need to be judged broadly so that they are overcome and there is an opportunity to move forward.”
He also expressed hope in settling a dispute over maritime borders, which is being settled in the same court between Peru and Chiles, saying that "it is right for Latin America to seek out international instances if the two countries pledge to accept the verdict, no matter how tough it may be."
In the context of the possibility that he will run in Ecuador's next presidential election to run for a third term, he noted that he perceives this possibility "with great optimism and joy, even if it is sometimes very difficult."
Correa said that if she loses in February 2013, she will retire from public life.
“I have never been personally interested in power, but such unfair situations as socio-economic poverty in Ecuador can only be corrected by political power.
“My political movement believed that it was me who guaranteed this likely victory, so we have to accept that responsibility.”
"If I won, it would be my last presidential term, and then I'll leave politics.
If we lose, I'll have the same.
I've already decided," he stated.
Correa also referred to the new health treatment of Venezuelan President Hugo Chavez in Cuba.
"I have just spoken to Venezuelan Vice-President Nicolás Maduro, who says President Chavez has gone for planned routine treatment and his return to Cuba has been expected after the campaign.
It's not a relapse in the president's health," he explained.
The head of the Ecuadorian state participated today in the Sixth Summit of the Heads of State and Government of the Union of South American Nations (Unasur), which ended with calls for greater regional integration in order to maintain development, equality and security.
Today, victims of AIDS are the result of a late diagnosis
Fabrizius was 21 when the result of his test was confirmed: HIV positive.
"It was as if lightning struck me," he recounts, remembering the moment of truth, which the doctor was trying to "allieve a little," but apparently without success.
The boy was hiding it from his family.
He decided to take his own illness on his shoulders and began to inform himself about it, with such commitment that he had already celebrated his 43rd birthday.
He is without a doubt one of the most ancient HIV units at the Civil Hospital in Guadalajara (HCG), which he came to in 1994 after several battles with the disease.
Fabrizio has been living with human immunodeficiency virus (HIV) since the age of 22, which was something unimaginable in the early 1990s, when there were a lot of doubt, few treatment options and greater stigma.
At that time, even the director of the Mexican Institute of Social Security (IMSS) did not say goodbye to him "because he had a cutting wound".
Back then, having AIDS was synonymous with death.
It is now possible to survive with this syndrome and live fully.
However, there are a lot of people who don't know about their illness and come only when the virus has wronged the body and has exhausted the immune system, and then become victims of accidental infections.
31 years since AIDS appeared in the world, or at least from the first documented cases, "means great success that the life expectancy of a patient who begins treatment in a timely and appropriate manner is exactly the same as that of healthy people," said Jaime Andrade Villanueva, head of the HIV Unit at the Civil Hospital in Guadalajara, adding that this information was confirmed in a prestigious scientific publication in April.
Infectologist and HIV/AIDS specialist Andrade Villanueva explained that the researchers concluded that since 2008 AIDS is not a death sentence, but that the length of survival and full life depends on the degree of disability of the immune system of patients who are diagnosed with the disease, with better those who don’t use drugs: people with 200 CD4 cells can live up to 30 years, and people with 500 CD4 cells up to 50.
To put it simply, one diagnosed with HIV at the age of 25 can, according to these findings, "in compliance with treatment to live without problems up to the age of 75," the doctor explained in an interview.
To emphasise this progress, it is worth remembering that the average life expectancy of Mexicans today is 76 years.
Although mortality has declined significantly in recent years and, in the case of Mexico, the number of people who have died from AIDS has declined, from 6 678 in 2007 to 4 862 in 2011 (the yearbook of the United Nations Joint Programme for HIV/AIDS - UNAIDS), it is also true that 60 percent of patients have died since the discovery of AIDS, according to national statistics.
In Jalisco alone, 255 people died in 2011 and 187 so far this May. However, since 2005, universal access to anti-retroviurals has been ensured.
- Why do people continue to die from the effects of the disease?
- "It seems to me that the problem is not in the possibilities of access to treatment.
That's how I see it, that's how it's happening in our hospital.
At least in the last 12 years, we have not exhausted supplies of drugs. The problem is that patients come at a very advanced stage, because they do not know the state of infection, that is, in the late stage of the disease."
It adds a convincing figure: "Nine out of ten patients arrive when an associated infection already manifests itself. The only thing that needs to be done in order to be able to more affect the overall mortality rate is to diagnose the disease earlier, and therefore it is necessary on a large scale to offer tests to detect the disease to all the persons who ask for it".
In his proposal, he coincides with specialists and officials from the State Council for the Prevention of AIDS in the state of Jalisco directly with patients like Fabrizio, who came to do tests in a private laboratory just because his friend did it, and despite his young age, AIDS has already erupted, and he also suffered from Kaposi sarcoma, a cancer that is one of the usual complications.
“Everything changes when you know you have AIDS.
Some think they're going to die, and they don't want to know anything else.
"If I'm going to die already, I'd rather do three times a week," they say, but that's not my case.
The change was for the better, I eat healthily, I exercise, I take medication.”
To this day, his parents only know that he suffered from cancer.
“I live a normal life like everyone else.
I work, I have a lot of activities, I travel, I lead an active sex life, but responsibly, I look out for myself and the other person," Fabrizio, who accepts the invitation to share his experience with the daily MILENIO JALISCO to motivate those who are afraid today as part of World AIDS Day.
"Let them take the tests. If they've taken risks, let them know as soon as possible if they're HIV-positive, it's better. And if they've already been diagnosed, let them realize that they can live like anyone else if they're responsible."
His message summarizes the motto of the battle against AIDS this year 2012.
Condomes behind the counter
There is a big divide between health programs and ordinary citizens, said Ricardo Salazar, a Guadalajar journalist who finds out information about the HIV virus.
And the biggest gaps are in prevention.
In places dedicated to this task "it has really increased the distribution of condoms, they used to give us one or two, today they give us packages even hundreds of condoms, which is very good, but there are still people who do not have access to condoms," he said.
Among the most susceptible to new outbreaks are teens.
"What do you want it for?" is a common question full of irony and judgments from social workers, school counselors, pharmacies staff and medical staff that teens want to avoid, a reporter says.
He proposed to change this ineffective distribution. Let the condoms not only be behind the counters, but let the hundreds be found in dispensers in public toilets, in places where young people often go.
“It’s not promoting promiscuity.
It's not even a matter of "paying for beer and a motel," as Governor Emilio González reacted when asked if he'd agree to distribute condoms in his county.
It’s best to make condoms accessible to those who already have a sex life,” he remarked.
Key numbers in Jalisco
There have been 13 435 cases (12 158 cases of AIDS and 1 317 cases of HIV).
This condition marks the fourth place in Mexico in new and detected AIDS cases and 13th place in connection with HIV.
92% of the infection occurs sexually, 6% by blood lying 2% perinatally.
It is estimated that about 50 thousand people can live with HIV, because there are four to five people who do not know about the disease for one registered case.
Judgment approved by the Court of Appeal of the United States, which does not recognize Grupo Vitro's debt restructuring established by bankruptcy in Mexico, raises an unfortunate precedent for any national enterprise with branches in a neighboring country and a solvency problem.
It appears, therefore, that the procedures for the restructuring of firms that enable Mexican laws do not apply in the land of stripes and stars despite international agreements.
Approval of a verdict issued on June 15 last year by Judge Harlin Hal at the bankruptcy court for northern Texas leaves, in the practice of Mexican firms, undefensed against the possibility of confiscating their property on the other side of the el Bravo River.
However, the verdict opens the door to Mexico's main glass makemer at the U.S. Supreme Court with an appeal against three inconsistencies.
First of all, it must be said that while the judge of this case argues that creditors are supposed to be governed by the insolvency law of the United States, the Court of Appeal of the Fifth County based in New Orleans says that the main procedure stipulates bankruptcy proceedings in Mexico.
Firstly, this would indicate ignorance of international procedural cooperation in cases of insolvency of transnational enterprises.
Essentially, for this purpose, a United Nations model law was created to unify international commercial law, which is determined by the Arbitrator to the American Law Institute.
Secondly, the judgment stipulates that, without a vote of branches from the internal corporate structure, when Vitro's indebtedness against branches is acknowledged, the required majority would not have been achieved for the approval of the restructuring.
However, Mexican laws allow this possibility.
The fact is that Vitro's case was not the first in which this scheme was adopted.
There are half a dozen examples like Agremex and Comercial Mexica, for whose intra-company debates were guaranteed by the Federal Bankruptcy Institute.
In addition, it is true that even after deducting the votes of branches, creditors - who opposed Vitro have stood trial in the United States, i.e. "vulture funds" such as Aurelios Capital, Aurelios Convergencia, Elliot Internacional and Liverpool Limited - would not have reached a majority.
In the vote, they would reach about 45 percent to 37.
The Court of Appeal does not take into account this.
From a different point of view, the court accuses Vitro of the difficult situation it has faced since 2008 - against the backdrop of a serious economic crisis that struck the United States.
In the meantime, Gonzáleze Sady’s family-owned firm will file an appeal for a re-assessment directly before the Court of Appeal that the assessment should take place with full participation, i.e. with the participation of five judges, because only three had originally voted.
If it fails here, it will demand, as an appeal, a revision of the higher-instance process, in this case by the Supreme Court of the United States.
What is serious about the case is that the court omitted a document sent by the Mexican government as an amicus curiae department, in which Vitro's procedure under the Bankruptcy Act is described in detail, pointing out that the company has eased itself by inclining itself to the agreements signed by both countries to confront the law in question with Chapter 15 of the United States Insolvency Act.
Moreover, it points out that the country has subordinated to the principles of the United Nations Commission on International Trade, i.e. rules intended for international insolvency cases that guarantee equality for debtors and creditors.
An impact on both sides: punishment for Vitro as well as the state.
Overall rating
Unnoticed, a criminal complaint by the Mexican aviation unions against the former owner of Gastón Azcárraga Andrade, who was accused of fraud, was on the table for several months, and, according to the pilot's union, the vessel had already overflowed.
An instance headed by Carlos DÍaz Chávez Morineau has just filed a lawsuit with the National Banking Commission and the Securities Commission, which he accuses of obstructions in achieving justice.
According to her, the Audit Office systematically refused to report to the Prosecutor General's Office on a transaction made by this entrepreneur, in which he collected 198 million pesos from IXE from fideikomis F/589 on behalf of the Mexican Aviation Company.
The funds were most likely directed to the purchase of shares in the Professional Administration of Hotels (Administradora Profesional de Hoteles).
As we know, Azcárraga Andrade is the majority shareholder of the Posadas hotel chain.
Siege of Dragon Martu
A group of domestic and foreign ecologists, academics, entrepreneurs and ordinary citizens, who approved the creation of the association, gathered at the Universidad de Caribe at the weekend in order to prevent the opening of the Chinese Dragon Martu in Kankun.
We are talking, as we know, about a huge commercial and distribution center with products from a "country with a large wall" focused on Mexico, Central America and the Caribbean, which at the bottom would include a residential zone for employees out of 150 companies.
Previously, Canacintra (Cámara Nacional de la Industria de Transformación) was able to unify the governors of the southeastern zone in order to oppose a monumental area that would partly devastate the protected areas and which represents the origin of all resistance to industry.
The end of the matter with the ACTA document
A request from the Senate, which was not taken into account by the government and which demanded an explanation of the conditions and circumstances under which the Mexican Ambassador signed the Anti-Counterfeiting Agreement in Japan, known as ACTA, according to the Mexican Institute of Industrial Property (Instituto Mexicano de la Propiedad Industrial), had ceased to date.
As is known, this happened despite the fact that the Senate itself ruled out the opportunity to regard it as a threat to freedom of expression on social media.
Homex in the long term
In an effort to repay long-term debts and not affect those short-term, developer Homex puts investment certificates on the market for 500 million pesos.
This series is the first of four identical ones that offer interest repayment every 28 days.
Competival is born
The merger of leading information technology companies such as NYCE, e-Quálity and Kernet was born by an association called Competival, which aims to market software services in Central and South America.
Investment in the business will exceed $1.5 billion.
Héctor "Hetin" Reyes: " Basketball was my life"
Basketball world-beater Héctor "Hetin" Reyes has been associated with this indoor sport for more than 60 years and has traveled all over the world.
Few people in Puerto Rico have as many memories of the history of local basketball as Héctor "Hetin" Reyes.
Reyes was a wheelchair-embrassed stroke in 2008 for more than 60 years, whether as a player in the younger categories, a BSN National Basketballing League player, owner and coach of Vaqueros de Bayamón in the National Basketball League, or the president of the Puerto Rican Basketball Federation.
“I’ve held a lot of roles in basketball over the course of my life, including several at the same time, like when I was president of the BSN National League in the 1990s, the general managing director of the national team and chairman of the federation,” Reyes recalled while visiting reporters at Primera Hora newspaper at home in Bayamón, where he has lived with Isabel for more than 50 years.
"Basketball was my life."
Reyes doesn't exaggerate in his claim.
The walls of his house, which are almost all decorated with photos and souvenirs from his long career, testify.
Bayamón in the heart
With the greatest emotion of all, he guards those commemorating his tenure in los Vaqueros from the mid-1950s, where he was a player until 1982, when at the same time he celebrated 15 years as a co-owner or team owner.
“That was my best years, which I enjoyed the most, because I had the opportunity to participate in eight championships within 1967 with los Vaqueros, whether as an owner, co-owner or coach.
It's been a number of successful years, including five championships held between 1971 and 1975.
And then I said goodbye with one championship - in 1981, when Jerome Mincy made his BSN debut.
That's when "Cuco" Ortiz took over the team, which was an excellent leader," Reyes said in a statement.
"I remember Gene Bartow, who used to be a coach here and worked at Alabama University in Birmingham at the time, told me: "I've got a two-metre player for you.
Do you want him?"
And that was the beginning of Mincy, one of the best players Puerto Rico has ever had.
Bartow then advised my quarterback Raymond Gausse, who became a member of the team and one of our best scorers.
I remember he said if Mincy brought Bayamon the championship win, Gausse would then contribute to the next one.”
He enjoyed the championship, playing Gausse for the los Vaqueros team, but only at a distance, because in 1988 he was the president of the basketball federation.
At that time, he wanted to devote himself fully to his functions and enjoy Mincy's placing in the national team.
“I remember for the first time beating the United States in a qualifying Olympic match in Mexico in 1989.
Then came the world championship in 1990, where we finished fourth, but we should have had bronze had it not been for that Canadian referee who let us repeat the final throw-in," Reyes said.
Is the team from the 1990 World Cup the best national team you've ever seen?
He's among the best, as is the one who beat the Dream Team at the 2004 Olympics.
But my favorite was the national team in the Pan-American Games in Cuba in 1991, when we won the gold and wrotched the United States team, which was quite similar to the one that blew our bronze at the world championships.
The team had not only Mincy, Gausse, Ramon Rivas, Fico López and "Piculín" (Ortiz), but also there were youngsters (Javier) "Toñito" Colón and James Carter, brothers León (Francisco and Edgar) and Mario "Quijote" Morales, who couldn't be on the team in 1990 because of a knee injury.
The team, which may not have had a perfect composition but was able to win a gold medal and bring great joy, was a team from an Olympic qualifier in 1995 in Neuquén, Argentina.
With roster players such as "Canito" Nieves, Pablo Alicea and junior player Rolando Hourruitiner, who replaced the players excluded due to a scuffle in the Pan-American Games in Mar de Plata, we won gold despite all the predictions.
What was Puerto Rico's best player?
It was without any doubt Piculín Ortiz.
His numbers from international tournaments are impressive.
No one from Puerto Rico has achieved such results at this level as Piculín.
Not to mention his tenure in the various leagues where he played.
Who was Puerto Rican coach?
That's very hard.
We had a very good generation, which includes Julio Toro, Flor Meléndez, Carlos Morales, Raymond Dalmau and Armandito Torres.
Of the youngsters, I really like Leo Arillo's work.
Which success do you consider to be the greatest in the Federation?
That I was part of the most famous era of the national team from 1988 to 1995 and that in the early 1990s the BSN national league had up to 17 teams in one season.
What didn't you get to prove?
There were things I wanted to introduce, like the division of youth categories by region.
For example, the guys from Ponce would play only in their area and compete with teams from other corners of the island only in the national play-offs.
Now the kids travel a lot and play too many matches, which is not necessary.
I also see benefits in mandatory certificates and courses for coaches, assistant referees and referees.
I'm delighted about that.
What are you doing now?
I listen to music the most, I watch YouTube videos with music from my time, I enjoy my grandchildren, and occasionally go to basketball games.
And of course I'm enjoying the company of my wife Isabel, who's always been by my side.
Actor Larry Hagman has died
Larry Hagman, who was born on September 21, 1931 in Forth Worth, Texas, the world-renowned John Ross Ewing, better known as "J.R." in the TV series Dallas, in which he embodied a man who acted recklessly and was insidious and manipulative.
Larry Hagman, whose role as predatory oil tycoon J.R. Ewing in the television series Dallas became a symbol of greed in the 1980s, has died.
He was 81 years old.
Hagman, who returned this year as J.R. in a new series of Dallas, has died according to an official announcement from a family provided to Associated Press by production company Dallas Warner Bros. on Friday afternoon for cancer-related complications.
"Larry has returned to his beloved hometown of Dallas, where he once again played the iconic role he liked best," his family said in a statement.
Larry's family and his closest friends were with him in Dallas for Thanksgiving.
"Linda Gray, who was the representative of his wife in both the original and new series, was with Hagman the moment he died in a Dallas hospital," her agent Jeffrey Lane said in a statement.
He brought joy to all he knew.
"He's been creative, generous, funny, kind, kind and talented and I'm going to miss him terribly.
He was unique and lived his life to the fullest," Linda Gray said in the announcement.
Hagman was diagnosed with liver cirrhosis in 1992, and he himself admitted that he had been drinking a lot for years.
In 1995, a malignant tumor was found on his liver, and Hagman underwent a transplant.
Years before the release of Dallas, Hagman became famous on television as a decent guy in the lightweight comedy I Dream of Jeannie aired by NBC from 1965 to 1970.
Here he starred Captain Tony Nelson, an astronaut whose life changes when he meets an attractive girl (genie) played by Barbara Eden, and takes her home to live together.
He was also the protagonist in two comedies that were aired only briefly: The Good Life (NBC, 1971-72) and Here We Go Again (ABC, 1973).
His performances in the films included roles that were well-accepted by criticism: The Group, Harry and Tonto and Primary Colors.
But it was Hangman's championship performance in the form of an incredibly repulsive J.R. that led him to the stellar peak.
CBS's dramatic series about the Ewing clan and other personalities around them was broadcast from April 1978 to May 1991.
The promotional slogan "Who shoot Jajar?", which was supposed to raise anticipations about a work full of emotions, when Hangman's character is almost murdered, caused not only a huge array of speculation by viewers around the world, but also risking millions of dollars in sazka offices.
It also helped this episode to reach the ratings record.
The November 1980 episode, in which the response was revealed, was watched by an average of 41 million television viewers. Dallas became the second most-watched entertainment show in history after the end of MASH in 1983, watched by 50 million viewers.
His sister-in-law Kristin (played by Mary Crosby).
Kristin and J.R. became pregnant, who then threatened to label her as a prostitute if she didn't leave the city, but there were others who had a reason to attack him.
Hagman played Ewing, an insatiable greed with a charismatic smile: a cunning businessman and an unfaithful husband trying to get his wife Sue Ellen (Linda Gray), who was an alcoholic, to be confined to rehab.
"I know what I want J.R. to have on the tombstone," Hagman said in 1988.
It should be there: "Here's an honorable citizen, J.R. Ewing."
That's the only thing that didn't work out.
Victoria Principal, who starred in the original series, recalled Hagman on Friday as someone who was "great on and off the screen."
It is unforgettable and irreplaceable for the millions of admirers in the world and in the hearts of all of us who were fortunate enough to know and love him.
Ten episodes of the new Dallas series aired by TNT a few months ago with great success.
"Five episodes of the second row have already been completed, and the sixth has just been filmed," the television station said.
Immediately after Hangman's death, there was no statement from Warner or TNT about how the series would proceed.
A native of Fort Worth, Texas, he was the son of actress and singer Mary Martin, who shone in classical works such as musicals South Pacific and Peter Pan.
Mary Martin was a very young girl when, in 1931, she gave birth to Larry, whose father was her husband, lawyer Ben Hagman.
In the early 1950s Larry tried his luck on the theatre scenes in New York, and then from 1952 to 1956 he served in England with the Air Force.
That's where he met young Swedish designer Maj Axelsson, whom he married.
The married couple had two children, Preston and Heidi, and for a long time lived in the California town of Malibu, where many celebrities reside.
In 2001, he published an autobiographical book, Hello Darlin': Tall (and Absolutely True) Tales about My Life.
"I didn't put anything out there I knew could hurt anyone or harm him in any way," Prensa Asociada said at the time.
After a liver transplant, he began to promote organ donation and worked as a volunteer at the hospital, where he assisted fearful patients.
"I advise them, I add courage, I meet with them when they go for surgery and afterwards," he said in 1996.
I'm trying to give them comfort in the sense of "don't worry, it's going to be a little uncomfortable for a while, but you'll be healthy."
He was also an anti-smoking activist and participated in various campaigns.
It begins with a course that examines the "end of the world"
Each week, students explore apocalyptic themes such as nuclear warfare, zombies, viruses and bacteria, and global warming.
When religion professor Stuart Charmé decided this semester to take a course about the end of the world, he knew it was an irresistible allure: The end of the "long number" of the Mayan calendar, which is scheduled to come on on December 21, has been caught by many people as proof that the end of the world is coming.
Charmé, however, had no idea what awaited him in the next few months: Catastrophic Hurricane Sandy, a budgetary divide that some call the "Debt Armageddon," and the growing conflict into which Israel is drawn, a place in which, according to Christians theorizing about the end of the world, the Apocalypse will begin.
"I didn't realize this was going to be the most apocalyptic semester he's ever been," Charmé said this week to students at Rutgers University of Camden, New Jersey.
When a person discusses what is happening in the world today, as if we have 30 days left, it is a really good time.
And remember that bad means good for apocalyptical-minded people.
And he's not the only professor offering courses on the "end of the world" this semester, which is theoretically the last in history.
At Temple University, assistant professor Barry Vacker organizes a "Means, Culture and the End of the World" course.
Each week, students explore apocalyptic themes such as nuclear warfare, zombies, viruses and bacteria, and global warming.
"We're discussing why these thoughts expand with time," he says, and how they offer hypothetical scenarios that govern certain human behaviors.
For example, if nuclear material falls into the hands of terrorists, war can break out.
This month, students are analyzing films with apocalyptic topics and researching how it differs from real-life examples.
"I've been trying to inform students about what's possible, probable, believable and impossible," Vacker says.
On the main campus of the State University of Pensylvania, Matthew Restall and his colleague Amara Solari, an assistant professor of art history and anthropology, came together to organize a course with the simple name The End of the World.
"We're not adding "2012" because we're always counting on the possibility of organizing the course again in case the world doesn't end," Restall said.
Despite "imminent doom", students must study, create projects and take final exams.
At Penn State, the final rehearsal will take place on the eve of the Apocalypse, giving students no choice but to work "just until the evening of the supposed end of the world," Restall said.
The courses have become quite popular.
"He stretched within two hours," Restall said of his course for high-average students, of which 35 have signed up.
Weeks and weeks before the beginning of the semester, we were e-mailed asking if there was still room.
The students themselves assure that the course is one of the most interesting.
"I find it fascinating to see what people are doing to comfort themselves," 23-year-old Bridgid Robinson, a student of religion and sociology at Haddonfield, New Jersey, said at Rutgers University in Camden.
Therefore, apocalyptic, secular, or religious thinking is just a matter of consolation or its absence.
Will Wekesa, a 25-year-old student in psychology and nursing, said he saw all the apocalyptic films.
“I’ve never heard of an hour that could be taught,” he said.
"I'm enjoying it."
But no confessed student, let alone a professor, said they believed on the date of the end of December 21.
"Our first project dealt with Mayan predictions, and in some way we're refuting it," said Julie Zeglen, a 21-year-old senior student at West Chester's Temple University.
"The Mayans have never predicted the end of the world; it's just a key point on the calendar," Restall said.
But he added that there is an apocalyptic anxiety in Western culture that goes back several centuries that people are responding to the changes of their surroundings with predictions about the end of the world.
This speculation flourished by the Internet.
"Elsewhere, people don't think about it," he said.
This happens in the vast majority in an English-speaking world.
Joseph Dougherty, a professor of religion at La Salle University, who lectures in the Philippines this year, when asked if he knew about any course about the "end of the world," he answered quickly.
"Philippines don't take part in the end of the world," he wrote, hinting at an exemption from the supreme office.
We have mercy from the Pope.
Restall noted that over the years there has been talk of many days of the last trial, saying that if nothing happens on December 21, "people will start thinking immediately about the next date" or philosophizing that December 21 is the beginning of a seven-year period, at the end of which the world will disappear.
Both students and professors take the date lightly.
A few have said they intend to go to the festivities of the "end of the world."
"I'm probably going to call some friends to laugher together," twenty-year-old communications student Samira Ford explained.
